{"level":30,"time":1776973730987,"pid":123,"hostname":"844f2260-bff","step":"review-runs","item":"16.md","index":2,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018yxCvnMWUts5jn2tHgiPkp","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Adjacent Property Context, Compatibility Screening, and Buffering\n     2→\n     3→## Description\n     4→\n     5→This grouping covers documentation of adjacent property context on site plans (land uses, zoning designations, building footprints, and proposed building dimensions) and requirements to buffer and screen proposed development from neighboring properties. It encompasses compatibility screening and buffering under Article 10 of the LDC (effective July 15, 2024), general-applicability compatibility noise and lighting controls from the General Site Plan Notes, parking area screening, Waterfront Overlay–specific screening requirements, and connectivity to adjacent lots. Documentation items apply to every site plan submittal; compatibility screening, buffering, and noise/lighting controls apply conditionally based on subject site zoning, proximity to triggering properties, and applicable overlay districts.\n     6→\n     7→## Regulatory Overview\n     8→\n     9→**Compatibility Standards (Article 10) — Framework and Applicability**\n    10→\n    11→Article 10 of LDC Subchapter C (§§ 25-2-1051–1063, as completely replaced by Ord. No. 20240516-004, effective July 15, 2024) governs compatibility of higher-intensity development adjacent to low-density residential uses. The former Article 10 — which used a four-tier distance framework at 50, 100, 300, and 540 feet, and which referenced separate sections for lighting (§ 25-2-1064), parking/equipment/storage screening (§ 25-2-1066), and noise (§ 25-2-1067) — was repealed in its entirety. Those sections no longer exist. The current Article 10 consolidates screening, noise, lighting, and design requirements in § 25-2-1063 and uses only two distance thresholds (50 ft and 75 ft) for height limits, with all screening and noise obligations measured at the shared property line. Any plan notes or compatibility exhibits that cite \"LDC 25-2-1064,\" \"25-2-1066,\" or \"25-2-1067\" are citing repealed authority and must be corrected.\n    12→\n    13→**Triggering Property Definition**\n    14→\n    15→Article 10 applies to a site that is: (1) zoned MF-4 or less restrictive (i.e., MF-4, MF-5, MF-6, or any commercial or industrial district), AND (2) located within 75 feet of a **triggering property** (LDC § 25-2-1051). Both conditions must be simultaneously satisfied. A triggering property is a site that (a) has at least one but fewer than four dwelling units (1–3 units), AND (b) is zoned SF-5 or more restrictive (LDC § 25-2-1053). \"SF-5 or more restrictive\" means one of these eight base residential districts: **LA (Lake Austin), RR (Rural Residence), SF-1, SF-2, SF-3, SF-4A, SF-4B, or SF-5**. SF-6 and higher-density residential districts (MF-1 through MF-6) are less restrictive than SF-5 and do NOT qualify. Special purpose districts (P, PUD, TOD, ERC, etc.) are outside the zoning hierarchy and cannot qualify (LDC § 25-2-33(C)). Elementary schools zoned P are not triggering properties under the current code — the former school criterion was part of the repealed Article 10 and no longer applies. For a property with qualifying zoning to count as a triggering property, it must also contain 1–3 dwelling units; a vacant lot or commercially used parcel zoned SF-3, for example, does not qualify. Because dwelling unit counts on adjacent properties are rarely documented in plan submissions, this element frequently requires field verification or City GIS confirmation.\n    16→\n    17→Sites zoned MF-3 or more restrictive (MF-3, MF-2, MF-1, and all SF districts) are NOT subject to Article 10 — they are more restrictive than MF-4. Sites zoned CBD or DMU are explicitly exempt under § 25-2-1052 regardless of proximity.\n    18→\n    19→**Article 10 Exceptions (§ 25-2-1052)**\n    20→\n    21→Even when the site is MF-4 or less restrictive and within 75 feet of a triggering property, Article 10 does not apply to: (a) structural alterations that do not increase square footage, area, or height; (b) sites zoned CBD or DMU; (c) sites used exclusively for duplex, single-family attached, single-family residential, small lot SF residential, two-unit residential, three-unit residential, adult care services (limited or general), or child care services (limited or general).\n    22→\n    23→**Compatibility Buffer (§ 25-2-1062 and § 25-8-700)**\n    24→\n    25→When Article 10 applies, a compatibility buffer is required along any property line shared with a triggering property. Standard buffer width is **25 feet** (reduced to **15 feet** if the site is less than 75 feet wide measured from the shared triggering property line). The buffer must be internally sub-divided into two labeled zones per LDC § 25-8-700(D):\n    26→\n    27→- **Screening zone:** First 10 feet from the triggering property line. Must include, at every 25 linear feet: 1 large shade tree + 1 small understory tree + 10 shrubs. May NOT contain vertical structures, parking, driveways, or mechanical equipment.\n    28→- **Restricted zone:** Next 15 feet (25-ft buffer) or the balance (15-ft buffer). May include surface parking, driveways, fire lanes, mechanical equipment, and refuse receptacles, in addition to everything allowed in the screening zone.\n    29→\n    30→A plan that shows a single undifferentiated \"25-foot compatibility buffer\" without separately labeled screening and restricted zones does not comply. All buffer vegetation must be from ECM Appendix N Preferred Plant List and must be irrigated, maintained, and certified per ECM (§ 25-8-700(C)(6)). Healthy existing vegetation in the screening zone does not need to be removed (§ 25-8-700(C)(7)).\n    31→\n    32→The compatibility buffer is NOT required when: (a) the site use is condominium residential or townhouse residential (§ 25-2-1062(A)); (b) the site includes only residential uses with ≤16 dwelling units (§ 25-2-1062(B)(2)(a)); or (c) the site's zoning establishes a maximum height of 40 feet or less AND the site is zoned NO, LO, or LR (§ 25-2-1062(B)(2)(b)).\n    33→\n    34→**Compatibility Screening, Noise, and Design Requirements (§ 25-2-1063)**\n    35→\n    36→The following must not be visible from the shared property line or alleyway with a triggering property, except through pedestrian or bicycle access points: vehicle lights from parking lots or structures; ground floor and rooftop mechanical equipment; outdoor storage; refuse receptacles and collection areas; common amenity areas (decks, patios, pools). Additional requirements measured at the shared triggering property line:\n    37→\n    38→- **Lighting:** Exterior lighting must be hooded or shielded so the light source is not visible. There is no foot-candle limit in the current Article 10 — the former 0.4 foot-candle limit was in the repealed § 25-2-1064 and is not carried forward in § 25-2-1063.\n    39→- **Noise:** Mechanical equipment may not produce sound exceeding 70 dB(A).\n    40→- **Refuse slab:** Concrete refuse receptacle slab may not be within 15 feet of triggering property.\n    41→- **On-site amenities:** Amenities available only to residents/occupants may not be within 25 feet of triggering property (multi-use trails excepted).\n    42→\n    43→**General Site Plan Notes — Broader Compatibility Scope**\n    44→\n    45→The City's standard General Site Plan Notes (Compatibility section) impose parallel obligations that run BROADER than Article 10 — they apply to all sites, not just MF-4 or less restrictive sites within 75 feet of triggering properties. These general requirements include: (a) exterior lighting must be hooded or shielded from view of adjacent residential uses or property zoned residential (any residential, not limited to triggering properties); (b) exterior lighting above the second floor is prohibited when adjacent to residential property; (c) mechanical equipment noise may not exceed 70 dB(A) at the property line adjacent to residential uses; and (d) dumpsters and permanently placed refuse receptacles must be located at a minimum of 20 feet from a property used or zoned SF-5 or more restrictive. Note: the 20-foot General Notes dumpster setback is stricter than the 15-foot Article 10 slab setback; both requirements coexist independently. A site within Article 10 must comply with both.\n    46→\n    47→**Parking Area Screening**\n    48→\n    49→Two independent code frameworks govern parking area screening near residential districts:\n    50→\n    51→- **LDC § 25-6-563 (Common boundary screening):** A parking facility in a non-residential district that adjoins a residential district must be separated by a wall or fence at least **4 feet** high along the common boundary (§ 25-6-563(A)). A parking facility of more than 10 spaces in a residential district that adjoins another residential district property must be separated by a wall, fence, or landscape screen at least **6 feet** high (§ 25-6-563(B)). Director may waive or Land Use Commission may modify design requirements under specified conditions.\n    52→- **LDC § 25-2-1006 (Article 9 Visual Screening):** Requires motor vehicle parking/storage/loading areas to be at least partially screened from street views; for commercial/industrial uses, also requires screening at any property line adjoining a residential district. Standards are prescribed by the ECM (ECM 2.4.3). This provision operates independently of Article 10 and is not \"compatibility screening along triggering property lines\" — it applies to any commercial/industrial use adjacent to any residential district.\n    53→\n    54→**Waterfront Overlay (WO) District**\n    55→\n    56→LDC § 25-2-721(D) applies only to sites in the Waterfront Overlay (WO) combining district — identified by a \"WO\" suffix in the zoning string (e.g., CS-WO, DMU-WO) or by the Combining District/Overlay field in facts.md. The WO district consists of 16 geographically defined subdistricts along Town Lake (Lady Bird Lake) and the Colorado River. For all WO sites: (a) surface parking must be screened from views of Town Lake, the Colorado River, parkland, and named creeks (§ 25-2-721(D)(1)(b)); and (b) above-grade parking structures must be architecturally screened from views of these same waterways/parkland and must incorporate pedestrian-oriented uses at ground level if adjacent (§ 25-2-721(D)(2)). ECM 2.8.3 provides WO-specific landscape screening standards for parking lots, requiring dense massing of trees, shrubs, or walls to screen parking from view; this standard does not apply to non-WO sites (confirmed by ECM 2.9.3). Check WO status by: facts.md Combining District field, zoning string \"WO\" suffix, cover sheet vicinity/zoning map, and geographic location within WO subdistrict boundaries.\n    57→\n    58→**Service Station Use**\n    59→\n    60→\"Service Station\" is the LDC-defined use category (§ 25-2-4(B)(67)) for sites providing fuel, lubricants, parts/accessories, or incidental services to motor vehicles. Plans labeling the use informally as \"gas station\" or \"fueling station\" should be flagged; the regulated LDC term is \"service station.\" Under LDC § 25-2-814, a service station must be screened from the street by a building or a landscape buffer that includes shade trees. Service stations may not have more than 16 fuel dispensers.\n    61→\n    62→**Connectivity to Adjacent Lots (LDC Subchapter E § 2.3.1.A)**\n    63→\n    64→Property connections to adjacent lots are required under LDC Subchapter E, § 2.3.1.A (Connectivity Between Sites), not BCM §2.3.1.A — that BCM section does not exist. The Subchapter E provision applies only when: (a) net site area is 3 acres or more in a non-residential zoning district; OR (b) parking is located between the building and the principal street in any zoning district. It is not a universal \"all sites\" requirement. The \"principal street\" is determined by the LDC Subchapter E Article 5 street hierarchy: Core Transit Corridor > Internal Circulation Route > Urban Roadway > Suburban Roadway > Highway/Hill Country Roadway. When multiple streets tie at the same priority, the principal street is the one with the highest transit service level (Director determination) or, if equal, the street designated by the lot owner.\n    65→\n    66→**Common Violation Patterns**\n    67→\n    68→- Plan notes or compatibility exhibits citing \"LDC 25-2-1064,\" \"25-2-1066,\" or \"25-2-1067\" — these sections are repealed; all current requirements are under § 25-2-1063.\n    69→- Compatibility based on proximity to an elementary school — the school criterion was in the repealed Article 10 and is no longer valid.\n    70→- A compatibility buffer shown as a single undifferentiated zone without separate labeled 10-ft screening zone and 15-ft restricted zone.\n    71→- Mechanical equipment, parking, or driveways shown within the first 10 feet of a compatibility buffer (the screening zone).\n    72→- A landscaping buffer per Article 9 (LDC § 25-2-1006 + ECM 2.4.3) presented as satisfying Article 10 compatibility buffer requirements — these are separate obligations and one does not substitute for the other.\n    73→- Plan notes citing \"BCM §2.3.1.A\" for property connections — this section does not exist; the correct provision is LDC Subchapter E, § 2.3.1.A and it applies only conditionally.\n    74→\n    75→**Key Thresholds:**\n    76→- **75 ft:** Maximum distance from subject site boundary to triggering property for Article 10 applicability\n    77→- **50 ft:** Inner height limit zone (structures within 50 ft of triggering property: max 40 ft height)\n    78→- **25 ft:** Standard compatibility buffer width along triggering property line; also minimum on-site amenity setback from triggering property\n    79→- **15 ft:** Reduced compatibility buffer width for narrow sites (<75 ft wide); also refuse receptacle slab setback from triggering property (Article 10)\n    80→- **20 ft:** Dumpster/permanently placed refuse setback from SF-5 or more restrictive property (General Notes — applies to all sites)\n    81→- **10 ft:** Width of screening zone (first zone of compatibility buffer)\n    82→- **70 dB(A):** Maximum mechanical equipment noise level at triggering property line or residential property line\n    83→- **4 ft:** Minimum wall/fence height for non-residential parking screening adjacent to residential district\n    84→- **6 ft:** Minimum height of wall/fence/landscape for residential parking (>10 spaces) screening adjacent to residential district; also minimum height for ECM 2.9.1 screening fence or evergreen hedge\n    85→- **3 acres / parking-between-building-and-street:** Dual thresholds for Subchapter E § 2.3.1.A connectivity requirement\n    86→\n    87→**Exceptions:**\n    88→- Article 10 does not apply to CBD/DMU zoning, structural alterations without increased square footage/area/height, or exclusively residential/care uses listed in § 25-2-1052\n    89→- Compatibility buffer not required for condominium or townhouse residential use, sites with ≤16 residential units only, or NO/LO/LR-zoned sites with ≤40 ft max height (§ 25-2-1062(A), (B)(2))\n    90→- Subchapter E § 2.3.1.A connectivity not required unless site meets net area or parking-location thresholds\n    91→- WO screening requirements apply only within the 16 defined WO subdistricts\n    92→\n    93→## Code References\n    94→\n    95→- LDC § 25-2-1051 — Article 10 Applicability: site must be zoned MF-4 or less restrictive AND within 75 feet of a triggering property\n    96→- LDC § 25-2-1052 — Article 10 Exceptions: CBD/DMU, structural alterations, listed residential/care uses\n    97→- LDC § 25-2-1053 — Triggering Property definition: 1–3 dwelling units, zoned SF-5 or more restrictive\n    98→- LDC § 25-2-1062 — Compatibility Buffers and Setbacks: 25-ft buffer, 15-ft narrow-site buffer, exceptions, NO/LO/LR setback minimums\n    99→- LDC § 25-2-1063 — Screening, Noise, and Design Requirements: lighting shielding, 70 dB(A) noise limit, 15-ft refuse slab setback, 25-ft amenity setback, and screening obligations at triggering property line (current replacement for repealed §§ 25-2-1064, 25-2-1066, 25-2-1067)\n   100→- LDC § 25-6-563 — Parking Area Screening (Common Boundary): 4-ft wall/fence for non-residential parking adj residential; 6-ft screen for residential parking >10 spaces adj residential\n   101→- LDC § 25-2-721(D) — Waterfront Overlay District Parking Requirements: surface parking screened from Town Lake/Colorado River/parkland; parking structure screening and ground-level pedestrian use requirements\n   102→- LDC § 25-2-814 — Service Station Use: street screening by building or landscape buffer with shade trees; 16-dispenser maximum\n   103→- LDC § 25-8-700 — Minimum Requirements for Compatibility Buffers: 10-ft screening zone planting requirements, 15-ft restricted zone use restrictions, Appendix N plant species requirement\n   104→- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.3.1.A — Connectivity Between Sites: property connections to adjacent lots; applicable to non-residential sites ≥3 acres or any site with parking between building and principal street\n   105→- ECM 2.9.1 — General Screening Standards: solid wood and/or masonry fence/wall ≥6 ft; evergreen hedge ≥6 ft mature; or existing vegetation ≥10 ft wide with specified density\n   106→- ECM 2.4.3 — Buffering (Landscape Buffer Composition): ≥2 plant elements, ≤50% single species, ≤¼ buffer absent of elements, 8-ft permeable area minimum\n   107→- ECM 2.8.3 — Waterfront Overlay District Parking Screening: dense massing of trees, shrubs, or wall/vegetative screening; WO sites only\n   108→- BCM 1.2.7 — Building Permit Submittal Requirements: site plan must show dimensions of lot and location/size of proposed building\n   109→- General Site Plan Notes (Compatibility) — Lighting, noise, dumpster setback, and reflective materials requirements applicable to all sites\n   110→\n   111→## Documents to Review\n   112→\n   113→- **Site plan sheets** — Adjacent land uses labeled on all sides; adjacent zoning designations and district boundaries shown; building footprints on adjoining lots within 50 feet shown or noted absent; proposed building dimensions and perimeter boundary line; parking layout relative to building and principal street; property connections to adjacent lots\n   114→- **Mylars** — Zoning designations must be included; compare to site plan sheets for consistency\n   115→- **Landscape / screening plan** — Screening material type (fence vs. vegetation), location along all triggering property lines, compatibility buffer with labeled screening zone (10 ft) and restricted zone (15 ft); composition details (species from ECM Appendix N, spacing, coverage); maintenance notes for existing vegetation; parking lot vegetative screening for WO sites\n   116→- **Photometric report / lighting plan** — Fixture locations relative to residential property lines; fixture cut sheets confirming hooding and shielding; no fixtures above second floor facing residential\n   117→- **Mechanical equipment schedule or specifications** — Equipment noise rating (dB(A)) at property line adjacent to residential; equipment location relative to compatibility buffer screening zone\n   118→- **Parking area details / garage elevations** — Headlight and vehicular movement screening details for parking adjacent to triggering property or residential district; parking garage screening shown on elevation sheets; WO parking screening from waterfront views\n   119→- **Cover sheet general notes / compatibility exhibit** — Verify code citations reference current § 25-2-1063, not repealed sections (25-2-1064, 25-2-1066, 25-2-1067)\n   120→- **Facts.md / project data sheet** — Site zoning (confirm MF-4 or less restrictive for Article 10), adjacent zoning designations, combining district/overlay fields (WO), dwelling unit count on adjacent properties if documented\n   121→\n   122→## Checklist Items\n   123→\n   124→| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n   125→|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n   126→| ZLU-16.1 | Adjoining land uses not noted on site plan for all surrounding properties on all sides (north, south, east, west) | Reviewer Convention | All sites |\n   127→| ZLU-16.2 | Adjoining land uses for properties across adjacent streets and rights-of-way not shown on site plan | Reviewer Convention | All sites |\n   128→| ZLU-16.3 | Vacant adjoining properties not identified as \"vacant\" on site plan | Reviewer Convention | All sites |\n   129→| ZLU-16.4 | Adjacent property zoning district designations not shown on site plan | Reviewer Convention | All sites |\n   130→| ZLU-16.5 | Adjacent zoning designations inaccurate or inconsistent with current City GIS zoning records | Reviewer Convention | All sites |\n   131→| ZLU-16.6 | Zoning district boundary lines not shown or labeled when site contains or abuts multiple zoning districts; sheet(s) where each boundary is depicted not identified | Reviewer Convention | Only when site is subject to or abuts multiple zoning districts. Check: zoning data in facts.md and GIS |\n   132→| ZLU-16.7 | PUD boundaries and land use categories not shown on site plan | Reviewer Convention | Only when site is within or adjacent to a PUD overlay. Check: project data and zoning ordinance |\n   133→| ZLU-16.8 | Zoning designations not included on mylars | Reviewer Convention | All sites |\n   134→| ZLU-16.9 | Existing building footprints on adjoining properties within 50 feet of the site not shown on site plan | Reviewer Convention | All sites |\n   135→| ZLU-16.10 | Note absent from site plan when no buildings exist on adjoining lots within 50 feet (required note: \"no buildings exist on adjoining lots within 50 feet\") | Reviewer Convention | All sites |\n   136→| ZLU-16.11 | Proposed building dimensions not shown on site plan to verify setback and lot coverage compliance | BCM 1.2.7 | All sites |\n   137→| ZLU-16.12 | Continuous perimeter boundary line for complete building footprint — including roofline and any attached canopies — not shown on site plan | BCM 1.2.7 | All sites |\n   138→| ZLU-16.13 | Property connections to adjacent lots not identified and called out on site plan per LDC Subchapter E, § 2.3.1.A — must provide private drive or public street connections to existing drives/streets on adjacent sites, or stub-outs if connections are not feasible | LDC Subchapter E, § 2.3.1.A | Only when: (a) net site area is 3 acres or more in a non-residential zoning district; or (b) parking is located between the building and the principal street in any zoning district. Check: net site area in site data table; parking layout on site plan. Note: the former citation \"BCM §2.3.1.A\" does not exist; this provision is LDC Subchapter E only |\n   139→| ZLU-16.14 | Article 10 compatibility applicability not confirmed or exception conditions not evaluated before applying compatibility standards — verify: (1) subject site is zoned MF-4 or less restrictive; (2) subject site is within 75 feet of a triggering property (1–3 dwelling units, zoned SF-5 or more restrictive: LA, RR, SF-1, SF-2, SF-3, SF-4A, SF-4B, or SF-5); and (3) no § 25-2-1052 exception applies | LDC §§ 25-2-1051, 25-2-1052, 25-2-1053 | Only when site is adjacent to SF-5 or more restrictive zoning or when any Article 10 checklist item is triggered. Check: site zoning in facts.md; adjacent zoning designations; project use type and description for exception conditions |\n   140→| ZLU-16.15 | Compatibility buffer applied without confirming buffer exceptions do not apply — verify use is not condominium or townhouse residential (§ 25-2-1062(A)), site does not include only residential uses with ≤16 dwelling units (§ 25-2-1062(B)(2)(a)), and site is not NO/LO/LR-zoned with maximum zoning height ≤40 feet (§ 25-2-1062(B)(2)(b)) | LDC § 25-2-1062(A), (B)(2) | Only when Article 10 applies and a compatibility buffer is being required. Check: site use type, unit count, and zoning max height |\n   141→| ZLU-16.16 | Compatibility screening not provided along all triggering property lines per LDC § 25-2-1063(E) — must screen vehicle lights, mechanical equipment, outdoor storage, refuse receptacles, and common amenity areas from view of shared property line/alleyway | LDC § 25-2-1063(E) | Only when Article 10 applies: subject site zoned MF-4 or less restrictive, within 75 feet of a triggering property, and no § 25-2-1052 exception applies. Check: site zoning and adjacent zoning/unit count in facts.md; distance from site boundary to nearest triggering property |\n   142→| ZLU-16.17 | Screening material non-compliant: must be (A) a solid wood and/or masonry fence or wall at least 6 feet in height; (B) a hedge-like screen of evergreen plant material capable of attaining 6 feet at maturity, planted at ≤4-foot spacing and ≥2.5 feet at time of planting; or (C) existing vegetation in a strip ≥10 feet wide containing the equivalent of 1 large tree + 2 small trees + 6 understory shrubs per 20 linear feet of screen, with limit-of-construction line clearly marked on plans | ECM 2.9.1 | Only when compatibility screening is required under Article 10. Check: screening material noted on landscape plan |\n   143→| ZLU-16.18 | Vegetative screening composition does not meet ECM 2.4.3 minimum standards: must include at least 2 plant elements, no more than 50% of trees or shrubs from a single species, no more than ¼ of the required buffer area absent of elements, and plants in a permeable landscape area at least 8 feet wide | ECM 2.4.3 | Only when vegetation is the proposed screening method along triggering property lines and ECM 2.4.3 buffering standards are cited as the compliance method. Check: plant schedule and landscape plan |\n   144→| ZLU-16.19 | Existing vegetation proposed as compatibility screening without a plan note confirming the vegetation will be maintained; existing vegetation screen must also be at least 10 feet wide with a limit-of-construction line marked on plans per ECM 2.9.1.C | ECM 2.9.1.C | Only when existing vegetation is proposed to satisfy screening requirements. Check: landscape plan notes and limit-of-construction callout |\n   145→| ZLU-16.20 | Screening fence placement impedes a pedestrian or bicycle access point | LDC § 25-2-1063(F) | Only when a screening fence is proposed along a triggering property line. Check: fence location on site plan relative to sidewalks, bike paths, and pedestrian entries |\n   146→| ZLU-16.21 | On-site amenities available only to residents or occupants (decks, patios, pools, recreation areas) placed within 25 feet of triggering property — multi-use trails are excepted | LDC § 25-2-1063(D) | Only when Article 10 applies. Check: site plan for on-site amenity locations relative to shared triggering property line |\n   147→| ZLU-16.22 | Compatibility buffer along triggering property line not shown, labeled, or provided; required buffer is 25 feet wide (or 15 feet if site is less than 75 feet wide measured from the shared triggering property line) | LDC § 25-2-1062(B); LDC § 25-8-700 | Only when a compatibility buffer is required under Article 10 (no applicable § 25-2-1062 exception). Check: landscape/compatibility plan for buffer zone callouts along triggering property lines |\n   148→| ZLU-16.23 | Compatibility buffer not divided into separately labeled 10-ft screening zone and 15-ft restricted use zone — a single undifferentiated buffer strip does not comply with zone-specific use restrictions under LDC § 25-8-700(D) | LDC § 25-8-700(D) | Only when a compatibility buffer is shown on plans. Check: landscape plan buffer callouts for separate zone labeling |\n   149→| ZLU-16.24 | Mechanical equipment, vertical structures, parking areas, or driveways placed within the 10-foot screening zone of the compatibility buffer — these uses are prohibited in the screening zone and are only permitted in the restricted zone | LDC § 25-8-700(C)(3), (C)(5) | Only when a compatibility buffer is required. Check: site plan and landscape plan for any equipment, paved areas, or structures within the first 10 feet of the buffer measured from the triggering property line |\n   150→| ZLU-16.25 | Compatibility buffer planting not specified from ECM Appendix N Preferred Plant List, or irrigation and maintenance plan not provided for buffer vegetation | LDC § 25-8-700(C)(6) | Only when a compatibility buffer with required planting is provided. Check: plant schedule species names against ECM Appendix N; landscape plan for irrigation notation |\n   151→| ZLU-16.26 | Refuse receptacle concrete slab placed within 15 feet of triggering property | LDC § 25-2-1063(C) | Only when Article 10 applies. Check: site plan for concrete refuse slab location measured from the nearest point of the triggering property line |\n   152→| ZLU-16.27 | Dumpster or permanently placed refuse receptacle located within 20 feet of a property used or zoned SF-5 or more restrictive (LA, RR, SF-1, SF-2, SF-3, SF-4A, SF-4B, or SF-5) | General Site Plan Notes (Compatibility) | All sites with a dumpster or permanently placed refuse receptacle. Check: site plan for refuse location relative to any adjacent SF-5 or more restrictive zoned property. Note: this 20-foot General Notes setback applies to all sites and coexists independently with the 15-foot Article 10 setback (ZLU-16.26) |\n   153→| ZLU-16.28 | Vehicle lights from parking lots or structures, or vehicular movement, not screened from shared triggering property line or alleyway per LDC § 25-2-1063(E)(1) | LDC § 25-2-1063(E)(1) | Only when Article 10 applies and parking or a parking structure is adjacent to or visible from the shared triggering property line. Check: site plan parking layout and screening details at triggering property boundary |\n   154→| ZLU-16.29 | Off-street parking in a non-residential district adjoining a residential district not screened by a wall or fence at least 4 feet in height along the common boundary adequate to screen car lights and vehicular movement | LDC § 25-6-563(A) | Only when non-residential-district parking abuts a residential-district property. Check: site zoning and adjacent zoning on site plan |\n   155→| ZLU-16.30 | Off-street parking of more than 10 spaces in a residential district adjoining another residential-district property not screened by a wall, fence, or landscape screen at least 6 feet in height along the common boundary | LDC § 25-6-563(B) | Only when residential-district parking (more than 10 spaces) abuts another residential-district property. Check: site zoning and parking space count |\n   156→| ZLU-16.31 | Surface parking lot in Waterfront Overlay district not screened from view by dense massing of trees, shrubs, walls, or other vegetative screening per ECM 2.8.3 | ECM 2.8.3 | Only when site is in the Waterfront Overlay combining district (zoning string contains \"WO\" suffix). Check: facts.md combining district field; cover sheet zoning designation |\n   157→| ZLU-16.32 | Surface parking not screened from views of Town Lake (Lady Bird Lake), Colorado River, parkland, or named creeks per LDC § 25-2-721(D)(1)(b) | LDC § 25-2-721(D)(1)(b) | Only when site is in the Waterfront Overlay combining district (zoning string contains \"WO\" suffix). Check: facts.md combining district field and cover sheet zoning |\n   158→| ZLU-16.33 | Service station use not screened from the street by a building or a landscape buffer that includes shade trees | LDC § 25-2-814 | Only when proposed use is a service station. Check: use classification in facts.md or cover sheet; confirm use provides fuel dispensing to motor vehicles (LDC term \"service station\" — informal terms such as \"gas station\" or \"fueling station\" should be flagged for terminology correction) |\n   159→| ZLU-16.34 | Exterior lighting not hooded or shielded so that the light source is not visible from the property line adjacent to residential uses or property zoned residential | LDC § 25-2-1063(A); General Site Plan Notes (Compatibility) | All sites with exterior lighting adjacent to residential uses or residential-zoned property. Under Article 10, this standard applies at the shared triggering property line/alleyway for qualifying sites; the General Site Plan Notes extend the same standard to any adjacent residential use or property zoned residential regardless of Article 10 status. Check: fixture cut sheets and lighting plan notes |\n   160→| ZLU-16.35 | Exterior lighting above the second floor installed in a location facing or adjacent to residential property | General Site Plan Notes (Compatibility) | All sites with exterior lighting adjacent to residential property. Check: lighting plan and building elevation sheets for any luminaires at third floor or higher facing adjacent residential property |\n   161→| ZLU-16.36 | Site plan general notes or compatibility exhibit cites repealed Article 10 sections (LDC 25-2-1064, LDC 25-2-1066, or LDC 25-2-1067) — these sections were repealed effective July 15, 2024 (Ord. No. 20240516-004) and all requirements are now consolidated in LDC § 25-2-1063 | LDC § 25-2-1063 | Only when site is subject to Article 10 compatibility requirements or site plan notes reference compatibility standards. Check: cover sheet general notes and any compatibility exhibit for section number citations |\n   162→| ZLU-16.37 | Mechanical equipment noise level not demonstrated to be at or below 70 dB(A) at the property line adjacent to residential uses | LDC § 25-2-1063(B); General Site Plan Notes (Compatibility) | All sites with mechanical equipment adjacent to residential uses. Under Article 10, measured at the shared triggering property line/alleyway; the General Site Plan Notes extend this standard to any property line adjacent to residential uses. Check: equipment schedule or manufacturer specifications for noise ratings at property line distance |\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"158c3787-95dd-4091-81cc-891db6165072","uuid":"c211a815-8eca-4a9e-80b0-dc897ffaaf47","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/zlu-md-exp/16.md","content":"# Adjacent Property Context, Compatibility Screening, and Buffering\n\n## Description\n\nThis grouping covers documentation of adjacent property context on site plans (land uses, zoning designations, building footprints, and proposed building dimensions) and requirements to buffer and screen proposed development from neighboring properties. It encompasses compatibility screening and buffering under Article 10 of the LDC (effective July 15, 2024), general-applicability compatibility noise and lighting controls from the General Site Plan Notes, parking area screening, Waterfront Overlay–specific screening requirements, and connectivity to adjacent lots. Documentation items apply to every site plan submittal; compatibility screening, buffering, and noise/lighting controls apply conditionally based on subject site zoning, proximity to triggering properties, and applicable overlay districts.\n\n## Regulatory Overview\n\n**Compatibility Standards (Article 10) — Framework and Applicability**\n\nArticle 10 of LDC Subchapter C (§§ 25-2-1051–1063, as completely replaced by Ord. No. 20240516-004, effective July 15, 2024) governs compatibility of higher-intensity development adjacent to low-density residential uses. The former Article 10 — which used a four-tier distance framework at 50, 100, 300, and 540 feet, and which referenced separate sections for lighting (§ 25-2-1064), parking/equipment/storage screening (§ 25-2-1066), and noise (§ 25-2-1067) — was repealed in its entirety. Those sections no longer exist. The current Article 10 consolidates screening, noise, lighting, and design requirements in § 25-2-1063 and uses only two distance thresholds (50 ft and 75 ft) for height limits, with all screening and noise obligations measured at the shared property line. Any plan notes or compatibility exhibits that cite \"LDC 25-2-1064,\" \"25-2-1066,\" or \"25-2-1067\" are citing repealed authority and must be corrected.\n\n**Triggering Property Definition**\n\nArticle 10 applies to a site that is: (1) zoned MF-4 or less restrictive (i.e., MF-4, MF-5, MF-6, or any commercial or industrial district), AND (2) located within 75 feet of a **triggering property** (LDC § 25-2-1051). Both conditions must be simultaneously satisfied. A triggering property is a site that (a) has at least one but fewer than four dwelling units (1–3 units), AND (b) is zoned SF-5 or more restrictive (LDC § 25-2-1053). \"SF-5 or more restrictive\" means one of these eight base residential districts: **LA (Lake Austin), RR (Rural Residence), SF-1, SF-2, SF-3, SF-4A, SF-4B, or SF-5**. SF-6 and higher-density residential districts (MF-1 through MF-6) are less restrictive than SF-5 and do NOT qualify. Special purpose districts (P, PUD, TOD, ERC, etc.) are outside the zoning hierarchy and cannot qualify (LDC § 25-2-33(C)). Elementary schools zoned P are not triggering properties under the current code — the former school criterion was part of the repealed Article 10 and no longer applies. For a property with qualifying zoning to count as a triggering property, it must also contain 1–3 dwelling units; a vacant lot or commercially used parcel zoned SF-3, for example, does not qualify. Because dwelling unit counts on adjacent properties are rarely documented in plan submissions, this element frequently requires field verification or City GIS confirmation.\n\nSites zoned MF-3 or more restrictive (MF-3, MF-2, MF-1, and all SF districts) are NOT subject to Article 10 — they are more restrictive than MF-4. Sites zoned CBD or DMU are explicitly exempt under § 25-2-1052 regardless of proximity.\n\n**Article 10 Exceptions (§ 25-2-1052)**\n\nEven when the site is MF-4 or less restrictive and within 75 feet of a triggering property, Article 10 does not apply to: (a) structural alterations that do not increase square footage, area, or height; (b) sites zoned CBD or DMU; (c) sites used exclusively for duplex, single-family attached, single-family residential, small lot SF residential, two-unit residential, three-unit residential, adult care services (limited or general), or child care services (limited or general).\n\n**Compatibility Buffer (§ 25-2-1062 and § 25-8-700)**\n\nWhen Article 10 applies, a compatibility buffer is required along any property line shared with a triggering property. Standard buffer width is **25 feet** (reduced to **15 feet** if the site is less than 75 feet wide measured from the shared triggering property line). The buffer must be internally sub-divided into two labeled zones per LDC § 25-8-700(D):\n\n- **Screening zone:** First 10 feet from the triggering property line. Must include, at every 25 linear feet: 1 large shade tree + 1 small understory tree + 10 shrubs. May NOT contain vertical structures, parking, driveways, or mechanical equipment.\n- **Restricted zone:** Next 15 feet (25-ft buffer) or the balance (15-ft buffer). May include surface parking, driveways, fire lanes, mechanical equipment, and refuse receptacles, in addition to everything allowed in the screening zone.\n\nA plan that shows a single undifferentiated \"25-foot compatibility buffer\" without separately labeled screening and restricted zones does not comply. All buffer vegetation must be from ECM Appendix N Preferred Plant List and must be irrigated, maintained, and certified per ECM (§ 25-8-700(C)(6)). Healthy existing vegetation in the screening zone does not need to be removed (§ 25-8-700(C)(7)).\n\nThe compatibility buffer is NOT required when: (a) the site use is condominium residential or townhouse residential (§ 25-2-1062(A)); (b) the site includes only residential uses with ≤16 dwelling units (§ 25-2-1062(B)(2)(a)); or (c) the site's zoning establishes a maximum height of 40 feet or less AND the site is zoned NO, LO, or LR (§ 25-2-1062(B)(2)(b)).\n\n**Compatibility Screening, Noise, and Design Requirements (§ 25-2-1063)**\n\nThe following must not be visible from the shared property line or alleyway with a triggering property, except through pedestrian or bicycle access points: vehicle lights from parking lots or structures; ground floor and rooftop mechanical equipment; outdoor storage; refuse receptacles and collection areas; common amenity areas (decks, patios, pools). Additional requirements measured at the shared triggering property line:\n\n- **Lighting:** Exterior lighting must be hooded or shielded so the light source is not visible. There is no foot-candle limit in the current Article 10 — the former 0.4 foot-candle limit was in the repealed § 25-2-1064 and is not carried forward in § 25-2-1063.\n- **Noise:** Mechanical equipment may not produce sound exceeding 70 dB(A).\n- **Refuse slab:** Concrete refuse receptacle slab may not be within 15 feet of triggering property.\n- **On-site amenities:** Amenities available only to residents/occupants may not be within 25 feet of triggering property (multi-use trails excepted).\n\n**General Site Plan Notes — Broader Compatibility Scope**\n\nThe City's standard General Site Plan Notes (Compatibility section) impose parallel obligations that run BROADER than Article 10 — they apply to all sites, not just MF-4 or less restrictive sites within 75 feet of triggering properties. These general requirements include: (a) exterior lighting must be hooded or shielded from view of adjacent residential uses or property zoned residential (any residential, not limited to triggering properties); (b) exterior lighting above the second floor is prohibited when adjacent to residential property; (c) mechanical equipment noise may not exceed 70 dB(A) at the property line adjacent to residential uses; and (d) dumpsters and permanently placed refuse receptacles must be located at a minimum of 20 feet from a property used or zoned SF-5 or more restrictive. Note: the 20-foot General Notes dumpster setback is stricter than the 15-foot Article 10 slab setback; both requirements coexist independently. A site within Article 10 must comply with both.\n\n**Parking Area Screening**\n\nTwo independent code frameworks govern parking area screening near residential districts:\n\n- **LDC § 25-6-563 (Common boundary screening):** A parking facility in a non-residential district that adjoins a residential district must be separated by a wall or fence at least **4 feet** high along the common boundary (§ 25-6-563(A)). A parking facility of more than 10 spaces in a residential district that adjoins another residential district property must be separated by a wall, fence, or landscape screen at least **6 feet** high (§ 25-6-563(B)). Director may waive or Land Use Commission may modify design requirements under specified conditions.\n- **LDC § 25-2-1006 (Article 9 Visual Screening):** Requires motor vehicle parking/storage/loading areas to be at least partially screened from street views; for commercial/industrial uses, also requires screening at any property line adjoining a residential district. Standards are prescribed by the ECM (ECM 2.4.3). This provision operates independently of Article 10 and is not \"compatibility screening along triggering property lines\" — it applies to any commercial/industrial use adjacent to any residential district.\n\n**Waterfront Overlay (WO) District**\n\nLDC § 25-2-721(D) applies only to sites in the Waterfront Overlay (WO) combining district — identified by a \"WO\" suffix in the zoning string (e.g., CS-WO, DMU-WO) or by the Combining District/Overlay field in facts.md. The WO district consists of 16 geographically defined subdistricts along Town Lake (Lady Bird Lake) and the Colorado River. For all WO sites: (a) surface parking must be screened from views of Town Lake, the Colorado River, parkland, and named creeks (§ 25-2-721(D)(1)(b)); and (b) above-grade parking structures must be architecturally screened from views of these same waterways/parkland and must incorporate pedestrian-oriented uses at ground level if adjacent (§ 25-2-721(D)(2)). ECM 2.8.3 provides WO-specific landscape screening standards for parking lots, requiring dense massing of trees, shrubs, or walls to screen parking from view; this standard does not apply to non-WO sites (confirmed by ECM 2.9.3). Check WO status by: facts.md Combining District field, zoning string \"WO\" suffix, cover sheet vicinity/zoning map, and geographic location within WO subdistrict boundaries.\n\n**Service Station Use**\n\n\"Service Station\" is the LDC-defined use category (§ 25-2-4(B)(67)) for sites providing fuel, lubricants, parts/accessories, or incidental services to motor vehicles. Plans labeling the use informally as \"gas station\" or \"fueling station\" should be flagged; the regulated LDC term is \"service station.\" Under LDC § 25-2-814, a service station must be screened from the street by a building or a landscape buffer that includes shade trees. Service stations may not have more than 16 fuel dispensers.\n\n**Connectivity to Adjacent Lots (LDC Subchapter E § 2.3.1.A)**\n\nProperty connections to adjacent lots are required under LDC Subchapter E, § 2.3.1.A (Connectivity Between Sites), not BCM §2.3.1.A — that BCM section does not exist. The Subchapter E provision applies only when: (a) net site area is 3 acres or more in a non-residential zoning district; OR (b) parking is located between the building and the principal street in any zoning district. It is not a universal \"all sites\" requirement. The \"principal street\" is determined by the LDC Subchapter E Article 5 street hierarchy: Core Transit Corridor > Internal Circulation Route > Urban Roadway > Suburban Roadway > Highway/Hill Country Roadway. When multiple streets tie at the same priority, the principal street is the one with the highest transit service level (Director determination) or, if equal, the street designated by the lot owner.\n\n**Common Violation Patterns**\n\n- Plan notes or compatibility exhibits citing \"LDC 25-2-1064,\" \"25-2-1066,\" or \"25-2-1067\" — these sections are repealed; all current requirements are under § 25-2-1063.\n- Compatibility based on proximity to an elementary school — the school criterion was in the repealed Article 10 and is no longer valid.\n- A compatibility buffer shown as a single undifferentiated zone without separate labeled 10-ft screening zone and 15-ft restricted zone.\n- Mechanical equipment, parking, or driveways shown within the first 10 feet of a compatibility buffer (the screening zone).\n- A landscaping buffer per Article 9 (LDC § 25-2-1006 + ECM 2.4.3) presented as satisfying Article 10 compatibility buffer requirements — these are separate obligations and one does not substitute for the other.\n- Plan notes citing \"BCM §2.3.1.A\" for property connections — this section does not exist; the correct provision is LDC Subchapter E, § 2.3.1.A and it applies only conditionally.\n\n**Key Thresholds:**\n- **75 ft:** Maximum distance from subject site boundary to triggering property for Article 10 applicability\n- **50 ft:** Inner height limit zone (structures within 50 ft of triggering property: max 40 ft height)\n- **25 ft:** Standard compatibility buffer width along triggering property line; also minimum on-site amenity setback from triggering property\n- **15 ft:** Reduced compatibility buffer width for narrow sites (<75 ft wide); also refuse receptacle slab setback from triggering property (Article 10)\n- **20 ft:** Dumpster/permanently placed refuse setback from SF-5 or more restrictive property (General Notes — applies to all sites)\n- **10 ft:** Width of screening zone (first zone of compatibility buffer)\n- **70 dB(A):** Maximum mechanical equipment noise level at triggering property line or residential property line\n- **4 ft:** Minimum wall/fence height for non-residential parking screening adjacent to residential district\n- **6 ft:** Minimum height of wall/fence/landscape for residential parking (>10 spaces) screening adjacent to residential district; also minimum height for ECM 2.9.1 screening fence or evergreen hedge\n- **3 acres / parking-between-building-and-street:** Dual thresholds for Subchapter E § 2.3.1.A connectivity requirement\n\n**Exceptions:**\n- Article 10 does not apply to CBD/DMU zoning, structural alterations without increased square footage/area/height, or exclusively residential/care uses listed in § 25-2-1052\n- Compatibility buffer not required for condominium or townhouse residential use, sites with ≤16 residential units only, or NO/LO/LR-zoned sites with ≤40 ft max height (§ 25-2-1062(A), (B)(2))\n- Subchapter E § 2.3.1.A connectivity not required unless site meets net area or parking-location thresholds\n- WO screening requirements apply only within the 16 defined WO subdistricts\n\n## Code References\n\n- LDC § 25-2-1051 — Article 10 Applicability: site must be zoned MF-4 or less restrictive AND within 75 feet of a triggering property\n- LDC § 25-2-1052 — Article 10 Exceptions: CBD/DMU, structural alterations, listed residential/care uses\n- LDC § 25-2-1053 — Triggering Property definition: 1–3 dwelling units, zoned SF-5 or more restrictive\n- LDC § 25-2-1062 — Compatibility Buffers and Setbacks: 25-ft buffer, 15-ft narrow-site buffer, exceptions, NO/LO/LR setback minimums\n- LDC § 25-2-1063 — Screening, Noise, and Design Requirements: lighting shielding, 70 dB(A) noise limit, 15-ft refuse slab setback, 25-ft amenity setback, and screening obligations at triggering property line (current replacement for repealed §§ 25-2-1064, 25-2-1066, 25-2-1067)\n- LDC § 25-6-563 — Parking Area Screening (Common Boundary): 4-ft wall/fence for non-residential parking adj residential; 6-ft screen for residential parking >10 spaces adj residential\n- LDC § 25-2-721(D) — Waterfront Overlay District Parking Requirements: surface parking screened from Town Lake/Colorado River/parkland; parking structure screening and ground-level pedestrian use requirements\n- LDC § 25-2-814 — Service Station Use: street screening by building or landscape buffer with shade trees; 16-dispenser maximum\n- LDC § 25-8-700 — Minimum Requirements for Compatibility Buffers: 10-ft screening zone planting requirements, 15-ft restricted zone use restrictions, Appendix N plant species requirement\n- LDC Subchapter E, § 2.3.1.A — Connectivity Between Sites: property connections to adjacent lots; applicable to non-residential sites ≥3 acres or any site with parking between building and principal street\n- ECM 2.9.1 — General Screening Standards: solid wood and/or masonry fence/wall ≥6 ft; evergreen hedge ≥6 ft mature; or existing vegetation ≥10 ft wide with specified density\n- ECM 2.4.3 — Buffering (Landscape Buffer Composition): ≥2 plant elements, ≤50% single species, ≤¼ buffer absent of elements, 8-ft permeable area minimum\n- ECM 2.8.3 — Waterfront Overlay District Parking Screening: dense massing of trees, shrubs, or wall/vegetative screening; WO sites only\n- BCM 1.2.7 — Building Permit Submittal Requirements: site plan must show dimensions of lot and location/size of proposed building\n- General Site Plan Notes (Compatibility) — Lighting, noise, dumpster setback, and reflective materials requirements applicable to all sites\n\n## Documents to Review\n\n- **Site plan sheets** — Adjacent land uses labeled on all sides; adjacent zoning designations and district boundaries shown; building footprints on adjoining lots within 50 feet shown or noted absent; proposed building dimensions and perimeter boundary line; parking layout relative to building and principal street; property connections to adjacent lots\n- **Mylars** — Zoning designations must be included; compare to site plan sheets for consistency\n- **Landscape / screening plan** — Screening material type (fence vs. vegetation), location along all triggering property lines, compatibility buffer with labeled screening zone (10 ft) and restricted zone (15 ft); composition details (species from ECM Appendix N, spacing, coverage); maintenance notes for existing vegetation; parking lot vegetative screening for WO sites\n- **Photometric report / lighting plan** — Fixture locations relative to residential property lines; fixture cut sheets confirming hooding and shielding; no fixtures above second floor facing residential\n- **Mechanical equipment schedule or specifications** — Equipment noise rating (dB(A)) at property line adjacent to residential; equipment location relative to compatibility buffer screening zone\n- **Parking area details / garage elevations** — Headlight and vehicular movement screening details for parking adjacent to triggering property or residential district; parking garage screening shown on elevation sheets; WO parking screening from waterfront views\n- **Cover sheet general notes / compatibility exhibit** — Verify code citations reference current § 25-2-1063, not repealed sections (25-2-1064, 25-2-1066, 25-2-1067)\n- **Facts.md / project data sheet** — Site zoning (confirm MF-4 or less restrictive for Article 10), adjacent zoning designations, combining district/overlay fields (WO), dwelling unit count on adjacent properties if documented\n\n## Checklist Items\n\n| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n| ZLU-16.1 | Adjoining land uses not noted on site plan for all surrounding properties on all sides (north, south, east, west) | Reviewer Convention | All sites |\n| ZLU-16.2 | Adjoining land uses for properties across adjacent streets and rights-of-way not shown on site plan | Reviewer Convention | All sites |\n| ZLU-16.3 | Vacant adjoining properties not identified as \"vacant\" on site plan | Reviewer Convention | All sites |\n| ZLU-16.4 | Adjacent property zoning district designations not shown on site plan | Reviewer Convention | All sites |\n| ZLU-16.5 | Adjacent zoning designations inaccurate or inconsistent with current City GIS zoning records | Reviewer Convention | All sites |\n| ZLU-16.6 | Zoning district boundary lines not shown or labeled when site contains or abuts multiple zoning districts; sheet(s) where each boundary is depicted not identified | Reviewer Convention | Only when site is subject to or abuts multiple zoning districts. Check: zoning data in facts.md and GIS |\n| ZLU-16.7 | PUD boundaries and land use categories not shown on site plan | Reviewer Convention | Only when site is within or adjacent to a PUD overlay. Check: project data and zoning ordinance |\n| ZLU-16.8 | Zoning designations not included on mylars | Reviewer Convention | All sites |\n| ZLU-16.9 | Existing building footprints on adjoining properties within 50 feet of the site not shown on site plan | Reviewer Convention | All sites |\n| ZLU-16.10 | Note absent from site plan when no buildings exist on adjoining lots within 50 feet (required note: \"no buildings exist on adjoining lots within 50 feet\") | Reviewer Convention | All sites |\n| ZLU-16.11 | Proposed building dimensions not shown on site plan to verify setback and lot coverage compliance | BCM 1.2.7 | All sites |\n| ZLU-16.12 | Continuous perimeter boundary line for complete building footprint — including roofline and any attached canopies — not shown on site plan | BCM 1.2.7 | All sites |\n| ZLU-16.13 | Property connections to adjacent lots not identified and called out on site plan per LDC Subchapter E, § 2.3.1.A — must provide private drive or public street connections to existing drives/streets on adjacent sites, or stub-outs if connections are not feasible | LDC Subchapter E, § 2.3.1.A | Only when: (a) net site area is 3 acres or more in a non-residential zoning district; or (b) parking is located between the building and the principal street in any zoning district. Check: net site area in site data table; parking layout on site plan. Note: the former citation \"BCM §2.3.1.A\" does not exist; this provision is LDC Subchapter E only |\n| ZLU-16.14 | Article 10 compatibility applicability not confirmed or exception conditions not evaluated before applying compatibility standards — verify: (1) subject site is zoned MF-4 or less restrictive; (2) subject site is within 75 feet of a triggering property (1–3 dwelling units, zoned SF-5 or more restrictive: LA, RR, SF-1, SF-2, SF-3, SF-4A, SF-4B, or SF-5); and (3) no § 25-2-1052 exception applies | LDC §§ 25-2-1051, 25-2-1052, 25-2-1053 | Only when site is adjacent to SF-5 or more restrictive zoning or when any Article 10 checklist item is triggered. Check: site zoning in facts.md; adjacent zoning designations; project use type and description for exception conditions |\n| ZLU-16.15 | Compatibility buffer applied without confirming buffer exceptions do not apply — verify use is not condominium or townhouse residential (§ 25-2-1062(A)), site does not include only residential uses with ≤16 dwelling units (§ 25-2-1062(B)(2)(a)), and site is not NO/LO/LR-zoned with maximum zoning height ≤40 feet (§ 25-2-1062(B)(2)(b)) | LDC § 25-2-1062(A), (B)(2) | Only when Article 10 applies and a compatibility buffer is being required. Check: site use type, unit count, and zoning max height |\n| ZLU-16.16 | Compatibility screening not provided along all triggering property lines per LDC § 25-2-1063(E) — must screen vehicle lights, mechanical equipment, outdoor storage, refuse receptacles, and common amenity areas from view of shared property line/alleyway | LDC § 25-2-1063(E) | Only when Article 10 applies: subject site zoned MF-4 or less restrictive, within 75 feet of a triggering property, and no § 25-2-1052 exception applies. Check: site zoning and adjacent zoning/unit count in facts.md; distance from site boundary to nearest triggering property |\n| ZLU-16.17 | Screening material non-compliant: must be (A) a solid wood and/or masonry fence or wall at least 6 feet in height; (B) a hedge-like screen of evergreen plant material capable of attaining 6 feet at maturity, planted at ≤4-foot spacing and ≥2.5 feet at time of planting; or (C) existing vegetation in a strip ≥10 feet wide containing the equivalent of 1 large tree + 2 small trees + 6 understory shrubs per 20 linear feet of screen, with limit-of-construction line clearly marked on plans | ECM 2.9.1 | Only when compatibility screening is required under Article 10. Check: screening material noted on landscape plan |\n| ZLU-16.18 | Vegetative screening composition does not meet ECM 2.4.3 minimum standards: must include at least 2 plant elements, no more than 50% of trees or shrubs from a single species, no more than ¼ of the required buffer area absent of elements, and plants in a permeable landscape area at least 8 feet wide | ECM 2.4.3 | Only when vegetation is the proposed screening method along triggering property lines and ECM 2.4.3 buffering standards are cited as the compliance method. Check: plant schedule and landscape plan |\n| ZLU-16.19 | Existing vegetation proposed as compatibility screening without a plan note confirming the vegetation will be maintained; existing vegetation screen must also be at least 10 feet wide with a limit-of-construction line marked on plans per ECM 2.9.1.C | ECM 2.9.1.C | Only when existing vegetation is proposed to satisfy screening requirements. Check: landscape plan notes and limit-of-construction callout |\n| ZLU-16.20 | Screening fence placement impedes a pedestrian or bicycle access point | LDC § 25-2-1063(F) | Only when a screening fence is proposed along a triggering property line. Check: fence location on site plan relative to sidewalks, bike paths, and pedestrian entries |\n| ZLU-16.21 | On-site amenities available only to residents or occupants (decks, patios, pools, recreation areas) placed within 25 feet of triggering property — multi-use trails are excepted | LDC § 25-2-1063(D) | Only when Article 10 applies. Check: site plan for on-site amenity locations relative to shared triggering property line |\n| ZLU-16.22 | Compatibility buffer along triggering property line not shown, labeled, or provided; required buffer is 25 feet wide (or 15 feet if site is less than 75 feet wide measured from the shared triggering property line) | LDC § 25-2-1062(B); LDC § 25-8-700 | Only when a compatibility buffer is required under Article 10 (no applicable § 25-2-1062 exception). Check: landscape/compatibility plan for buffer zone callouts along triggering property lines |\n| ZLU-16.23 | Compatibility buffer not divided into separately labeled 10-ft screening zone and 15-ft restricted use zone — a single undifferentiated buffer strip does not comply with zone-specific use restrictions under LDC § 25-8-700(D) | LDC § 25-8-700(D) | Only when a compatibility buffer is shown on plans. Check: landscape plan buffer callouts for separate zone labeling |\n| ZLU-16.24 | Mechanical equipment, vertical structures, parking areas, or driveways placed within the 10-foot screening zone of the compatibility buffer — these uses are prohibited in the screening zone and are only permitted in the restricted zone | LDC § 25-8-700(C)(3), (C)(5) | Only when a compatibility buffer is required. Check: site plan and landscape plan for any equipment, paved areas, or structures within the first 10 feet of the buffer measured from the triggering property line |\n| ZLU-16.25 | Compatibility buffer planting not specified from ECM Appendix N Preferred Plant List, or irrigation and maintenance plan not provided for buffer vegetation | LDC § 25-8-700(C)(6) | Only when a compatibility buffer with required planting is provided. Check: plant schedule species names against ECM Appendix N; landscape plan for irrigation notation |\n| ZLU-16.26 | Refuse receptacle concrete slab placed within 15 feet of triggering property | LDC § 25-2-1063(C) | Only when Article 10 applies. Check: site plan for concrete refuse slab location measured from the nearest point of the triggering property line |\n| ZLU-16.27 | Dumpster or permanently placed refuse receptacle located within 20 feet of a property used or zoned SF-5 or more restrictive (LA, RR, SF-1, SF-2, SF-3, SF-4A, SF-4B, or SF-5) | General Site Plan Notes (Compatibility) | All sites with a dumpster or permanently placed refuse receptacle. Check: site plan for refuse location relative to any adjacent SF-5 or more restrictive zoned property. Note: this 20-foot General Notes setback applies to all sites and coexists independently with the 15-foot Article 10 setback (ZLU-16.26) |\n| ZLU-16.28 | Vehicle lights from parking lots or structures, or vehicular movement, not screened from shared triggering property line or alleyway per LDC § 25-2-1063(E)(1) | LDC § 25-2-1063(E)(1) | Only when Article 10 applies and parking or a parking structure is adjacent to or visible from the shared triggering property line. Check: site plan parking layout and screening details at triggering property boundary |\n| ZLU-16.29 | Off-street parking in a non-residential district adjoining a residential district not screened by a wall or fence at least 4 feet in height along the common boundary adequate to screen car lights and vehicular movement | LDC § 25-6-563(A) | Only when non-residential-district parking abuts a residential-district property. Check: site zoning and adjacent zoning on site plan |\n| ZLU-16.30 | Off-street parking of more than 10 spaces in a residential district adjoining another residential-district property not screened by a wall, fence, or landscape screen at least 6 feet in height along the common boundary | LDC § 25-6-563(B) | Only when residential-district parking (more than 10 spaces) abuts another residential-district property. Check: site zoning and parking space count |\n| ZLU-16.31 | Surface parking lot in Waterfront Overlay district not screened from view by dense massing of trees, shrubs, walls, or other vegetative screening per ECM 2.8.3 | ECM 2.8.3 | Only when site is in the Waterfront Overlay combining district (zoning string contains \"WO\" suffix). Check: facts.md combining district field; cover sheet zoning designation |\n| ZLU-16.32 | Surface parking not screened from views of Town Lake (Lady Bird Lake), Colorado River, parkland, or named creeks per LDC § 25-2-721(D)(1)(b) | LDC § 25-2-721(D)(1)(b) | Only when site is in the Waterfront Overlay combining district (zoning string contains \"WO\" suffix). Check: facts.md combining district field and cover sheet zoning |\n| ZLU-16.33 | Service station use not screened from the street by a building or a landscape buffer that includes shade trees | LDC § 25-2-814 | Only when proposed use is a service station. Check: use classification in facts.md or cover sheet; confirm use provides fuel dispensing to motor vehicles (LDC term \"service station\" — informal terms such as \"gas station\" or \"fueling station\" should be flagged for terminology correction) |\n| ZLU-16.34 | Exterior lighting not hooded or shielded so that the light source is not visible from the property line adjacent to residential uses or property zoned residential | LDC § 25-2-1063(A); General Site Plan Notes (Compatibility) | All sites with exterior lighting adjacent to residential uses or residential-zoned property. Under Article 10, this standard applies at the shared triggering property line/alleyway for qualifying sites; the General Site Plan Notes extend the same standard to any adjacent residential use or property zoned residential regardless of Article 10 status. Check: fixture cut sheets and lighting plan notes |\n| ZLU-16.35 | Exterior lighting above the second floor installed in a location facing or adjacent to residential property | General Site Plan Notes (Compatibility) | All sites with exterior lighting adjacent to residential property. Check: lighting plan and building elevation sheets for any luminaires at third floor or higher facing adjacent residential property |\n| ZLU-16.36 | Site plan general notes or compatibility exhibit cites repealed Article 10 sections (LDC 25-2-1064, LDC 25-2-1066, or LDC 25-2-1067) — these sections were repealed effective July 15, 2024 (Ord. No. 20240516-004) and all requirements are now consolidated in LDC § 25-2-1063 | LDC § 25-2-1063 | Only when site is subject to Article 10 compatibility requirements or site plan notes reference compatibility standards. Check: cover sheet general notes and any compatibility exhibit for section number citations |\n| ZLU-16.37 | Mechanical equipment noise level not demonstrated to be at or below 70 dB(A) at the property line adjacent to residential uses | LDC § 25-2-1063(B); General Site Plan Notes (Compatibility) | All sites with mechanical equipment adjacent to residential uses. Under Article 10, measured at the shared triggering property line/alleyway; the General Site Plan Notes extend this standard to any property line adjacent to residential uses. Check: equipment schedule or manufacturer specifications for noise ratings at property line distance |","numLines":162,"startLine":1,"totalLines":162}}}
