{"level":30,"time":1776973731148,"pid":123,"hostname":"844f2260-bff","step":"review-runs","item":"15.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SoFbjM2cJap9EnbD7puf6i","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Setback Requirements and Compatibility Provisions\n     2→\n     3→## Description\n     4→\n     5→This grouping covers all setback and buffer requirements that a site plan must demonstrate compliance with: standard zoning district setbacks (front, rear, side, street side), Austin's compatibility standards for development near SF-5-or-more-restrictive-zoned properties (Article 10 height limits, buffers, and screening), waterfront overlay subdistrict setbacks, Lake Austin district shoreline setbacks, special ordinance/CO/PUD/NCCD setbacks, and miscellaneous clearances (hazardous pipelines, open waterways, fire access, transformer pads). It also covers the procedural steps required when setback relief is needed (BOA variances, site-specific amendments, letters of non-enforcement) and fence height and design limits at property lines. A reviewer examining this grouping checks both that required setback lines are correctly drawn and labeled on plans and that no improvements violate those lines.\n     6→\n     7→## Regulatory Overview\n     8→\n     9→**Standard Zoning Setbacks.** LDC § 25-2-492 establishes minimum front, street side, interior side, and rear yard setbacks for every base zoning district. For industrial districts (IP, MI, LI), yard setbacks adjacent to residential, office, and commercial uses are governed by the adjacency-based table in § 25-2-601 (50 ft adjacent to LA, RR, or SF-1 through SF-6; 25 ft adjacent to MF-1 through MF-6 or MH; 15 ft adjacent to NO, LO, GO, CR, LR, or GR; 10 ft adjacent to L or less restrictive). Where a Subchapter provision conflicts with § 25-2-492, the more restrictive regulation governs (§ 25-2-492(B)). All required yards must remain open and unobstructed from finished grade to sky (§ 25-2-513(A)); through lots must apply the front yard minimum to both street frontages (§ 25-2-515). Special overlay designations—conditional overlays (CO), planned unit developments (PUD), and neighborhood conservation and combining districts (NCCD)—can impose site-specific setbacks that override or supplement the base district table; these requirements appear only in the individual ordinance or development agreement, not in the LDC tables.\n    10→\n    11→**Compatibility Standards (Article 10 — Effective July 15, 2024).** Article 10 was completely repealed and replaced by Ord. No. 20240516-004 effective July 15, 2024. The former Article 10 used four distance bands at 50, 100, 300, and 540 feet from a triggering property and included a compatibility waiver mechanism (§§ 25-2-1081/1082). **None of that framework survives; any plan citing those distance bands, those section numbers, or \"compatibility waiver\" for work submitted on or after July 15, 2024 is relying on repealed authority.** The current Article 10 (§§ 25-2-1051–25-2-1063) applies when a site is (1) zoned MF-4 or any less restrictive district (MF-5, MF-6, or any commercial or industrial zone) AND (2) located within 75 feet of a **triggering property**. A triggering property (§ 25-2-1053) is a site that has at least one but fewer than four dwelling units AND is zoned **SF-5 or more restrictive**. The qualifying zones for \"SF-5 or more restrictive\" are: **LA, RR, SF-1, SF-2, SF-3, SF-4A, SF-4B, and SF-5**. SF-6 is less restrictive than SF-5 and does NOT create a triggering property. Historic designation (H) is a combining district that does not appear in the § 25-2-1052 exceptions list; H-designated sites are not exempt from Article 10. Article 10 does not apply to: sites zoned CBD or DMU; structural alterations that do not increase square footage, area, or height; and sites used exclusively for duplex, single-family attached, single-family residential, small-lot SF, two-unit, three-unit, adult care services, or child care services (§ 25-2-1052). There is no small-site/large-site distinction under the current framework. Current relief paths: BOA variance with hardship findings (§§ 25-2-472/474) or site-specific amendment treated as a rezoning (§ 25-2-1054; not available for special purpose base districts, DB combining districts, or UNO sites). The current compatibility standards impose: (a) **height limits** (§ 25-2-1061): structures < 50 ft from triggering property boundary ≤ 40 ft; 50–74 ft ≤ 60 ft; ≥ 75 ft = base zoning; (b) **compatibility buffer** (§ 25-2-1062(B)): 25-ft landscaped buffer along the shared property line, reduced to 15 ft if the site is < 75 ft wide measured from the shared triggering property line, complying with § 25-8-700 planting requirements; buffer is not required for condominium or townhouse residential use (§ 25-2-1062(A)), sites with only residential uses with ≤ 16 dwelling units, or NO/LO/LR-zoned sites with max zoning height ≤ 40 ft (§ 25-2-1062(B)(2)); (c) **setbacks** (§ 25-2-1062(C)): for NO, LO, LR districts, 10-ft rear yard setback from the shared triggering property line; LR only: additional 5-ft interior side setback; (d) **screening, noise, and design** (§ 25-2-1063): lighting hooded/shielded so light source not visible from shared property line; mechanical equipment noise ≤ 70 dB at shared property line; refuse receptacle concrete slab ≥ 15 ft from triggering property; on-site amenities available to residents/occupants only ≥ 25 ft from triggering property (multi-use trails excepted); vehicle lights from parking facilities, ground floor and rooftop mechanical equipment, outdoor storage, refuse receptacles and collection areas, and common amenity areas (decks, patios, pools) must be screened from the shared property line or alleyway except through pedestrian/bicycle access points. Per § 25-8-700, the first 10 ft of the buffer from the shared property line is the **screening zone** (vertical structures, parking, driveways, and mechanical equipment prohibited; paths, landscaping, utility infrastructure, and ground-floor personal open space permitted); the next 15 ft is the **restricted zone** (surface parking, driveways, fire lanes, utility infrastructure, and refuse receptacles permitted; vertical structures generally prohibited except per § 25-8-700(C)(4)).\n    12→\n    13→**Waterfront Overlay and Shoreline Setbacks.** The Waterfront Overlay (WO) combining district (\"WO\" suffix in zoning designation) applies to properties along Town Lake/Lady Bird Lake and the Colorado River, as defined in LDC Appendix B. The WO district consists of 16 geographically defined subdistricts. PARD director review is required before site plan approval for all WO sites (§ 25-2-721(A)(2)). Primary setback distances vary by subdistrict and must be shown and maintained on plans:\n    14→\n    15→| Subdistrict | From Town Lake shoreline | Creek setbacks |\n    16→|---|---|---|\n    17→| North Shore Central (§ 25-2-738) | 100 ft | 60 ft from Shoal Creek centerline; 50 ft from Waller Creek centerline |\n    18→| Rainey Street (§ 25-2-739) | 150 ft | 50 ft from Waller Creek centerline |\n    19→| South Shore Central (§ 25-2-742) | 150 ft | 80 ft from East Bouldin Creek centerline; 35 ft north of Riverside Drive ROW |\n    20→| South Lakeshore (§ 25-2-741) | 65 ft | 50 ft south of Lakeshore Blvd |\n    21→| East Riverside (§ 25-2-734) | 100 ft | — |\n    22→\n    23→There is no single \"Lady Bird Lake = 150 ft\" rule; the applicable distance depends on the specific subdistrict. Several WO subdistrict boundaries (South Lakeshore, Travis Heights, Auditorium Shores, Butler Shores) reference lot lines as they existed in 1986; boundary determination for recently reconfigured parcels in those subdistricts may require historical plat research. Separately, the Lake Austin (LA) zoning district sets its own shoreline setback under § 25-2-551(B): 75 ft from the 492.8-ft contour line (the shoreline), reduced to 25 ft for lots in pre-April 22, 1982 recorded subdivisions where the shoreline to the front lot line is ≤ 200 ft. The CWQZ (§ 25-8-92) is a separate environmental requirement establishing a 100-ft Critical Water Quality Zone from the Lady Bird Lake 429.0-ft contour and from the Lake Austin 492.8-ft contour (75 ft for detached single-family residential); these are distinct environmental setbacks from the LA district or WO setbacks. In WO primary setback areas, parking is generally prohibited (§ 25-2-721(B)(1)). In the secondary setback area, impervious cover may not exceed 30% (§ 25-2-721(B)(2)).\n    24→\n    25→**Other Setbacks and Common Violation Patterns.** A **hazardous pipeline** (§ 25-2-516(A)) must meet two criteria: ≥ 8-inch inside diameter AND designed to carry a hazardous liquid per 49 CFR § 195.2 (petroleum, petroleum products, anhydrous ammonia, ethanol). Natural gas pipelines are not hazardous pipelines under this definition. Uses requiring evacuation assistance (congregate living, hospitals, medical offices > 5,000 sq ft GFA, schools, large-site retirement housing, commercial day care, etc.) are prohibited within 500 ft of a hazardous pipeline; placement within 200–500 ft requires City Council resolution after Fire Chief recommendation (§ 25-2-516(B)). New construction within 200 ft requires Fire Chief determination that the project meets performance-based design standards or FPCM construction requirements (§ 25-2-516(C)). *Common violation pattern:* designers omit City Council resolution or Fire Chief approval letter when proposing evacuation-assistance uses within 200–500 ft; check the AFD Fire Analysis Table \"City of Austin Hazardous Pipeline Ordinance\" field (Yes/No) on the cover sheet. *Common violation patterns in compatibility reviews:* (1) post-July 2024 plans still cite § 25-2-1081/1082, \"compatibility waiver,\" or Planning Commission compatibility waiver approvals — these are all repealed; (2) triggering property incorrectly evaluated using the pre-2024 \"shared lot line\" standard rather than the current \"within 75 feet\" test; (3) SF-6 incorrectly treated as a triggering property (SF-6 is less restrictive than SF-5 and does not qualify); (4) Historic (H) designation incorrectly claimed as an Article 10 exception (H is not listed in § 25-2-1052). *Transformer pad:* using the 5-ft brick/masonry clearance standard for non-masonry buildings when the 12-ft non-masonry standard applies is a common plan deficiency.\n    26→\n    27→**Key Thresholds:**\n    28→- Article 10 applicability: site zoned MF-4 or less restrictive + within 75 ft of triggering property (LDC § 25-2-1051)\n    29→- Triggering property: 1–3 dwelling units + zoned SF-5 or more restrictive — qualifying zones: LA, RR, SF-1, SF-2, SF-3, SF-4A, SF-4B, SF-5 only (LDC § 25-2-1053)\n    30→- Compatibility height limits: < 50 ft from triggering property = 40 ft max; 50–74 ft = 60 ft max; ≥ 75 ft = base zoning height (LDC § 25-2-1061)\n    31→- Compatibility buffer widths: 25 ft standard; 15 ft where site is < 75 ft wide measured from shared triggering property line (LDC § 25-2-1062(B))\n    32→- Buffer screening zone (10 ft): vertical structures, parking, driveways, mechanical equipment prohibited (LDC § 25-8-700)\n    33→- Refuse receptacle concrete slab: ≥ 15 ft from triggering property (LDC § 25-2-1063(C))\n    34→- On-site amenity setback: ≥ 25 ft from triggering property (LDC § 25-2-1063(D))\n    35→- Noise limit: mechanical equipment ≤ 70 dB at shared property line (LDC § 25-2-1063(B))\n    36→- Fence heights: 6 ft max at standard; 7 ft where grade change ≥ 1 ft along fence run; 8 ft under § 25-2-899(F)(G)(H) conditions (LDC § 25-2-899)\n    37→- Hazardous pipeline qualifying criteria: ≥ 8-inch inside diameter + hazardous liquid per 49 CFR § 195.2 (LDC § 25-2-516(A))\n    38→- Parking facility fence: ≥ 4 ft where nonresidential parking facility adjoins residential district (LDC § 25-6-563(A)); ≥ 6 ft where residential parking facility with > 10 spaces adjoins residential property (LDC § 25-6-563(B))\n    39→- LA district shoreline setback: 75 ft from 492.8-ft contour; 25 ft for pre-1982 lots where shoreline to front lot line ≤ 200 ft (LDC § 25-2-551(B))\n    40→\n    41→**Exceptions:**\n    42→- Article 10 does not apply: CBD or DMU zoning; structural alterations without size/height increase; duplex, SF attached, SF residential, small-lot SF, two-unit, three-unit, adult care, or child care uses (LDC § 25-2-1052)\n    43→- Compatibility buffer not required even when Article 10 applies: condominium or townhouse residential use; sites with only residential uses ≤ 16 DUs; sites zoned NO/LO/LR with max zoning height ≤ 40 ft (LDC § 25-2-1062(A)(B)(2))\n    44→- Compatibility waiver (§§ 25-2-1081/1082): REPEALED July 15, 2024 — not available for any submittal on or after that date\n    45→\n    46→## Code References\n    47→\n    48→- LDC § 25-2-472 — Board of Adjustment variance authority over Chapter 25-2 requirements, including Article 10\n    49→- LDC § 25-2-474 — Required findings for BOA variances (hardship, uniqueness, no harm to adjacent properties)\n    50→- LDC § 25-2-492 — Zoning district setback table (front, rear, interior side, street side by district)\n    51→- LDC § 25-2-513 — Required yards must be open and unobstructed from finished grade to sky; limited exceptions for eaves, steps, stoops, disability ramps, landscaping\n    52→- LDC § 25-2-515 — Through lots: front yard setback applied to rear yard\n    53→- LDC § 25-2-516 — Development near a hazardous pipeline: definitions, 200-ft and 500-ft zones, evacuation-assistance use restrictions, FPCM construction requirements\n    54→- LDC § 25-2-551 — Lake Austin (LA) district regulations, including 75-ft (or 25-ft for pre-1982 lots) shoreline setback from 492.8-ft contour\n    55→- LDC § 25-2-601 — Industrial district (IP, MI, LI) setbacks adjacent to residential, office, and commercial uses\n    56→- LDC § 25-2-712 — Waterfront Overlay definitions (primary setback area, secondary setback area)\n    57→- LDC § 25-2-721 — Waterfront Overlay combining district regulations: PARD review, primary and secondary setback area use restrictions, parking prohibition, impervious cover limits\n    58→- LDC § 25-2-734 — East Riverside WO subdistrict regulations (100-ft primary setback from Town Lake shoreline)\n    59→- LDC § 25-2-738 — North Shore Central WO subdistrict regulations (100-ft from Town Lake; 60-ft from Shoal Creek; 50-ft from Waller Creek)\n    60→- LDC § 25-2-739 — Rainey Street WO subdistrict regulations (150-ft from Town Lake; 50-ft from Waller Creek)\n    61→- LDC § 25-2-741 — South Lakeshore WO subdistrict regulations (65-ft from Town Lake; 50-ft south of Lakeshore Blvd)\n    62→- LDC § 25-2-742 — South Shore Central WO subdistrict regulations (150-ft from Town Lake; 80-ft from East Bouldin Creek; 35-ft north of Riverside Drive ROW)\n    63→- LDC § 25-2-899 — Fences as accessory uses: height limits (6 ft standard, 7 ft grade-change, 8 ft under specific conditions); July 2023 design restrictions\n    64→- LDC § 25-2-1051 — Compatibility Standards (Article 10) applicability: site zoned MF-4 or less restrictive + within 75 ft of triggering property (eff. July 15, 2024)\n    65→- LDC § 25-2-1052 — Exceptions to compatibility standards: CBD/DMU, structural alterations, certain residential uses (eff. July 15, 2024)\n    66→- LDC § 25-2-1053 — Triggering property definition: 1–3 dwelling units + SF-5 or more restrictive (eff. July 15, 2024)\n    67→- LDC § 25-2-1054 — Site-specific amendments to Article 10 height and buffer requirements: treated as a rezoning; not available for special purpose base districts, DB combining districts, or UNO sites\n    68→- LDC § 25-2-1061 — Compatibility height limits: < 50 ft = 40 ft max; 50–74 ft = 60 ft max; ≥ 75 ft = base zoning (eff. July 15, 2024)\n    69→- LDC § 25-2-1062 — Compatibility buffers and setbacks: 25-ft buffer along shared triggering property line (15 ft for narrow sites); planting per § 25-8-700; setbacks for NO/LO/LR districts; exceptions (eff. July 15, 2024)\n    70→- LDC § 25-2-1063 — Screening, noise, and design requirements: lighting, 70-dB noise limit, refuse receptacle placement, amenity setback, comprehensive screening list (eff. July 15, 2024)\n    71→- LDC § 25-2-1205 — Site development regulations for mobile home parks, including minimum 6-ft boundary fence requirement\n    72→- LDC § 25-5-146 — Conditions of approval for conditional use site plans: 200-ft parking separation for late-hours cocktail lounge/restaurant\n    73→- LDC § 25-6-563 — Screening requirements for off-street parking facilities adjoining residential districts\n    74→- LDC § 25-8-92 — Critical Water Quality Zones established (Lady Bird Lake, Lake Austin, other watersheds)\n    75→- LDC § 25-8-700 — Minimum requirements for compatibility buffers: screening zone and restricted zone composition, planting requirements, vegetation standards\n    76→- Austin Energy Design Criteria 1.10.4 — Clearances from padmount transformer equipment\n    77→- Ordinance 030605-24 — 50-foot setback from centerline of open waterways (not verified in Bureau; preserve as active requirement pending confirmation)\n    78→\n    79→## Documents to Review\n    80→\n    81→- **Site plan sheets** — Verify all required setback lines (zoning, compatibility, waterfront, LA shoreline, special ordinance) are drawn, labeled, dimensioned, and that no buildings, structures, parking areas, or driveways violate them\n    82→- **Landscape plan sheets** — Verify compatibility buffer lines are shown with screening/planting notations; confirm dimensions match site plan; confirm § 25-8-700 plantings shown at 25 LF intervals within the screening zone\n    83→- **Compatibility exhibit (separate sheet)** — Verify property boundaries, on-site and off-site zoning boundaries (all triggering property zones and applicable overlays), distances from triggering property line to existing and proposed buildings, and surface improvements are shown\n    84→- **Compatibility cross-section sheets** — Verify height vs. distance relationships from triggering property boundary with distance increments (50 ft, 75 ft) and building elevations labeled to confirm §25-2-1061 compliance\n    85→- **Elevation sheets** — Verify building heights, setback dimensions from property lines, and parapets labeled\n    86→- **Recorded plat / survey** — Check for any platted setback lines that differ from standard code requirements\n    87→- **Project data / facts sheet (zoning field)** — Identify zoning district, COs, PUDs, NCCDs, waterfront overlay subdistrict, and proximity to triggering properties to determine which setback sets apply\n    88→- **Pipeline easement documentation / utility plan / AFD Fire Analysis Table** — Verify proximity to hazardous pipelines (check \"City of Austin Hazardous Pipeline Ordinance: Yes/No\" field) and open waterways\n    89→- **Cover sheet approval block** — Confirm plan submission date to determine whether pre- or post-July 15, 2024 Article 10 applies\n    90→\n    91→## Checklist Items\n    92→\n    93→| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n    94→|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n    95→| ZLU-15.1 | Required zoning district setback lines (front yard, rear yard, interior side yard, street side yard) not shown, labeled, or dimensioned on the site plan | LDC § 25-2-492 | All sites |\n    96→| ZLU-15.2 | Zoning district setback distances not maintained: buildings, structures, or improvements encroach into required front, rear, interior side, or street side setbacks per the applicable district table; industrial districts use the adjacency-based table for interior and rear yards adjacent to residential, office, and commercial uses | LDC § 25-2-492; LDC § 25-2-601 | All sites |\n    97→| ZLU-15.3 | Shoreline or CWQZ setback not shown and labeled on site plan: (a) Lake Austin (LA) district shoreline setback — 75 ft from the 492.8-ft topographic contour line (the shoreline); or 25 ft for lots in pre-April 22, 1982 recorded subdivisions where shoreline to front lot line is ≤ 200 ft — per LDC § 25-2-551(B); and (b) Critical Water Quality Zone (CWQZ) — 100 ft from Lady Bird Lake 429.0-ft contour or Lake Austin 492.8-ft contour (75 ft for detached single-family residential) — per LDC § 25-8-92. These are two distinct setback frameworks; both may apply simultaneously on the same site | LDC § 25-2-551(B); LDC § 25-8-92 | Only when site has shoreline frontage on Lady Bird Lake or Lake Austin, or is zoned LA. Check: zoning field and site location in facts.md; confirm which of the two setback frameworks applies based on zoning and shoreline presence |\n    98→| ZLU-15.4 | Through lot not applying the front yard setback requirement to the rear yard per LDC § 25-2-515 | LDC § 25-2-515 | Only when site is a through lot (fronts on two parallel streets). Check: survey and street layout on site plan |\n    99→| ZLU-15.5 | Dumpster enclosures not maintaining required setbacks from property lines per the applicable zoning district setback table | LDC § 25-2-492 | Only when dumpster enclosures are proposed. Check: site plan for dumpster locations relative to property lines |\n   100→| ZLU-15.6 | Fences proposed within required building setback lines in violation of applicable zoning restrictions | LDC § 25-2-899 | Only when fences are proposed near building setback boundaries. Check: fence locations versus labeled setback lines on site plan |\n   101→| ZLU-15.7 | Compatibility standards (Article 10) applicability incorrectly determined: Article 10 applies when a site is zoned MF-4 or less restrictive AND is located within 75 feet of a triggering property; a triggering property is a site with 1–3 dwelling units zoned SF-5 or more restrictive (LA, RR, SF-1, SF-2, SF-3, SF-4A, SF-4B, or SF-5); SF-6 is NOT more restrictive than SF-5 and does not create a triggering property; Historic (H) designation is not an exception under § 25-2-1052; sites zoned CBD or DMU are exempt; the proximity test is within 75 feet, not a shared lot line | LDC § 25-2-1051; LDC § 25-2-1052; LDC § 25-2-1053 | Only when site is zoned MF-4 or less restrictive. Check: Zoning field in facts.md or plan cover sheet for MF-4, MF-5, MF-6, or commercial/industrial base district designation; then check adjacent property zoning to identify any SF-5-or-more-restrictive parcels with 1–3 dwelling units within 75 ft of the site boundary |\n   102→| ZLU-15.8 | Compatibility height limits not shown or not maintained under Article 10: structures located less than 50 feet from any part of a triggering property may not exceed 40 feet in height; structures located 50 to 74 feet from a triggering property may not exceed 60 feet in height; structures at 75 feet or more from a triggering property are subject only to base zoning height; distance is measured from the triggering property boundary | LDC § 25-2-1061 | Only when compatibility is triggered. Check: site plan and elevation sheets for distance measurements from triggering property boundary to proposed structures and labeled building heights |\n   103→| ZLU-15.9 | Compatibility exhibit does not show the approximate location of existing structures on the triggering property; this information is necessary to verify the distance measurement from the triggering property boundary and to document compliance with the height-limit distance bands under § 25-2-1061 when proposed structures are positioned near the 50-ft and 75-ft thresholds | Reviewer Convention | Only when compatibility is triggered and structures are proposed within 75 ft of the triggering property boundary. Check: compatibility exhibit or site plan for triggering property structure notation |\n   104→| ZLU-15.10 | Compatibility buffer not shown at the correct width along the shared triggering property line: the required buffer is 25 feet wide (standard) or 15 feet wide if the development site is less than 75 feet wide measured from the shared triggering property line; the buffer must be shown as a landscaped zone complying with § 25-8-700 planting requirements and must extend along the full length of the property line shared with a triggering property; the old multi-distance band framework (50/100/300/540 ft) from the pre-July 2024 Article 10 does not apply | LDC § 25-2-1062(B); LDC § 25-8-700 | Only when compatibility is triggered and the compatibility buffer is required (not exempt under § 25-2-1062(A)(B)(2)). Check: site plan and landscape sheets for buffer width notation along shared triggering property line |\n   105→| ZLU-15.11 | Compatibility buffer line mislabeled (e.g., labeled \"No Build Zone\" instead of \"Compatibility Buffer\"), undimensioned, or not applied along all property lines where a triggering property shares the line on multiple sides | LDC § 25-2-1062(B) | Only when compatibility is triggered. Check: labels and dimensions on each compatibility buffer line shown on site plan and landscape sheets |\n   106→| ZLU-15.12 | Compatibility buffer lines incorrectly applied adjacent to areas where no triggering property exists — e.g., adjacent to transmission line easements, access easements, open space areas, or commercial properties that are not SF-5-or-more-restrictive-zoned with 1–3 dwelling units; the buffer is required only along property lines shared with qualifying triggering properties | LDC § 25-2-1051; LDC § 25-2-1053 | Only when compatibility is triggered. Check: that buffer lines terminate where the adjacent property does not qualify as a triggering property |\n   107→| ZLU-15.13 | Triggering property or properties not clearly identified on the site plan compatibility sheets; adjacent properties that trigger compatibility requirements must be labeled to confirm they qualify as triggering properties under § 25-2-1053 | LDC § 25-2-1053; LDC § 25-2-1063 | Only when compatibility is triggered. Check: compatibility exhibit or site plan for triggering property notation including zoning label and dwelling unit count |\n   108→| ZLU-15.14 | Property line not graphically prominent and clearly distinguishable as the measurement datum for compatibility buffer and height limit compliance; required screening vegetation along the compatibility buffer screening zone not shown on plans or not included in the plan legend per § 25-8-700 | LDC § 25-2-1062; LDC § 25-8-700 | Only when compatibility is triggered and buffer is required. Check: plan legend and property line depiction on compatibility and landscape sheets |\n   109→| ZLU-15.15 | Proposed improvements within the 10-foot screening zone of the compatibility buffer that are prohibited by § 25-8-700: vertical structures (buildings, covered structures, walls not functioning as screening berms), parking areas, driveways, or mechanical equipment within the first 10 feet from the shared triggering property line; the screening zone must consist of landscaping, fences/berms, paths/trails, utility infrastructure, and ground-floor personal open space only; relief from this requirement requires a BOA variance (§ 25-2-472) or site-specific amendment (§ 25-2-1054) | LDC § 25-2-1062(B); LDC § 25-8-700 | Only when compatibility is triggered and buffer is required. Check: site plan and grading plan for impervious or structural elements within 10 ft of the triggering property line |\n   110→| ZLU-15.16 | Covered structures available to residents or occupants (covered bike parking areas, covered patio areas, covered recreational facilities) proposed within 25 feet of the triggering property line without meeting an exception or obtaining valid current relief; on-site amenities available only to residents/occupants and guests may not be located within 25 feet of triggering property per § 25-2-1063(D); additionally, covered structures constitute vertical structures prohibited within the 10-foot screening zone per § 25-8-700; relief requires a BOA variance (§ 25-2-472) or site-specific amendment (§ 25-2-1054) | LDC § 25-2-1063(D); LDC § 25-8-700 | Only when compatibility is triggered and covered structures are proposed within 25 feet of triggering property line. Check: site plan for covered amenity structures within the buffer zone |\n   111→| ZLU-15.17 | Above-grade drainage features (level spreaders with concrete footings, detention structures with above-grade walls, concrete drainage channels) proposed within the 10-foot screening zone of the compatibility buffer, in conflict with § 25-8-700's prohibition on vertical structures in the screening zone; ground-level drainage features not constituting vertical structures may be acceptable if they do not interfere with required buffer planting; relief for above-grade structures requires a BOA variance (§ 25-2-472) or site-specific amendment (§ 25-2-1054) | LDC § 25-2-1062(B); LDC § 25-8-700 | Only when compatibility is triggered and drainage features are proposed near the triggering property line. Check: grading and drainage plan against the 10-ft screening zone boundary |\n   112→| ZLU-15.18 | On-site amenities (bicycle racks, swimming pools, tennis courts, ball courts, playgrounds, outdoor gyms) available only to residents, occupants, and their guests placed within 25 feet of the triggering property line; multi-use trails open to the public are excepted | LDC § 25-2-1063(D) | Only when compatibility is triggered and amenities are proposed. Check: amenity locations on site plan against the 25-foot distance from the triggering property boundary |\n   113→| ZLU-15.19 | Parking area or drive aisle within the 25-foot compatibility buffer not redesigned or relocated: the 25-foot buffer must be maintained as a landscaped zone complying with § 25-8-700; parking is prohibited within the 10-foot screening zone; additionally, vehicle lights from parking lots and structures must be screened so they are not visible from the shared triggering property line or alleyway | LDC § 25-2-1062(B); LDC § 25-2-1063(E)(1); LDC § 25-8-700 | Only when compatibility is triggered and parking or drive aisles are proposed near the triggering property line. Check: parking layout and landscaping plan against the 25-foot buffer and screening requirements |\n   114→| ZLU-15.20 | Exterior lighting not hooded or shielded to prevent the light source from being visible from the shared property line or alleyway with the triggering property | LDC § 25-2-1063(A) | Only when compatibility is triggered. Check: lighting plan notes and fixture specifications for hooding/shielding requirement at the shared property line |\n   115→| ZLU-15.21 | Mechanical equipment (HVAC units, cooling towers, generators, exhaust fans) proposed to produce sound exceeding 70 decibels measured at the shared property line or alleyway with the triggering property | LDC § 25-2-1063(B) | Only when compatibility is triggered and mechanical equipment is proposed. Check: mechanical equipment noise calculations or acoustical notes on plans relative to shared property line |\n   116→| ZLU-15.22 | Required elements not screened from the shared triggering property line or alleyway: vehicle lights from parking lots or structures; ground floor and rooftop mechanical equipment; outdoor storage; refuse receptacles and refuse collection areas; or common amenity areas (decks, patios, pools) are visible from the shared property line; screening must prevent visibility except through designated pedestrian or bicycle access points; rooftop mechanical equipment may be screened by a parapet | LDC § 25-2-1063(E); LDC § 25-2-1063(G) | Only when compatibility is triggered. Check: site plan, landscape plan, and elevation sheets for screening provisions at the shared property line |\n   117→| ZLU-15.23 | Parking area for a late-hours cocktail lounge or restaurant not separated from property used or zoned SF-6 or more restrictive by a minimum of 200 feet as a condition of conditional use site plan approval; exceptions require (1) the lounge/restaurant is in an enclosed shopping center, or (2) the Land Use Commission granted a variance at site plan approval | LDC § 25-5-146(B) | Only when site has a cocktail lounge or restaurant use with a late-hours permit as part of a conditional use site plan. Check: use designation, conditional use permit, and parking lot boundary distance from nearest SF-6 or more restrictive property |\n   118→| ZLU-15.24 | Required minimum 4-foot fence or wall not provided along the common boundary between an off-street parking facility in a nonresidential zoning district and an adjoining residential zoning district; or fence placed off the property line without documented justification that property-line placement would harm existing vegetation, interfere with drainage, or create a utility conflict per § 25-6-563(C)/(D) | LDC § 25-6-563(A) | Only when an off-street parking facility in a nonresidential district abuts a residential district boundary. Check: parking lot boundary treatment on landscape or site plan |\n   119→| ZLU-15.25 | Required minimum 6-foot screening (wall, fence, or landscape) not provided along the common boundary where a parking facility containing more than 10 spaces in a residential zoning district adjoins another residential district property | LDC § 25-6-563(B) | Only when a parking facility with more than 10 spaces in a residential district adjoins another residential property. Check: parking facility boundary treatment and space count on site plan |\n   120→| ZLU-15.26 | Separate compatibility exhibit not provided or incomplete: the exhibit must show property boundaries, on-site and off-site zoning boundaries (including all triggering properties and applicable combining districts), distances from triggering property boundary to existing and proposed structures, distances to surface improvements (parking, driveways, drainage features), and the location of existing structures on triggering properties; this documentation is required to demonstrate compliance with §§ 25-2-1061 (height limits), 25-2-1062 (buffer), and 25-2-1063 (screening) | Reviewer Convention | Only when compatibility is triggered. Check: whether a standalone compatibility exhibit sheet exists in the plan set and contains all required information |\n   121→| ZLU-15.27 | Compatibility cross-sections not provided or incomplete: cross-sections must show the triggering property boundary, distance measurements from the triggering property boundary to proposed structures at the 50-ft and 75-ft thresholds, proposed building heights at each distance band, and elevation of the triggering property grade; these are required to demonstrate compliance with the § 25-2-1061 height limit bands (< 50 ft = 40-ft max; 50–74 ft = 60-ft max) | LDC § 25-2-1061 | Only when compatibility is triggered and structures are proposed within 75 ft of the triggering property boundary. Check: cross-section sheet(s) for labeled distances and building heights at both threshold distances |\n   122→| ZLU-15.28 | Waterfront overlay subdistrict setback not shown, labeled, or maintained on site plan: applicable primary setback distances vary by subdistrict as follows — North Shore Central: 100 ft from Town Lake shoreline, 60 ft from Shoal Creek centerline, 50 ft from Waller Creek centerline; Rainey Street: 150 ft from Town Lake shoreline, 50 ft from Waller Creek centerline; South Shore Central: 150 ft from Town Lake shoreline, 80 ft from East Bouldin Creek centerline, 35 ft north of Riverside Drive ROW; South Lakeshore: 65 ft from Town Lake shoreline, 50 ft south of Lakeshore Boulevard; East Riverside: 100 ft from Town Lake shoreline; setback distances for other WO subdistricts must be verified against the applicable subsection of LDC §§ 25-2-731 through 25-2-746; there is no single \"Lady Bird Lake = 150 ft\" rule; the applicable distance depends entirely on which subdistrict the site is located in | LDC § 25-2-738; LDC § 25-2-739; LDC § 25-2-741; LDC § 25-2-742; LDC § 25-2-734 | Only when site is within a waterfront overlay (WO) combining district subdistrict. Check: overlay subdistrict designation in facts.md zoning field (WO suffix) and applicable subdistrict identification on cover sheet or GIS |\n   123→| ZLU-15.29 | Gross square footage contained within the waterfront overlay primary setback area and secondary setback area not computed and shown; percentage of total site area within the primary setback not shown | LDC § 25-2-721 | Only when site is within a waterfront overlay subdistrict. Check: plan sheets for setback area tabulation |\n   124→| ZLU-15.30 | Impervious coverage within the waterfront overlay secondary setback area exceeds 30% per § 25-2-721(B)(2); no parking structures, parking areas, or surface parking permitted within the primary setback area except for public transportation, handicapped, or park access uses | LDC § 25-2-721(B) | Only when site is within a waterfront overlay subdistrict. Check: impervious cover calculations within the primary and secondary setback zones |\n   125→| ZLU-15.31 | Conditional overlay (CO) setback requirements not shown on site plan or setback distances not maintained per the applicable conditional ordinance | Per CO ordinance conditions | Only when site has a CO designation with specific setback conditions. Check: CO ordinance conditions listed in facts.md or on plan cover sheet |\n   126→| ZLU-15.32 | PUD-required setback lines not shown on site plan per the applicable planned unit development approval document | Per PUD ordinance conditions | Only when site is within a PUD. Check: PUD ordinance for required setbacks; verify setbacks shown on plans |\n   127→| ZLU-15.33 | NCCD setback requirements not shown or not met on site plan per the applicable neighborhood conservation and combining district ordinance | Per NCCD ordinance conditions | Only when site is within an NCCD boundary. Check: NCCD ordinance conditions for setback requirements; verify shown on plans; note that NCCD ordinance text must be obtained from the applicant or City staff as it is not available in Bureau |\n   128→| ZLU-15.34 | Named ordinance setback requirements (site-specific setbacks from particular property lines established by recorded ordinance, e.g., 50-ft from southern property line, 35-ft landscape buffer from highway ROW) not shown on plans or not maintained | Per recorded ordinance conditions | Only when site has a recorded ordinance imposing specific setbacks beyond standard code minimums. Check: ordinance conditions in facts.md and plan cover sheet data |\n   129→| ZLU-15.35 | Uses requiring evacuation assistance proposed within 500 feet of a hazardous pipeline without required approval: uses within 200 feet are prohibited as new construction; uses between 200 and 500 feet require City Council resolution following a Fire Chief recommendation based on performance-based design, early detection, and engineer certification per § 25-2-516(B); qualifying uses include: congregate living, convalescent services, detention facilities, commercial day care, hospitals (general/limited), medical offices exceeding 5,000 sq ft GFA, private/public primary and secondary educational facilities, and large-site retirement housing. Violation pattern: plan proposes qualifying use within 200–500 ft range with no Council resolution or Fire Chief approval letter in the submittal; check cover sheet AFD Fire Analysis Table \"City of Austin Hazardous Pipeline Ordinance\" field (Yes/No) | LDC § 25-2-516(B) | Only when site is within 500 feet of a hazardous pipeline (≥ 8-inch inside diameter carrying hazardous liquid per 49 CFR § 195.2). Check: AFD Fire Analysis Table on cover sheet, pipeline easement location on utility plan or survey, and proposed use type |\n   130→| ZLU-15.36 | New construction proposed within 200 feet of a hazardous pipeline without demonstrating compliance with Fire Protection Criteria Manual (FPCM) construction requirements or obtaining a Fire Chief determination that the project meets performance-based design standards; the FPCM establishes distance-based tiers (< 50 ft, 50–100 ft, 100–150 ft, 150–200 ft) with construction type and opening requirements that vary by proximity and whether an easement barrier wall is present | LDC § 25-2-516(C) | Only when new construction is proposed within 200 feet of a qualifying hazardous pipeline. Check: utility plan for pipeline location, distance from centerline to proposed structures, and presence of Fire Chief determination letter or FPCM compliance documentation in submittal |\n   131→| ZLU-15.37 | 50-foot setback from the centerline of an open waterway not maintained per Ordinance 030605-24 | Ordinance 030605-24 | Only when site is adjacent to or contains an open waterway. Check: waterway centerline location on grading or drainage plan versus proposed improvements. Note: this ordinance has not been verified in Bureau; flag for human confirmation of current status |\n   132→| ZLU-15.38 | Buildings not set back a minimum of 30 feet from the centerline of streets with less than 60-foot right-of-way for fire department access | Reviewer Convention | Only when site has frontage on a street with less than 60-foot ROW. Check: ROW width on survey or street cross-section; building setback from street centerline |\n   133→| ZLU-15.39 | Patios or balconies proposed within a required rear yard setback: required yards must be open and unobstructed from finished grade to sky per § 25-2-513(A); structures including balconies and covered patios cannot encroach into required rear yards; parking areas in required yards are permitted unless prohibited by Article 10 (§ 25-2-513(D)) | LDC § 25-2-513(A) | Only when patios or balconies are proposed and a rear yard setback applies. Check: site plan and floor plans for patio/balcony extent versus rear setback line |\n   134→| ZLU-15.40 | Transformer pad clearances not maintained per Austin Energy Design Criteria 1.10.4: (a) 10 feet of open area required on hot-stick-use (pad-locked) sides to any building or structure; (b) 5 feet to brick or masonry building or structure (may be reduced to 3 ft with AE Design written approval under specific conditions); (c) 12 feet to non-brick/masonry buildings or mixed structures (oil-filled equipment); (d) 3 feet from sides without operators/controls to removable fence; (e) 5 feet from sides with operators/controls to removable fence; (f) 15 feet in confined space or vault installations; (g) 2 feet from back of sidewalks; (h) equipment pads within 6 feet of parking/traffic areas require bollards when ≤ 4 feet. Violation pattern: site plan shows transformer adjacent to a wood-frame commercial building with only 5-ft clearance using the brick/masonry standard when the 12-ft non-masonry standard applies — verify building construction type before applying clearance row | Austin Energy Design Criteria 1.10.4 | Only when transformer pads are proposed on site. Check: transformer pad locations and surrounding clearance dimensions; confirm building construction type to select the correct clearance standard |\n   135→| ZLU-15.41 | Fencing 4 feet or greater in height adjacent to fire hydrants creates an obstruction that must be accounted for when measuring required hydrant distances; fencing under 4 feet may be disregarded, but fencing at or above 4 feet must be treated as an obstruction | Reviewer Convention | Only when fencing is proposed within the fire hydrant measurement area. Check: fence height and location relative to hydrants on site plan |\n   136→| ZLU-15.42 | Plans for projects subject to Article 10 that encroach on compatibility buffer, height limit, or setback requirements without a valid current-code relief mechanism: as of July 15, 2024, §§ 25-2-1081/1082 (compatibility waivers) are repealed; the only current relief paths are (1) BOA variance under §§ 25-2-472/474 — requires BOA order or certificate of variance demonstrating hardship findings in the submittal — or (2) site-specific amendment treated as a rezoning under § 25-2-1054 — requires Council ordinance granting the amendment. Plans submitted on or after July 15, 2024 that cite §25-2-1081, \"compatibility waiver,\" \"Planning Commission compatibility waiver,\" or \"notification/waiver fee\" are relying on repealed authority and the claimed relief has no legal basis | LDC § 25-2-472; LDC § 25-2-474; LDC § 25-2-1054 | Only when an Article 10 compliance deficiency (buffer encroachment, height limit exceedance) is identified and the applicant claims relief. Check: whether the approval document in the submittal is a current BOA variance order or a Council ordinance for a site-specific amendment; reject claims of compatibility waiver for post-July-2024 submittals |\n   137→| ZLU-15.43 | Site-specific amendment required but not initiated: Council-level relief from Article 10 height limits or compatibility buffer width requirements is available only through a site-specific amendment under § 25-2-1054, treated as a rezoning; such an amendment is not available for sites zoned a special purpose base district (PUD, P, TOD, NBG, ERC, etc.), sites carrying a DB combining district, or sites subject to the University Neighborhood Overlay (UNO); if a site-specific amendment has been approved, the Council ordinance granting it must be provided and must specify which Article 10 standard is modified | LDC § 25-2-1054 | Only when plans claim or need Council-level modification of Article 10 height or buffer requirements. Check: whether a Council ordinance granting a site-specific amendment is in the submittal documents |\n   138→| ZLU-15.44 | Zoning variance not obtained or applied for when the standard zoning district setback must be reduced below the code minimum (e.g., rear yard setback reduction for a use not involving compatibility standards); variance application must be submitted with required documentation and BOA approval must be documented | LDC § 25-2-472; LDC § 25-2-474 | Only when a reduction of a standard zoning setback (not compatibility) is required. Check: whether encroaching improvements require a variance and whether a BOA variance order is in the submittal |\n   139→| ZLU-15.45 | Letter of non-enforcement not obtained when proposed construction does not comply with a setback line recorded on the final plat; request must be directed to the appropriate City staff per applicable City procedures | Reviewer Convention | Only when a setback line appears on the site's recorded final plat. Check: recorded plat for any platted setback lines differing from code setbacks |\n   140→| ZLU-15.46 | Solid fence along a property line exceeds applicable height limits without meeting a qualifying exception: base maximum is 6 feet measured from natural grade (§ 25-2-899(D)); up to 7 feet is permitted where a grade change of 1 foot or more occurs along any run of fence (§ 25-2-899(E)); up to 8 feet is permitted under: (F) written consent of all adjoining owners PLUS grade change ≥ 2 ft within 50 ft of boundary OR structure enabling a child to climb over fence near hazard; (G) fence within building setback lines; (H) fence between a residential use and commercial/industrial property or an alley separating them. The \"6-foot average height\" language does not appear in the current code; the standard is a 6-foot maximum, not an average | LDC § 25-2-899(D); LDC § 25-2-899(E) | Only when solid fences are proposed at or near property lines. Check: fence height callouts on site plan or landscape plan against applicable exception in § 25-2-899 |\n   141→| ZLU-15.47 | New fence constructed after July 31, 2023 violates design restrictions under § 25-2-899(I): spiked pickets above the top horizontal backer rail are prohibited; vertical pickets above the top rail with 2-to-9-inch spacing are prohibited; razor wire is prohibited; barbed wire is prohibited except at airports; fences creating a substantial risk of entrapment or impalement are prohibited; solid chain link must use knuckle selvage; these restrictions do not apply to fences constructed before July 31, 2023, or fences greater than 6 feet in height on non-residential property | LDC § 25-2-899(I) | Only when new fences are proposed to be constructed after July 31, 2023, and the construction type could implicate the design restrictions. Check: fence detail specifications on site plan or landscape plan; confirm construction date and fence height/property type to determine whether § 25-2-899(I) applies |\n   142→| ZLU-15.48 | Site-specific ordinance or NCCD fence height requirements not shown or not met on plans: e.g., ordinance-required minimum fence heights along designated property lines; playground and pocket park perimeter fencing limited to 4-foot maximum see-through type with at least 2 pedestrian entry points; mobile home park boundary fencing minimum 6 feet per LDC § 25-2-1205 | Per applicable NCCD/CO/ordinance conditions; LDC § 25-2-1205 (mobile home park boundary fence) | Only when site is within an NCCD boundary, PUD, or has a named ordinance imposing fence height requirements, or when playgrounds, pocket parks, or mobile home parks are proposed. Check: applicable ordinance conditions and fence height callouts on plans |\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d203a61d-b956-4bf7-a62f-71a0e7d71a2a","uuid":"32c9010b-d558-4ee1-b805-7365f084eaa4","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/zlu-md-exp/15.md","content":"# Setback Requirements and Compatibility Provisions\n\n## Description\n\nThis grouping covers all setback and buffer requirements that a site plan must demonstrate compliance with: standard zoning district setbacks (front, rear, side, street side), Austin's compatibility standards for development near SF-5-or-more-restrictive-zoned properties (Article 10 height limits, buffers, and screening), waterfront overlay subdistrict setbacks, Lake Austin district shoreline setbacks, special ordinance/CO/PUD/NCCD setbacks, and miscellaneous clearances (hazardous pipelines, open waterways, fire access, transformer pads). It also covers the procedural steps required when setback relief is needed (BOA variances, site-specific amendments, letters of non-enforcement) and fence height and design limits at property lines. A reviewer examining this grouping checks both that required setback lines are correctly drawn and labeled on plans and that no improvements violate those lines.\n\n## Regulatory Overview\n\n**Standard Zoning Setbacks.** LDC § 25-2-492 establishes minimum front, street side, interior side, and rear yard setbacks for every base zoning district. For industrial districts (IP, MI, LI), yard setbacks adjacent to residential, office, and commercial uses are governed by the adjacency-based table in § 25-2-601 (50 ft adjacent to LA, RR, or SF-1 through SF-6; 25 ft adjacent to MF-1 through MF-6 or MH; 15 ft adjacent to NO, LO, GO, CR, LR, or GR; 10 ft adjacent to L or less restrictive). Where a Subchapter provision conflicts with § 25-2-492, the more restrictive regulation governs (§ 25-2-492(B)). All required yards must remain open and unobstructed from finished grade to sky (§ 25-2-513(A)); through lots must apply the front yard minimum to both street frontages (§ 25-2-515). Special overlay designations—conditional overlays (CO), planned unit developments (PUD), and neighborhood conservation and combining districts (NCCD)—can impose site-specific setbacks that override or supplement the base district table; these requirements appear only in the individual ordinance or development agreement, not in the LDC tables.\n\n**Compatibility Standards (Article 10 — Effective July 15, 2024).** Article 10 was completely repealed and replaced by Ord. No. 20240516-004 effective July 15, 2024. The former Article 10 used four distance bands at 50, 100, 300, and 540 feet from a triggering property and included a compatibility waiver mechanism (§§ 25-2-1081/1082). **None of that framework survives; any plan citing those distance bands, those section numbers, or \"compatibility waiver\" for work submitted on or after July 15, 2024 is relying on repealed authority.** The current Article 10 (§§ 25-2-1051–25-2-1063) applies when a site is (1) zoned MF-4 or any less restrictive district (MF-5, MF-6, or any commercial or industrial zone) AND (2) located within 75 feet of a **triggering property**. A triggering property (§ 25-2-1053) is a site that has at least one but fewer than four dwelling units AND is zoned **SF-5 or more restrictive**. The qualifying zones for \"SF-5 or more restrictive\" are: **LA, RR, SF-1, SF-2, SF-3, SF-4A, SF-4B, and SF-5**. SF-6 is less restrictive than SF-5 and does NOT create a triggering property. Historic designation (H) is a combining district that does not appear in the § 25-2-1052 exceptions list; H-designated sites are not exempt from Article 10. Article 10 does not apply to: sites zoned CBD or DMU; structural alterations that do not increase square footage, area, or height; and sites used exclusively for duplex, single-family attached, single-family residential, small-lot SF, two-unit, three-unit, adult care services, or child care services (§ 25-2-1052). There is no small-site/large-site distinction under the current framework. Current relief paths: BOA variance with hardship findings (§§ 25-2-472/474) or site-specific amendment treated as a rezoning (§ 25-2-1054; not available for special purpose base districts, DB combining districts, or UNO sites). The current compatibility standards impose: (a) **height limits** (§ 25-2-1061): structures < 50 ft from triggering property boundary ≤ 40 ft; 50–74 ft ≤ 60 ft; ≥ 75 ft = base zoning; (b) **compatibility buffer** (§ 25-2-1062(B)): 25-ft landscaped buffer along the shared property line, reduced to 15 ft if the site is < 75 ft wide measured from the shared triggering property line, complying with § 25-8-700 planting requirements; buffer is not required for condominium or townhouse residential use (§ 25-2-1062(A)), sites with only residential uses with ≤ 16 dwelling units, or NO/LO/LR-zoned sites with max zoning height ≤ 40 ft (§ 25-2-1062(B)(2)); (c) **setbacks** (§ 25-2-1062(C)): for NO, LO, LR districts, 10-ft rear yard setback from the shared triggering property line; LR only: additional 5-ft interior side setback; (d) **screening, noise, and design** (§ 25-2-1063): lighting hooded/shielded so light source not visible from shared property line; mechanical equipment noise ≤ 70 dB at shared property line; refuse receptacle concrete slab ≥ 15 ft from triggering property; on-site amenities available to residents/occupants only ≥ 25 ft from triggering property (multi-use trails excepted); vehicle lights from parking facilities, ground floor and rooftop mechanical equipment, outdoor storage, refuse receptacles and collection areas, and common amenity areas (decks, patios, pools) must be screened from the shared property line or alleyway except through pedestrian/bicycle access points. Per § 25-8-700, the first 10 ft of the buffer from the shared property line is the **screening zone** (vertical structures, parking, driveways, and mechanical equipment prohibited; paths, landscaping, utility infrastructure, and ground-floor personal open space permitted); the next 15 ft is the **restricted zone** (surface parking, driveways, fire lanes, utility infrastructure, and refuse receptacles permitted; vertical structures generally prohibited except per § 25-8-700(C)(4)).\n\n**Waterfront Overlay and Shoreline Setbacks.** The Waterfront Overlay (WO) combining district (\"WO\" suffix in zoning designation) applies to properties along Town Lake/Lady Bird Lake and the Colorado River, as defined in LDC Appendix B. The WO district consists of 16 geographically defined subdistricts. PARD director review is required before site plan approval for all WO sites (§ 25-2-721(A)(2)). Primary setback distances vary by subdistrict and must be shown and maintained on plans:\n\n| Subdistrict | From Town Lake shoreline | Creek setbacks |\n|---|---|---|\n| North Shore Central (§ 25-2-738) | 100 ft | 60 ft from Shoal Creek centerline; 50 ft from Waller Creek centerline |\n| Rainey Street (§ 25-2-739) | 150 ft | 50 ft from Waller Creek centerline |\n| South Shore Central (§ 25-2-742) | 150 ft | 80 ft from East Bouldin Creek centerline; 35 ft north of Riverside Drive ROW |\n| South Lakeshore (§ 25-2-741) | 65 ft | 50 ft south of Lakeshore Blvd |\n| East Riverside (§ 25-2-734) | 100 ft | — |\n\nThere is no single \"Lady Bird Lake = 150 ft\" rule; the applicable distance depends on the specific subdistrict. Several WO subdistrict boundaries (South Lakeshore, Travis Heights, Auditorium Shores, Butler Shores) reference lot lines as they existed in 1986; boundary determination for recently reconfigured parcels in those subdistricts may require historical plat research. Separately, the Lake Austin (LA) zoning district sets its own shoreline setback under § 25-2-551(B): 75 ft from the 492.8-ft contour line (the shoreline), reduced to 25 ft for lots in pre-April 22, 1982 recorded subdivisions where the shoreline to the front lot line is ≤ 200 ft. The CWQZ (§ 25-8-92) is a separate environmental requirement establishing a 100-ft Critical Water Quality Zone from the Lady Bird Lake 429.0-ft contour and from the Lake Austin 492.8-ft contour (75 ft for detached single-family residential); these are distinct environmental setbacks from the LA district or WO setbacks. In WO primary setback areas, parking is generally prohibited (§ 25-2-721(B)(1)). In the secondary setback area, impervious cover may not exceed 30% (§ 25-2-721(B)(2)).\n\n**Other Setbacks and Common Violation Patterns.** A **hazardous pipeline** (§ 25-2-516(A)) must meet two criteria: ≥ 8-inch inside diameter AND designed to carry a hazardous liquid per 49 CFR § 195.2 (petroleum, petroleum products, anhydrous ammonia, ethanol). Natural gas pipelines are not hazardous pipelines under this definition. Uses requiring evacuation assistance (congregate living, hospitals, medical offices > 5,000 sq ft GFA, schools, large-site retirement housing, commercial day care, etc.) are prohibited within 500 ft of a hazardous pipeline; placement within 200–500 ft requires City Council resolution after Fire Chief recommendation (§ 25-2-516(B)). New construction within 200 ft requires Fire Chief determination that the project meets performance-based design standards or FPCM construction requirements (§ 25-2-516(C)). *Common violation pattern:* designers omit City Council resolution or Fire Chief approval letter when proposing evacuation-assistance uses within 200–500 ft; check the AFD Fire Analysis Table \"City of Austin Hazardous Pipeline Ordinance\" field (Yes/No) on the cover sheet. *Common violation patterns in compatibility reviews:* (1) post-July 2024 plans still cite § 25-2-1081/1082, \"compatibility waiver,\" or Planning Commission compatibility waiver approvals — these are all repealed; (2) triggering property incorrectly evaluated using the pre-2024 \"shared lot line\" standard rather than the current \"within 75 feet\" test; (3) SF-6 incorrectly treated as a triggering property (SF-6 is less restrictive than SF-5 and does not qualify); (4) Historic (H) designation incorrectly claimed as an Article 10 exception (H is not listed in § 25-2-1052). *Transformer pad:* using the 5-ft brick/masonry clearance standard for non-masonry buildings when the 12-ft non-masonry standard applies is a common plan deficiency.\n\n**Key Thresholds:**\n- Article 10 applicability: site zoned MF-4 or less restrictive + within 75 ft of triggering property (LDC § 25-2-1051)\n- Triggering property: 1–3 dwelling units + zoned SF-5 or more restrictive — qualifying zones: LA, RR, SF-1, SF-2, SF-3, SF-4A, SF-4B, SF-5 only (LDC § 25-2-1053)\n- Compatibility height limits: < 50 ft from triggering property = 40 ft max; 50–74 ft = 60 ft max; ≥ 75 ft = base zoning height (LDC § 25-2-1061)\n- Compatibility buffer widths: 25 ft standard; 15 ft where site is < 75 ft wide measured from shared triggering property line (LDC § 25-2-1062(B))\n- Buffer screening zone (10 ft): vertical structures, parking, driveways, mechanical equipment prohibited (LDC § 25-8-700)\n- Refuse receptacle concrete slab: ≥ 15 ft from triggering property (LDC § 25-2-1063(C))\n- On-site amenity setback: ≥ 25 ft from triggering property (LDC § 25-2-1063(D))\n- Noise limit: mechanical equipment ≤ 70 dB at shared property line (LDC § 25-2-1063(B))\n- Fence heights: 6 ft max at standard; 7 ft where grade change ≥ 1 ft along fence run; 8 ft under § 25-2-899(F)(G)(H) conditions (LDC § 25-2-899)\n- Hazardous pipeline qualifying criteria: ≥ 8-inch inside diameter + hazardous liquid per 49 CFR § 195.2 (LDC § 25-2-516(A))\n- Parking facility fence: ≥ 4 ft where nonresidential parking facility adjoins residential district (LDC § 25-6-563(A)); ≥ 6 ft where residential parking facility with > 10 spaces adjoins residential property (LDC § 25-6-563(B))\n- LA district shoreline setback: 75 ft from 492.8-ft contour; 25 ft for pre-1982 lots where shoreline to front lot line ≤ 200 ft (LDC § 25-2-551(B))\n\n**Exceptions:**\n- Article 10 does not apply: CBD or DMU zoning; structural alterations without size/height increase; duplex, SF attached, SF residential, small-lot SF, two-unit, three-unit, adult care, or child care uses (LDC § 25-2-1052)\n- Compatibility buffer not required even when Article 10 applies: condominium or townhouse residential use; sites with only residential uses ≤ 16 DUs; sites zoned NO/LO/LR with max zoning height ≤ 40 ft (LDC § 25-2-1062(A)(B)(2))\n- Compatibility waiver (§§ 25-2-1081/1082): REPEALED July 15, 2024 — not available for any submittal on or after that date\n\n## Code References\n\n- LDC § 25-2-472 — Board of Adjustment variance authority over Chapter 25-2 requirements, including Article 10\n- LDC § 25-2-474 — Required findings for BOA variances (hardship, uniqueness, no harm to adjacent properties)\n- LDC § 25-2-492 — Zoning district setback table (front, rear, interior side, street side by district)\n- LDC § 25-2-513 — Required yards must be open and unobstructed from finished grade to sky; limited exceptions for eaves, steps, stoops, disability ramps, landscaping\n- LDC § 25-2-515 — Through lots: front yard setback applied to rear yard\n- LDC § 25-2-516 — Development near a hazardous pipeline: definitions, 200-ft and 500-ft zones, evacuation-assistance use restrictions, FPCM construction requirements\n- LDC § 25-2-551 — Lake Austin (LA) district regulations, including 75-ft (or 25-ft for pre-1982 lots) shoreline setback from 492.8-ft contour\n- LDC § 25-2-601 — Industrial district (IP, MI, LI) setbacks adjacent to residential, office, and commercial uses\n- LDC § 25-2-712 — Waterfront Overlay definitions (primary setback area, secondary setback area)\n- LDC § 25-2-721 — Waterfront Overlay combining district regulations: PARD review, primary and secondary setback area use restrictions, parking prohibition, impervious cover limits\n- LDC § 25-2-734 — East Riverside WO subdistrict regulations (100-ft primary setback from Town Lake shoreline)\n- LDC § 25-2-738 — North Shore Central WO subdistrict regulations (100-ft from Town Lake; 60-ft from Shoal Creek; 50-ft from Waller Creek)\n- LDC § 25-2-739 — Rainey Street WO subdistrict regulations (150-ft from Town Lake; 50-ft from Waller Creek)\n- LDC § 25-2-741 — South Lakeshore WO subdistrict regulations (65-ft from Town Lake; 50-ft south of Lakeshore Blvd)\n- LDC § 25-2-742 — South Shore Central WO subdistrict regulations (150-ft from Town Lake; 80-ft from East Bouldin Creek; 35-ft north of Riverside Drive ROW)\n- LDC § 25-2-899 — Fences as accessory uses: height limits (6 ft standard, 7 ft grade-change, 8 ft under specific conditions); July 2023 design restrictions\n- LDC § 25-2-1051 — Compatibility Standards (Article 10) applicability: site zoned MF-4 or less restrictive + within 75 ft of triggering property (eff. July 15, 2024)\n- LDC § 25-2-1052 — Exceptions to compatibility standards: CBD/DMU, structural alterations, certain residential uses (eff. July 15, 2024)\n- LDC § 25-2-1053 — Triggering property definition: 1–3 dwelling units + SF-5 or more restrictive (eff. July 15, 2024)\n- LDC § 25-2-1054 — Site-specific amendments to Article 10 height and buffer requirements: treated as a rezoning; not available for special purpose base districts, DB combining districts, or UNO sites\n- LDC § 25-2-1061 — Compatibility height limits: < 50 ft = 40 ft max; 50–74 ft = 60 ft max; ≥ 75 ft = base zoning (eff. July 15, 2024)\n- LDC § 25-2-1062 — Compatibility buffers and setbacks: 25-ft buffer along shared triggering property line (15 ft for narrow sites); planting per § 25-8-700; setbacks for NO/LO/LR districts; exceptions (eff. July 15, 2024)\n- LDC § 25-2-1063 — Screening, noise, and design requirements: lighting, 70-dB noise limit, refuse receptacle placement, amenity setback, comprehensive screening list (eff. July 15, 2024)\n- LDC § 25-2-1205 — Site development regulations for mobile home parks, including minimum 6-ft boundary fence requirement\n- LDC § 25-5-146 — Conditions of approval for conditional use site plans: 200-ft parking separation for late-hours cocktail lounge/restaurant\n- LDC § 25-6-563 — Screening requirements for off-street parking facilities adjoining residential districts\n- LDC § 25-8-92 — Critical Water Quality Zones established (Lady Bird Lake, Lake Austin, other watersheds)\n- LDC § 25-8-700 — Minimum requirements for compatibility buffers: screening zone and restricted zone composition, planting requirements, vegetation standards\n- Austin Energy Design Criteria 1.10.4 — Clearances from padmount transformer equipment\n- Ordinance 030605-24 — 50-foot setback from centerline of open waterways (not verified in Bureau; preserve as active requirement pending confirmation)\n\n## Documents to Review\n\n- **Site plan sheets** — Verify all required setback lines (zoning, compatibility, waterfront, LA shoreline, special ordinance) are drawn, labeled, dimensioned, and that no buildings, structures, parking areas, or driveways violate them\n- **Landscape plan sheets** — Verify compatibility buffer lines are shown with screening/planting notations; confirm dimensions match site plan; confirm § 25-8-700 plantings shown at 25 LF intervals within the screening zone\n- **Compatibility exhibit (separate sheet)** — Verify property boundaries, on-site and off-site zoning boundaries (all triggering property zones and applicable overlays), distances from triggering property line to existing and proposed buildings, and surface improvements are shown\n- **Compatibility cross-section sheets** — Verify height vs. distance relationships from triggering property boundary with distance increments (50 ft, 75 ft) and building elevations labeled to confirm §25-2-1061 compliance\n- **Elevation sheets** — Verify building heights, setback dimensions from property lines, and parapets labeled\n- **Recorded plat / survey** — Check for any platted setback lines that differ from standard code requirements\n- **Project data / facts sheet (zoning field)** — Identify zoning district, COs, PUDs, NCCDs, waterfront overlay subdistrict, and proximity to triggering properties to determine which setback sets apply\n- **Pipeline easement documentation / utility plan / AFD Fire Analysis Table** — Verify proximity to hazardous pipelines (check \"City of Austin Hazardous Pipeline Ordinance: Yes/No\" field) and open waterways\n- **Cover sheet approval block** — Confirm plan submission date to determine whether pre- or post-July 15, 2024 Article 10 applies\n\n## Checklist Items\n\n| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n| ZLU-15.1 | Required zoning district setback lines (front yard, rear yard, interior side yard, street side yard) not shown, labeled, or dimensioned on the site plan | LDC § 25-2-492 | All sites |\n| ZLU-15.2 | Zoning district setback distances not maintained: buildings, structures, or improvements encroach into required front, rear, interior side, or street side setbacks per the applicable district table; industrial districts use the adjacency-based table for interior and rear yards adjacent to residential, office, and commercial uses | LDC § 25-2-492; LDC § 25-2-601 | All sites |\n| ZLU-15.3 | Shoreline or CWQZ setback not shown and labeled on site plan: (a) Lake Austin (LA) district shoreline setback — 75 ft from the 492.8-ft topographic contour line (the shoreline); or 25 ft for lots in pre-April 22, 1982 recorded subdivisions where shoreline to front lot line is ≤ 200 ft — per LDC § 25-2-551(B); and (b) Critical Water Quality Zone (CWQZ) — 100 ft from Lady Bird Lake 429.0-ft contour or Lake Austin 492.8-ft contour (75 ft for detached single-family residential) — per LDC § 25-8-92. These are two distinct setback frameworks; both may apply simultaneously on the same site | LDC § 25-2-551(B); LDC § 25-8-92 | Only when site has shoreline frontage on Lady Bird Lake or Lake Austin, or is zoned LA. Check: zoning field and site location in facts.md; confirm which of the two setback frameworks applies based on zoning and shoreline presence |\n| ZLU-15.4 | Through lot not applying the front yard setback requirement to the rear yard per LDC § 25-2-515 | LDC § 25-2-515 | Only when site is a through lot (fronts on two parallel streets). Check: survey and street layout on site plan |\n| ZLU-15.5 | Dumpster enclosures not maintaining required setbacks from property lines per the applicable zoning district setback table | LDC § 25-2-492 | Only when dumpster enclosures are proposed. Check: site plan for dumpster locations relative to property lines |\n| ZLU-15.6 | Fences proposed within required building setback lines in violation of applicable zoning restrictions | LDC § 25-2-899 | Only when fences are proposed near building setback boundaries. Check: fence locations versus labeled setback lines on site plan |\n| ZLU-15.7 | Compatibility standards (Article 10) applicability incorrectly determined: Article 10 applies when a site is zoned MF-4 or less restrictive AND is located within 75 feet of a triggering property; a triggering property is a site with 1–3 dwelling units zoned SF-5 or more restrictive (LA, RR, SF-1, SF-2, SF-3, SF-4A, SF-4B, or SF-5); SF-6 is NOT more restrictive than SF-5 and does not create a triggering property; Historic (H) designation is not an exception under § 25-2-1052; sites zoned CBD or DMU are exempt; the proximity test is within 75 feet, not a shared lot line | LDC § 25-2-1051; LDC § 25-2-1052; LDC § 25-2-1053 | Only when site is zoned MF-4 or less restrictive. Check: Zoning field in facts.md or plan cover sheet for MF-4, MF-5, MF-6, or commercial/industrial base district designation; then check adjacent property zoning to identify any SF-5-or-more-restrictive parcels with 1–3 dwelling units within 75 ft of the site boundary |\n| ZLU-15.8 | Compatibility height limits not shown or not maintained under Article 10: structures located less than 50 feet from any part of a triggering property may not exceed 40 feet in height; structures located 50 to 74 feet from a triggering property may not exceed 60 feet in height; structures at 75 feet or more from a triggering property are subject only to base zoning height; distance is measured from the triggering property boundary | LDC § 25-2-1061 | Only when compatibility is triggered. Check: site plan and elevation sheets for distance measurements from triggering property boundary to proposed structures and labeled building heights |\n| ZLU-15.9 | Compatibility exhibit does not show the approximate location of existing structures on the triggering property; this information is necessary to verify the distance measurement from the triggering property boundary and to document compliance with the height-limit distance bands under § 25-2-1061 when proposed structures are positioned near the 50-ft and 75-ft thresholds | Reviewer Convention | Only when compatibility is triggered and structures are proposed within 75 ft of the triggering property boundary. Check: compatibility exhibit or site plan for triggering property structure notation |\n| ZLU-15.10 | Compatibility buffer not shown at the correct width along the shared triggering property line: the required buffer is 25 feet wide (standard) or 15 feet wide if the development site is less than 75 feet wide measured from the shared triggering property line; the buffer must be shown as a landscaped zone complying with § 25-8-700 planting requirements and must extend along the full length of the property line shared with a triggering property; the old multi-distance band framework (50/100/300/540 ft) from the pre-July 2024 Article 10 does not apply | LDC § 25-2-1062(B); LDC § 25-8-700 | Only when compatibility is triggered and the compatibility buffer is required (not exempt under § 25-2-1062(A)(B)(2)). Check: site plan and landscape sheets for buffer width notation along shared triggering property line |\n| ZLU-15.11 | Compatibility buffer line mislabeled (e.g., labeled \"No Build Zone\" instead of \"Compatibility Buffer\"), undimensioned, or not applied along all property lines where a triggering property shares the line on multiple sides | LDC § 25-2-1062(B) | Only when compatibility is triggered. Check: labels and dimensions on each compatibility buffer line shown on site plan and landscape sheets |\n| ZLU-15.12 | Compatibility buffer lines incorrectly applied adjacent to areas where no triggering property exists — e.g., adjacent to transmission line easements, access easements, open space areas, or commercial properties that are not SF-5-or-more-restrictive-zoned with 1–3 dwelling units; the buffer is required only along property lines shared with qualifying triggering properties | LDC § 25-2-1051; LDC § 25-2-1053 | Only when compatibility is triggered. Check: that buffer lines terminate where the adjacent property does not qualify as a triggering property |\n| ZLU-15.13 | Triggering property or properties not clearly identified on the site plan compatibility sheets; adjacent properties that trigger compatibility requirements must be labeled to confirm they qualify as triggering properties under § 25-2-1053 | LDC § 25-2-1053; LDC § 25-2-1063 | Only when compatibility is triggered. Check: compatibility exhibit or site plan for triggering property notation including zoning label and dwelling unit count |\n| ZLU-15.14 | Property line not graphically prominent and clearly distinguishable as the measurement datum for compatibility buffer and height limit compliance; required screening vegetation along the compatibility buffer screening zone not shown on plans or not included in the plan legend per § 25-8-700 | LDC § 25-2-1062; LDC § 25-8-700 | Only when compatibility is triggered and buffer is required. Check: plan legend and property line depiction on compatibility and landscape sheets |\n| ZLU-15.15 | Proposed improvements within the 10-foot screening zone of the compatibility buffer that are prohibited by § 25-8-700: vertical structures (buildings, covered structures, walls not functioning as screening berms), parking areas, driveways, or mechanical equipment within the first 10 feet from the shared triggering property line; the screening zone must consist of landscaping, fences/berms, paths/trails, utility infrastructure, and ground-floor personal open space only; relief from this requirement requires a BOA variance (§ 25-2-472) or site-specific amendment (§ 25-2-1054) | LDC § 25-2-1062(B); LDC § 25-8-700 | Only when compatibility is triggered and buffer is required. Check: site plan and grading plan for impervious or structural elements within 10 ft of the triggering property line |\n| ZLU-15.16 | Covered structures available to residents or occupants (covered bike parking areas, covered patio areas, covered recreational facilities) proposed within 25 feet of the triggering property line without meeting an exception or obtaining valid current relief; on-site amenities available only to residents/occupants and guests may not be located within 25 feet of triggering property per § 25-2-1063(D); additionally, covered structures constitute vertical structures prohibited within the 10-foot screening zone per § 25-8-700; relief requires a BOA variance (§ 25-2-472) or site-specific amendment (§ 25-2-1054) | LDC § 25-2-1063(D); LDC § 25-8-700 | Only when compatibility is triggered and covered structures are proposed within 25 feet of triggering property line. Check: site plan for covered amenity structures within the buffer zone |\n| ZLU-15.17 | Above-grade drainage features (level spreaders with concrete footings, detention structures with above-grade walls, concrete drainage channels) proposed within the 10-foot screening zone of the compatibility buffer, in conflict with § 25-8-700's prohibition on vertical structures in the screening zone; ground-level drainage features not constituting vertical structures may be acceptable if they do not interfere with required buffer planting; relief for above-grade structures requires a BOA variance (§ 25-2-472) or site-specific amendment (§ 25-2-1054) | LDC § 25-2-1062(B); LDC § 25-8-700 | Only when compatibility is triggered and drainage features are proposed near the triggering property line. Check: grading and drainage plan against the 10-ft screening zone boundary |\n| ZLU-15.18 | On-site amenities (bicycle racks, swimming pools, tennis courts, ball courts, playgrounds, outdoor gyms) available only to residents, occupants, and their guests placed within 25 feet of the triggering property line; multi-use trails open to the public are excepted | LDC § 25-2-1063(D) | Only when compatibility is triggered and amenities are proposed. Check: amenity locations on site plan against the 25-foot distance from the triggering property boundary |\n| ZLU-15.19 | Parking area or drive aisle within the 25-foot compatibility buffer not redesigned or relocated: the 25-foot buffer must be maintained as a landscaped zone complying with § 25-8-700; parking is prohibited within the 10-foot screening zone; additionally, vehicle lights from parking lots and structures must be screened so they are not visible from the shared triggering property line or alleyway | LDC § 25-2-1062(B); LDC § 25-2-1063(E)(1); LDC § 25-8-700 | Only when compatibility is triggered and parking or drive aisles are proposed near the triggering property line. Check: parking layout and landscaping plan against the 25-foot buffer and screening requirements |\n| ZLU-15.20 | Exterior lighting not hooded or shielded to prevent the light source from being visible from the shared property line or alleyway with the triggering property | LDC § 25-2-1063(A) | Only when compatibility is triggered. Check: lighting plan notes and fixture specifications for hooding/shielding requirement at the shared property line |\n| ZLU-15.21 | Mechanical equipment (HVAC units, cooling towers, generators, exhaust fans) proposed to produce sound exceeding 70 decibels measured at the shared property line or alleyway with the triggering property | LDC § 25-2-1063(B) | Only when compatibility is triggered and mechanical equipment is proposed. Check: mechanical equipment noise calculations or acoustical notes on plans relative to shared property line |\n| ZLU-15.22 | Required elements not screened from the shared triggering property line or alleyway: vehicle lights from parking lots or structures; ground floor and rooftop mechanical equipment; outdoor storage; refuse receptacles and refuse collection areas; or common amenity areas (decks, patios, pools) are visible from the shared property line; screening must prevent visibility except through designated pedestrian or bicycle access points; rooftop mechanical equipment may be screened by a parapet | LDC § 25-2-1063(E); LDC § 25-2-1063(G) | Only when compatibility is triggered. Check: site plan, landscape plan, and elevation sheets for screening provisions at the shared property line |\n| ZLU-15.23 | Parking area for a late-hours cocktail lounge or restaurant not separated from property used or zoned SF-6 or more restrictive by a minimum of 200 feet as a condition of conditional use site plan approval; exceptions require (1) the lounge/restaurant is in an enclosed shopping center, or (2) the Land Use Commission granted a variance at site plan approval | LDC § 25-5-146(B) | Only when site has a cocktail lounge or restaurant use with a late-hours permit as part of a conditional use site plan. Check: use designation, conditional use permit, and parking lot boundary distance from nearest SF-6 or more restrictive property |\n| ZLU-15.24 | Required minimum 4-foot fence or wall not provided along the common boundary between an off-street parking facility in a nonresidential zoning district and an adjoining residential zoning district; or fence placed off the property line without documented justification that property-line placement would harm existing vegetation, interfere with drainage, or create a utility conflict per § 25-6-563(C)/(D) | LDC § 25-6-563(A) | Only when an off-street parking facility in a nonresidential district abuts a residential district boundary. Check: parking lot boundary treatment on landscape or site plan |\n| ZLU-15.25 | Required minimum 6-foot screening (wall, fence, or landscape) not provided along the common boundary where a parking facility containing more than 10 spaces in a residential zoning district adjoins another residential district property | LDC § 25-6-563(B) | Only when a parking facility with more than 10 spaces in a residential district adjoins another residential property. Check: parking facility boundary treatment and space count on site plan |\n| ZLU-15.26 | Separate compatibility exhibit not provided or incomplete: the exhibit must show property boundaries, on-site and off-site zoning boundaries (including all triggering properties and applicable combining districts), distances from triggering property boundary to existing and proposed structures, distances to surface improvements (parking, driveways, drainage features), and the location of existing structures on triggering properties; this documentation is required to demonstrate compliance with §§ 25-2-1061 (height limits), 25-2-1062 (buffer), and 25-2-1063 (screening) | Reviewer Convention | Only when compatibility is triggered. Check: whether a standalone compatibility exhibit sheet exists in the plan set and contains all required information |\n| ZLU-15.27 | Compatibility cross-sections not provided or incomplete: cross-sections must show the triggering property boundary, distance measurements from the triggering property boundary to proposed structures at the 50-ft and 75-ft thresholds, proposed building heights at each distance band, and elevation of the triggering property grade; these are required to demonstrate compliance with the § 25-2-1061 height limit bands (< 50 ft = 40-ft max; 50–74 ft = 60-ft max) | LDC § 25-2-1061 | Only when compatibility is triggered and structures are proposed within 75 ft of the triggering property boundary. Check: cross-section sheet(s) for labeled distances and building heights at both threshold distances |\n| ZLU-15.28 | Waterfront overlay subdistrict setback not shown, labeled, or maintained on site plan: applicable primary setback distances vary by subdistrict as follows — North Shore Central: 100 ft from Town Lake shoreline, 60 ft from Shoal Creek centerline, 50 ft from Waller Creek centerline; Rainey Street: 150 ft from Town Lake shoreline, 50 ft from Waller Creek centerline; South Shore Central: 150 ft from Town Lake shoreline, 80 ft from East Bouldin Creek centerline, 35 ft north of Riverside Drive ROW; South Lakeshore: 65 ft from Town Lake shoreline, 50 ft south of Lakeshore Boulevard; East Riverside: 100 ft from Town Lake shoreline; setback distances for other WO subdistricts must be verified against the applicable subsection of LDC §§ 25-2-731 through 25-2-746; there is no single \"Lady Bird Lake = 150 ft\" rule; the applicable distance depends entirely on which subdistrict the site is located in | LDC § 25-2-738; LDC § 25-2-739; LDC § 25-2-741; LDC § 25-2-742; LDC § 25-2-734 | Only when site is within a waterfront overlay (WO) combining district subdistrict. Check: overlay subdistrict designation in facts.md zoning field (WO suffix) and applicable subdistrict identification on cover sheet or GIS |\n| ZLU-15.29 | Gross square footage contained within the waterfront overlay primary setback area and secondary setback area not computed and shown; percentage of total site area within the primary setback not shown | LDC § 25-2-721 | Only when site is within a waterfront overlay subdistrict. Check: plan sheets for setback area tabulation |\n| ZLU-15.30 | Impervious coverage within the waterfront overlay secondary setback area exceeds 30% per § 25-2-721(B)(2); no parking structures, parking areas, or surface parking permitted within the primary setback area except for public transportation, handicapped, or park access uses | LDC § 25-2-721(B) | Only when site is within a waterfront overlay subdistrict. Check: impervious cover calculations within the primary and secondary setback zones |\n| ZLU-15.31 | Conditional overlay (CO) setback requirements not shown on site plan or setback distances not maintained per the applicable conditional ordinance | Per CO ordinance conditions | Only when site has a CO designation with specific setback conditions. Check: CO ordinance conditions listed in facts.md or on plan cover sheet |\n| ZLU-15.32 | PUD-required setback lines not shown on site plan per the applicable planned unit development approval document | Per PUD ordinance conditions | Only when site is within a PUD. Check: PUD ordinance for required setbacks; verify setbacks shown on plans |\n| ZLU-15.33 | NCCD setback requirements not shown or not met on site plan per the applicable neighborhood conservation and combining district ordinance | Per NCCD ordinance conditions | Only when site is within an NCCD boundary. Check: NCCD ordinance conditions for setback requirements; verify shown on plans; note that NCCD ordinance text must be obtained from the applicant or City staff as it is not available in Bureau |\n| ZLU-15.34 | Named ordinance setback requirements (site-specific setbacks from particular property lines established by recorded ordinance, e.g., 50-ft from southern property line, 35-ft landscape buffer from highway ROW) not shown on plans or not maintained | Per recorded ordinance conditions | Only when site has a recorded ordinance imposing specific setbacks beyond standard code minimums. Check: ordinance conditions in facts.md and plan cover sheet data |\n| ZLU-15.35 | Uses requiring evacuation assistance proposed within 500 feet of a hazardous pipeline without required approval: uses within 200 feet are prohibited as new construction; uses between 200 and 500 feet require City Council resolution following a Fire Chief recommendation based on performance-based design, early detection, and engineer certification per § 25-2-516(B); qualifying uses include: congregate living, convalescent services, detention facilities, commercial day care, hospitals (general/limited), medical offices exceeding 5,000 sq ft GFA, private/public primary and secondary educational facilities, and large-site retirement housing. Violation pattern: plan proposes qualifying use within 200–500 ft range with no Council resolution or Fire Chief approval letter in the submittal; check cover sheet AFD Fire Analysis Table \"City of Austin Hazardous Pipeline Ordinance\" field (Yes/No) | LDC § 25-2-516(B) | Only when site is within 500 feet of a hazardous pipeline (≥ 8-inch inside diameter carrying hazardous liquid per 49 CFR § 195.2). Check: AFD Fire Analysis Table on cover sheet, pipeline easement location on utility plan or survey, and proposed use type |\n| ZLU-15.36 | New construction proposed within 200 feet of a hazardous pipeline without demonstrating compliance with Fire Protection Criteria Manual (FPCM) construction requirements or obtaining a Fire Chief determination that the project meets performance-based design standards; the FPCM establishes distance-based tiers (< 50 ft, 50–100 ft, 100–150 ft, 150–200 ft) with construction type and opening requirements that vary by proximity and whether an easement barrier wall is present | LDC § 25-2-516(C) | Only when new construction is proposed within 200 feet of a qualifying hazardous pipeline. Check: utility plan for pipeline location, distance from centerline to proposed structures, and presence of Fire Chief determination letter or FPCM compliance documentation in submittal |\n| ZLU-15.37 | 50-foot setback from the centerline of an open waterway not maintained per Ordinance 030605-24 | Ordinance 030605-24 | Only when site is adjacent to or contains an open waterway. Check: waterway centerline location on grading or drainage plan versus proposed improvements. Note: this ordinance has not been verified in Bureau; flag for human confirmation of current status |\n| ZLU-15.38 | Buildings not set back a minimum of 30 feet from the centerline of streets with less than 60-foot right-of-way for fire department access | Reviewer Convention | Only when site has frontage on a street with less than 60-foot ROW. Check: ROW width on survey or street cross-section; building setback from street centerline |\n| ZLU-15.39 | Patios or balconies proposed within a required rear yard setback: required yards must be open and unobstructed from finished grade to sky per § 25-2-513(A); structures including balconies and covered patios cannot encroach into required rear yards; parking areas in required yards are permitted unless prohibited by Article 10 (§ 25-2-513(D)) | LDC § 25-2-513(A) | Only when patios or balconies are proposed and a rear yard setback applies. Check: site plan and floor plans for patio/balcony extent versus rear setback line |\n| ZLU-15.40 | Transformer pad clearances not maintained per Austin Energy Design Criteria 1.10.4: (a) 10 feet of open area required on hot-stick-use (pad-locked) sides to any building or structure; (b) 5 feet to brick or masonry building or structure (may be reduced to 3 ft with AE Design written approval under specific conditions); (c) 12 feet to non-brick/masonry buildings or mixed structures (oil-filled equipment); (d) 3 feet from sides without operators/controls to removable fence; (e) 5 feet from sides with operators/controls to removable fence; (f) 15 feet in confined space or vault installations; (g) 2 feet from back of sidewalks; (h) equipment pads within 6 feet of parking/traffic areas require bollards when ≤ 4 feet. Violation pattern: site plan shows transformer adjacent to a wood-frame commercial building with only 5-ft clearance using the brick/masonry standard when the 12-ft non-masonry standard applies — verify building construction type before applying clearance row | Austin Energy Design Criteria 1.10.4 | Only when transformer pads are proposed on site. Check: transformer pad locations and surrounding clearance dimensions; confirm building construction type to select the correct clearance standard |\n| ZLU-15.41 | Fencing 4 feet or greater in height adjacent to fire hydrants creates an obstruction that must be accounted for when measuring required hydrant distances; fencing under 4 feet may be disregarded, but fencing at or above 4 feet must be treated as an obstruction | Reviewer Convention | Only when fencing is proposed within the fire hydrant measurement area. Check: fence height and location relative to hydrants on site plan |\n| ZLU-15.42 | Plans for projects subject to Article 10 that encroach on compatibility buffer, height limit, or setback requirements without a valid current-code relief mechanism: as of July 15, 2024, §§ 25-2-1081/1082 (compatibility waivers) are repealed; the only current relief paths are (1) BOA variance under §§ 25-2-472/474 — requires BOA order or certificate of variance demonstrating hardship findings in the submittal — or (2) site-specific amendment treated as a rezoning under § 25-2-1054 — requires Council ordinance granting the amendment. Plans submitted on or after July 15, 2024 that cite §25-2-1081, \"compatibility waiver,\" \"Planning Commission compatibility waiver,\" or \"notification/waiver fee\" are relying on repealed authority and the claimed relief has no legal basis | LDC § 25-2-472; LDC § 25-2-474; LDC § 25-2-1054 | Only when an Article 10 compliance deficiency (buffer encroachment, height limit exceedance) is identified and the applicant claims relief. Check: whether the approval document in the submittal is a current BOA variance order or a Council ordinance for a site-specific amendment; reject claims of compatibility waiver for post-July-2024 submittals |\n| ZLU-15.43 | Site-specific amendment required but not initiated: Council-level relief from Article 10 height limits or compatibility buffer width requirements is available only through a site-specific amendment under § 25-2-1054, treated as a rezoning; such an amendment is not available for sites zoned a special purpose base district (PUD, P, TOD, NBG, ERC, etc.), sites carrying a DB combining district, or sites subject to the University Neighborhood Overlay (UNO); if a site-specific amendment has been approved, the Council ordinance granting it must be provided and must specify which Article 10 standard is modified | LDC § 25-2-1054 | Only when plans claim or need Council-level modification of Article 10 height or buffer requirements. Check: whether a Council ordinance granting a site-specific amendment is in the submittal documents |\n| ZLU-15.44 | Zoning variance not obtained or applied for when the standard zoning district setback must be reduced below the code minimum (e.g., rear yard setback reduction for a use not involving compatibility standards); variance application must be submitted with required documentation and BOA approval must be documented | LDC § 25-2-472; LDC § 25-2-474 | Only when a reduction of a standard zoning setback (not compatibility) is required. Check: whether encroaching improvements require a variance and whether a BOA variance order is in the submittal |\n| ZLU-15.45 | Letter of non-enforcement not obtained when proposed construction does not comply with a setback line recorded on the final plat; request must be directed to the appropriate City staff per applicable City procedures | Reviewer Convention | Only when a setback line appears on the site's recorded final plat. Check: recorded plat for any platted setback lines differing from code setbacks |\n| ZLU-15.46 | Solid fence along a property line exceeds applicable height limits without meeting a qualifying exception: base maximum is 6 feet measured from natural grade (§ 25-2-899(D)); up to 7 feet is permitted where a grade change of 1 foot or more occurs along any run of fence (§ 25-2-899(E)); up to 8 feet is permitted under: (F) written consent of all adjoining owners PLUS grade change ≥ 2 ft within 50 ft of boundary OR structure enabling a child to climb over fence near hazard; (G) fence within building setback lines; (H) fence between a residential use and commercial/industrial property or an alley separating them. The \"6-foot average height\" language does not appear in the current code; the standard is a 6-foot maximum, not an average | LDC § 25-2-899(D); LDC § 25-2-899(E) | Only when solid fences are proposed at or near property lines. Check: fence height callouts on site plan or landscape plan against applicable exception in § 25-2-899 |\n| ZLU-15.47 | New fence constructed after July 31, 2023 violates design restrictions under § 25-2-899(I): spiked pickets above the top horizontal backer rail are prohibited; vertical pickets above the top rail with 2-to-9-inch spacing are prohibited; razor wire is prohibited; barbed wire is prohibited except at airports; fences creating a substantial risk of entrapment or impalement are prohibited; solid chain link must use knuckle selvage; these restrictions do not apply to fences constructed before July 31, 2023, or fences greater than 6 feet in height on non-residential property | LDC § 25-2-899(I) | Only when new fences are proposed to be constructed after July 31, 2023, and the construction type could implicate the design restrictions. Check: fence detail specifications on site plan or landscape plan; confirm construction date and fence height/property type to determine whether § 25-2-899(I) applies |\n| ZLU-15.48 | Site-specific ordinance or NCCD fence height requirements not shown or not met on plans: e.g., ordinance-required minimum fence heights along designated property lines; playground and pocket park perimeter fencing limited to 4-foot maximum see-through type with at least 2 pedestrian entry points; mobile home park boundary fencing minimum 6 feet per LDC § 25-2-1205 | Per applicable NCCD/CO/ordinance conditions; LDC § 25-2-1205 (mobile home park boundary fence) | Only when site is within an NCCD boundary, PUD, or has a named ordinance imposing fence height requirements, or when playgrounds, pocket parks, or mobile home parks are proposed. Check: applicable ordinance conditions and fence height callouts on plans |","numLines":142,"startLine":1,"totalLines":142}}}
