    22→- **Sheet 10 [Fire Protection Plan]:** Site plan illustrating fire lanes, hydrants, and fire zone details. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-10/guide.md`\n    23→- **Sheet 11 [Grading Plan]:** This sheet displays the detailed grading plan for the development site. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-11/guide.md`\n    24→- **Sheet 12 [Storm Sewer Plan]:** Displays the proposed storm sewer layout and drainage infrastructure details. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-12/guide.md`\n    25→- **Sheet 13 [Drainage Area Map]:** Existing and proposed drainage area maps with summary tables. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-13/guide.md`\n    26→- **Sheet 14 [Inlet Drainage Area Map]:** Site drainage area map and rational method runoff calculations. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-14/guide.md`\n    27→- **Sheet 15 [Biofiltration-Detention Pond Plan & Calculations]:** Biofiltration and detention pond plan, details, and engineering calculations. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-15/guide.md`\n    28→- **Sheet 16 [Biofiltration-Detention Pond Profiles]:** Biofiltration pond profiles, outfall details, and comprehensive maintenance requirements. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-16/guide.md`\n    29→- **Sheet 17 [Conveyance Cross Sections]:** Conveyance cross-section graphs and corresponding site plan view. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-17/guide.md`\n    30→- **Sheet 18 [Austin Water General Information & Construction Notes]:** Austin Water general information, construction notes, and flow tests. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-18/guide.md`\n    31→- **Sheet 19 [Utility Tap Plan]:** Utility tap plan showing proposed water and wastewater connections. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-19/guide.md`\n    32→- **Sheet 20 [Utility Tap Public Plan & Profile]:** Utility tap public plan and profile for townhomes project. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-20/guide.md`\n    33→- **Sheet 21 [Preliminary Electrical Design Plan]:** Layout of preliminary electrical design, routing, transformers, and meters. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-21/guide.md`\n    34→- **Sheet 22 [Construction Details 1]:** Construction details for curbs, sidewalks, driveways, and bicycle parking. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-22/guide.md`\n    35→- **Sheet 23 [Construction Details 2]:** Standard construction details for water infrastructure, valves, and fire hydrants. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-23/guide.md`\n    36→- **Sheet 24 [Construction Details 3]:** Standard construction details for water services and trench repairs. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-24/guide.md`\n    37→- **Sheet 25 [Construction Details 4]:** Standard details, plan, and section views for fire line meters. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-25/guide.md`\n    38→- **Sheet 26 [Construction Details 5]:** Standard details for wastewater service connections and sewer cleanouts. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-26/guide.md`\n    39→- **Sheet 27 [Construction Details 6]:** Construction details for wastewater manholes and duplex grinder pumps. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-27/guide.md`\n    40→- **Sheet 28 [Construction Details 7]:** Construction details for storm drain manholes and catch basins. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-28/guide.md`\n    41→- **Sheet 29 [Addressing Plan]:** Site plan showing building layouts and individual unit addresses. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-29/guide.md`\n    42→- **Sheet 30 [Existing Tree Plan]:** Site plan displaying existing trees, protection measures, and care notes. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-30/guide.md`\n    43→- **Sheet 31 [Landscape Plan]:** Landscape design plan featuring planting details and irrigation notes. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-31/guide.md`\n    44→- **Sheet 32 [Tree List]:** This page provides a detailed list of surveyed trees. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-32/guide.md`\n    45→- **Sheet 33 [Canopy Impact Exhibit]:** Exhibits detailing tree canopy calculations and construction impact analysis. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-33/guide.md`\n    46→- **Sheet 34 [Root Zone Impact Exhibit]:** Critical root zone impact diagrams, calculations, and tree photos. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-34/guide.md`\n    47→- **Sheet 35 [Landscape Calculations]:** Provides landscape calculations, plant lists, and tree preservation notes. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-35/guide.md`\n    48→- **Sheet 36 [Biofiltration Pond Plans & Calculations]:** Detailed planting plans and calculations for the biofiltration pond. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-36/guide.md`\n    49→- **Sheet 37 [Landscape Details]:** Contains landscape details for tree planting, tree protection, and shrubs. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-37/guide.md`\n    50→- **Sheet 38 [Wall Plan]:** Plan view, section details, and notes for retaining walls. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-38/guide.md`\n    51→- **Sheet 39 [Wall Plan Details And Notes]:** Details and cross-sections for multiple pond retaining walls. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-39/guide.md`\n    52→\n    53→## Supplementary Documents\n    54→\n    55→1. **Erosion Control Fiscal Estimate** (documentId: `e77f8b2c-e37c-41b5-8c16-0319b3c00cda`) — An engineer's cost estimate for erosion and sedimentation controls required for the project site. → `supplementary-docs/erosion-control-fiscal-estimate/overview.md`\n    56→2. **Consolidated Site Plan Application** (documentId: `2fa9891b-9ede-4030-9300-cce670e070e1`) — Completeness check submittal for a consolidated site plan permit application in the City of Austin. → `supplementary-docs/consolidated-site-plan-application/overview.md`\n    57→3. **Engineer's Summary Letter** (documentId: `726dac64-638d-4735-8a24-c3e2d243a2b8`) — An engineer's summary letter detailing existing conditions, proposed improvements, and utilities for the project site plan. → `supplementary-docs/engineer-s-summary-letter/overview.md`\n    58→4. **Completeness Check Results** (documentId: `ba9815a3-047c-473a-a152-86ba84aae6d1`) — City of Austin site plan completeness check results detailing reviewer statuses and applicant responses to deficiencies. → `supplementary-docs/completeness-check-results/overview.md`\n    59→5. **Travis County Tax Certificate** (documentId: `cc3df4f3-7134-4ca3-9571-48c0260bfeed`) — Official Travis County document certifying that property taxes for the specified lot are fully paid. → `supplementary-docs/travis-county-tax-certificate/overview.md`\n    60→6. **TIA Determination Worksheet** (documentId: `0dbc90ac-37a5-4a6d-a51f-42b839ecb91b`) — Completed worksheet determining that a traffic impact analysis is not required for the proposed site plan development. → `supplementary-docs/tia-determination-worksheet/overview.md`\n    61→7. **Master Comment Report** (documentId: `ca29fbe6-6361-4580-848e-7807ae462499`) — A comprehensive compilation of city staff review comments requiring updates for a site plan application. → `supplementary-docs/master-comment-report/overview.md`\n    62→8. **Project Review Form** (documentId: `e1c5768d-263b-4500-98ff-f6723ae5876c`) — A completed City of Austin form indicating applicable land development codes and site plan application details for the project. → `supplementary-docs/project-review-form/overview.md`\n    63→9. **Project Location Map** (documentId: `10f3f1b9-e25e-4118-bfe3-08ea287ce738`) — A map displaying the geographical location of the project site relative to surrounding streets and parcels. → `supplementary-docs/project-location-map/overview.md`\n    64→10. **Property Tax Documentation** (documentId: `135639d9-9070-45b5-8b3b-8df3dbb0c795`) — Provides evidence that formal tax certificates have been ordered and confirms zero property taxes are currently due. → `supplementary-docs/property-tax-documentation/overview.md`\n    65→\n    66→## Other Files\n    67→\n    68→- `facts.md` — Surveyed property facts (address, zoning, flood zone, etc.)\n    69→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"38ff40a3-baa1-4588-8a44-429884d301ed","uuid":"b68d3c09-0a42-402e-8983-16ff267ff151","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/README.md","content":"# Site Plan: Valley View Townhomes\n\n**Site Address:** N/A\n**Site Plan Number:** N/A\n**Zoning:** N/A\n\n## Primary Site Plan (39 sheets)\n\n**Document ID (for vision tool):** `1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777`\n\n### Sheet Index\n\n- **Sheet 1 [Cover]:** Cover sheet containing project details, location map, and sheet index. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-01/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 2 [Plat]:** Subdivision plat map showing legal boundaries, easements, and signatures. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-02/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 3 [General Notes]:** Comprehensive general notes, sequence of construction, and project legend. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-03/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 4 [Existing Conditions Survey]:** Topographic survey detailing existing structures, trees, and property boundaries. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-04/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 5 [Erosion and Sedimentation Control Plan]:** Site layout detailing erosion and sedimentation control measures. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-05/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 6 [Erosion Control Notes]:** Contains erosion control, tree protection, and special construction notes. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-06/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 7 [Erosion Control Details]:** Standard details and specifications for erosion and sediment control. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-07/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 8 [Tree Removal & Demolition Plan]:** Site plan detailing existing structure demolition and tree removal. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-08/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 9 [Site Plan]:** Detailed site plan layout with associated building and parking data. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-09/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 10 [Fire Protection Plan]:** Site plan illustrating fire lanes, hydrants, and fire zone details. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-10/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 11 [Grading Plan]:** This sheet displays the detailed grading plan for the development site. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-11/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 12 [Storm Sewer Plan]:** Displays the proposed storm sewer layout and drainage infrastructure details. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-12/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 13 [Drainage Area Map]:** Existing and proposed drainage area maps with summary tables. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-13/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 14 [Inlet Drainage Area Map]:** Site drainage area map and rational method runoff calculations. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-14/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 15 [Biofiltration-Detention Pond Plan & Calculations]:** Biofiltration and detention pond plan, details, and engineering calculations. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-15/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 16 [Biofiltration-Detention Pond Profiles]:** Biofiltration pond profiles, outfall details, and comprehensive maintenance requirements. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-16/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 17 [Conveyance Cross Sections]:** Conveyance cross-section graphs and corresponding site plan view. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-17/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 18 [Austin Water General Information & Construction Notes]:** Austin Water general information, construction notes, and flow tests. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-18/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 19 [Utility Tap Plan]:** Utility tap plan showing proposed water and wastewater connections. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-19/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 20 [Utility Tap Public Plan & Profile]:** Utility tap public plan and profile for townhomes project. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-20/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 21 [Preliminary Electrical Design Plan]:** Layout of preliminary electrical design, routing, transformers, and meters. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-21/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 22 [Construction Details 1]:** Construction details for curbs, sidewalks, driveways, and bicycle parking. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-22/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 23 [Construction Details 2]:** Standard construction details for water infrastructure, valves, and fire hydrants. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-23/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 24 [Construction Details 3]:** Standard construction details for water services and trench repairs. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-24/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 25 [Construction Details 4]:** Standard details, plan, and section views for fire line meters. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-25/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 26 [Construction Details 5]:** Standard details for wastewater service connections and sewer cleanouts. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-26/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 27 [Construction Details 6]:** Construction details for wastewater manholes and duplex grinder pumps. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-27/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 28 [Construction Details 7]:** Construction details for storm drain manholes and catch basins. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-28/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 29 [Addressing Plan]:** Site plan showing building layouts and individual unit addresses. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-29/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 30 [Existing Tree Plan]:** Site plan displaying existing trees, protection measures, and care notes. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-30/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 31 [Landscape Plan]:** Landscape design plan featuring planting details and irrigation notes. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-31/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 32 [Tree List]:** This page provides a detailed list of surveyed trees. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-32/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 33 [Canopy Impact Exhibit]:** Exhibits detailing tree canopy calculations and construction impact analysis. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-33/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 34 [Root Zone Impact Exhibit]:** Critical root zone impact diagrams, calculations, and tree photos. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-34/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 35 [Landscape Calculations]:** Provides landscape calculations, plant lists, and tree preservation notes. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-35/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 36 [Biofiltration Pond Plans & Calculations]:** Detailed planting plans and calculations for the biofiltration pond. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-36/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 37 [Landscape Details]:** Contains landscape details for tree planting, tree protection, and shrubs. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-37/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 38 [Wall Plan]:** Plan view, section details, and notes for retaining walls. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-38/guide.md`\n- **Sheet 39 [Wall Plan Details And Notes]:** Details and cross-sections for multiple pond retaining walls. → `primary-site-plan/sheet-39/guide.md`\n\n## Supplementary Documents\n\n1. **Erosion Control Fiscal Estimate** (documentId: `e77f8b2c-e37c-41b5-8c16-0319b3c00cda`) — An engineer's cost estimate for erosion and sedimentation controls required for the project site. → `supplementary-docs/erosion-control-fiscal-estimate/overview.md`\n2. **Consolidated Site Plan Application** (documentId: `2fa9891b-9ede-4030-9300-cce670e070e1`) — Completeness check submittal for a consolidated site plan permit application in the City of Austin. → `supplementary-docs/consolidated-site-plan-application/overview.md`\n3. **Engineer's Summary Letter** (documentId: `726dac64-638d-4735-8a24-c3e2d243a2b8`) — An engineer's summary letter detailing existing conditions, proposed improvements, and utilities for the project site plan. → `supplementary-docs/engineer-s-summary-letter/overview.md`\n4. **Completeness Check Results** (documentId: `ba9815a3-047c-473a-a152-86ba84aae6d1`) — City of Austin site plan completeness check results detailing reviewer statuses and applicant responses to deficiencies. → `supplementary-docs/completeness-check-results/overview.md`\n5. **Travis County Tax Certificate** (documentId: `cc3df4f3-7134-4ca3-9571-48c0260bfeed`) — Official Travis County document certifying that property taxes for the specified lot are fully paid. → `supplementary-docs/travis-county-tax-certificate/overview.md`\n6. **TIA Determination Worksheet** (documentId: `0dbc90ac-37a5-4a6d-a51f-42b839ecb91b`) — Completed worksheet determining that a traffic impact analysis is not required for the proposed site plan development. → `supplementary-docs/tia-determination-worksheet/overview.md`\n7. **Master Comment Report** (documentId: `ca29fbe6-6361-4580-848e-7807ae462499`) — A comprehensive compilation of city staff review comments requiring updates for a site plan application. → `supplementary-docs/master-comment-report/overview.md`\n8. **Project Review Form** (documentId: `e1c5768d-263b-4500-98ff-f6723ae5876c`) — A completed City of Austin form indicating applicable land development codes and site plan application details for the project. → `supplementary-docs/project-review-form/overview.md`\n9. **Project Location Map** (documentId: `10f3f1b9-e25e-4118-bfe3-08ea287ce738`) — A map displaying the geographical location of the project site relative to surrounding streets and parcels. → `supplementary-docs/project-location-map/overview.md`\n10. **Property Tax Documentation** (documentId: `135639d9-9070-45b5-8b3b-8df3dbb0c795`) — Provides evidence that formal tax certificates have been ordered and confirms zero property taxes are currently due. → `supplementary-docs/property-tax-documentation/overview.md`\n\n## Other Files\n\n- `facts.md` — Surveyed property facts (address, zoning, flood zone, etc.)\n","numLines":69,"startLine":1,"totalLines":69}}}
{"level":30,"time":1776973732326,"pid":123,"hostname":"844f2260-bff","step":"review-runs","item":"32.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01URSBHQgz191KHuydBJz4We","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Zoning Overlay Programs, Compatibility Standards, and Special Use Requirements\n     2→\n     3→## Description\n     4→\n     5→This guide covers Austin site plan review checks for overlay-specific and special district requirements beyond base use permissions and dimensional standards. It addresses the Downtown Density Bonus Program (DDBP), DMU downtown design standards, TOD and UNO subdistrict requirements, Waterfront Overlay, corridor plans, PUD/MUD compliance, Article 10 Compatibility Standards, ETOD requirements, residential use-specific standards (townhouse, ADU, UNO accessibility, multifamily transit proximity, tenant notification, condominium), the redevelopment exception and watershed rules, vesting rights and nonconforming use limits, and signage compliance.\n     6→\n     7→## Regulatory Overview\n     8→\n     9→This guide covers overlay-specific and special district compliance checks applied after basic use authorization and dimensional standards have been confirmed. Several combining districts and overlays materially affect development standards and must be identified before applying any specific standard. The **Downtown Density Bonus Program (DDBP)** under LDC § 25-2-586 allows CBD and eligible downtown properties to exceed base FAR and height in exchange for community benefits. The downtown DDBP boundary runs roughly from 17th Street (north) to Lady Bird Lake (south), Lamar Blvd (west) to IH-35 (east). All DDBP approvals must be obtained before site plan submittal (§ 25-2-586(H)); gatekeeper requirements include Urban Design Guidelines compliance (with Design Commission recommendation), Great Streets restrictive covenant, and Austin Energy Green Building 2-star certification. The director issues a written approval notice stating allowable FAR and height (§ 25-2-586(I)) — this letter must be present in the submittal. **Critical:** LDC § 25-2-714 governs WO additional floor area and is NOT a DDBP provision; citing § 25-2-714 for DDBP bonus calculations is a citation error. In the Rainey Street WO subdistrict, DDBP applies only for FAR above 8:1 or height above 350 feet; below those thresholds, LDC § 25-2-739 governs. The **UNO** (University Neighborhood Overlay, map code: UNO) is located roughly west of UT campus, bounded by W. 29th St (north), Guadalupe/University Ave (east), MLK Blvd (south), and a complex western boundary. UNO parking under § 25-6-591 is a **maximum ceiling** — a UNO project may provide no more than 60% of Appendix A parking; this is not a minimum. Division 9 regulations apply only upon owner election (§ 25-2-751). The **ETOD** (Equitable Transit-Oriented Development) combining district (map code: ETOD) under LDC § 25-2-182 is a recently established combining district for sites near transit corridors; check for \"ETOD\" in the zoning string and address applicable overlay requirements. The **CURE** combining district (§ 25-2-311) is property-specific; its parking provisions under § 25-6-593 were repealed November 13, 2023 — do not apply them.\n    10→\n    11→Article 10 Compatibility Standards was completely repealed and replaced effective July 15, 2024 (Ord. 20240516-004). The current Article 10 (LDC §§ 25-2-1051–25-2-1063) applies to sites zoned **MF-4 or less restrictive** AND within **75 feet** of a **triggering property**. A triggering property is a site with 1–3 dwelling units zoned SF-5 or more restrictive. **\"SF-5 or more restrictive\"** means: LA (Lake Austin), RR (Rural Residence), SF-1, SF-2, SF-3, SF-4A, SF-4B, or SF-5 — combining district suffixes (-NP, -CO, -H) do not affect this hierarchy; only the base zoning district code matters. Article 10 does NOT apply to: sites zoned CBD or DMU; structural alterations that do not increase square footage, area, or height; or projects with uses limited to duplex, SF, SF-attached, small-lot SF, two-unit, three-unit, adult care, or child care (§ 25-2-1052). Current Article 10 development standards: height limits of 40 ft (within 50 ft of triggering property), 60 ft (50–75 ft), or base zoning (≥75 ft); a 25-foot compatibility buffer along shared property lines with triggering property (reduced to 15 ft if the site is less than 75 ft wide); and screening, shielded lighting, 70 dB noise limit, and 25-foot amenity setback requirements at the shared property line (§ 25-2-1063). Condominium and townhouse uses are exempt from the buffer and setback requirements (§ 25-2-1062) but remain subject to height limits and screening/noise/design requirements. The former provisions at § 25-2-1065 (scale and clustering), § 25-2-1067 (50-foot intensive recreational use buffer), and § 25-2-1081 (BOA compatibility setback waiver) were all repealed July 15, 2024 and no longer govern. The former four-tier distance framework at 50/100/300/540 feet no longer applies.\n    12→\n    13→The redevelopment exception is an elective pathway for sites where existing development exceeds current watershed impervious cover limits. Three parallel sections govern based on watershed type: § 25-8-25 (urban/suburban watersheds — any development); § 25-8-26 (Barton Springs Zone — commercial only; City Council approval required if project adds more than 2,000 vehicle trips per day); and § 25-8-27 (Water Supply Rural/Suburban — commercial or residential >2 DU/lot; same Council approval trigger). **The 2,000 additional-trip threshold does NOT appear in § 25-8-25** (urban/suburban) — applying a trip generation limit to § 25-8-25 projects is an error. For vesting rights claims, a written Vested Rights Determination letter from the City Director under § 25-1-541(D) is required; the governing sections are LDC §§ 25-1-531–25-1-552 (Article 12). The shorthand \"25-1-12\" is not a valid section number. § 25-2-841 (group home proximity requirement) was repealed December 18, 2023 — do not apply it.\n    14→\n    15→**Key Thresholds:**\n    16→- **Article 10 site applicability:** Site zoned MF-4 or less restrictive AND within 75 feet of a triggering property (§ 25-2-1051)\n    17→- **Article 10 height:** 40 ft maximum within 50 ft of triggering property; 60 ft at 50–75 ft; base zoning at ≥75 ft (§ 25-2-1061)\n    18→- **Article 10 compatibility buffer:** 25 ft along shared property line; 15 ft if site width <75 ft from shared line (§ 25-2-1062)\n    19→- **Article 10 noise limit:** 70 dB maximum at shared property line or alleyway (§ 25-2-1063(B))\n    20→- **Article 10 amenity setback:** 25 ft from triggering property for on-site amenities available only to residents/occupants; multi-use trails are excepted (§ 25-2-1063(D))\n    21→- **DDBP:** All approvals must precede site plan submittal (§ 25-2-586(H)); director's approval letter required per § 25-2-586(I)\n    22→- **Rainey Street subdistrict:** DDBP applies only above 8:1 FAR / 350 ft height; § 25-2-739 governs below those thresholds\n    23→- **UNO parking:** 60% of Appendix A is the maximum allowed — not a minimum floor (§ 25-6-591)\n    24→- **Downtown Design maximum setback:** 10-foot maximum front and street-side yard setback for first 4 stories in CBD and DMU (§ 25-2-594; applicability per § 25-2-591)\n    25→- **Redevelopment exception 2,000-trip Council approval trigger:** Applies only to § 25-8-26 (Barton Springs Zone) and § 25-8-27 (WSR/WSS) — not to § 25-8-25 (urban/suburban)\n    26→\n    27→**Exceptions:**\n    28→- Article 10 does NOT apply to CBD/DMU sites; structural alterations; or duplex, SF, SF-attached, small-lot SF, two-unit, three-unit, adult care, or child care uses (§ 25-2-1052)\n    29→- Article 10 compatibility buffer and setback (§ 25-2-1062) do NOT apply to condominium or townhouse use (height limits and § 25-2-1063 screening/noise requirements still apply to those uses)\n    30→- Site-specific compatibility amendments may be granted by Council through rezoning per § 25-2-1054\n    31→- CURE combining district regulations supersede Subchapter E requirements to the extent of conflict (LDC Subchapter E, § 1.2.5(B)(2)(c))\n    32→- § 25-2-714 (WO additional floor area) governs Waterfront Overlay bonus FAR — it is NOT a DDBP provision; do not cite for DDBP compliance\n    33→- § 25-6-593 (CURE parking) is RESERVED/repealed effective November 13, 2023 — do not apply\n    34→- § 25-2-841 (group home proximity) is REPEALED effective December 18, 2023 — do not apply\n    35→\n    36→**Calculation Methods:**\n    37→- **DDBP bonus amount:** Established by the director's written approval notice (§ 25-2-586(I)); delineated on the site plan per the DDBP formal request (§ 25-2-586(E))\n    38→\n    39→## Code References\n    40→\n    41→- LDC § 25-2-182 — ETOD (Equitable Transit-Oriented Development) Combining District\n    42→- LDC § 25-2-586 — Downtown Density Bonus Program (DDBP) requirements and approvals\n    43→- LDC § 25-2-591 — Downtown Design Subpart B applicability (CBD and DMU)\n    44→- LDC § 25-2-594 — Downtown Design Maximum Setback Requirement (CBD and DMU)\n    45→- LDC § 25-2-739 — Rainey Street Subdistrict Regulations\n    46→- LDC § 25-2-691(B) — Waterfront Overlay District use provisions\n    47→- LDC §§ 25-2-754, 25-2-775, 25-2-776 — UNO multifamily, townhouse, and condominium use standards\n    48→- LDC § 25-2-751 — UNO Division 9 election and applicability\n    49→- LDC § 25-2-837(E) — Community Recreation use proximity requirement\n    50→- LDC § 25-2-897 — Accessory Dwelling Unit accessory to civic use occupancy requirement\n    51→- LDC § 25-2-947(D) — Group D nonconforming use expansion limit\n    52→- LDC § 25-2-1054 — Site-Specific Compatibility Amendments\n    53→- LDC §§ 25-2-1051–25-2-1063 — Article 10 Compatibility Standards (eff. July 15, 2024): § 25-2-1051 applicability; § 25-2-1052 exceptions; § 25-2-1053 triggering property; § 25-2-1054 site-specific amendments; § 25-2-1061 height limits; § 25-2-1062 buffers/setbacks; § 25-2-1063 screening/noise/design\n    54→- LDC § 25-1-703(B)(4) — Multifamily residential transit proximity requirement\n    55→- LDC § 25-1-712 — Tenant notification for multifamily demolition/displacement\n    56→- LDC §§ 25-1-531–25-1-552 — Vesting rights (Article 12): petition, determination, criteria, expiration\n    57→- LDC § 25-8-25 — Redevelopment exception in urban and suburban watersheds\n    58→- LDC § 25-8-26 — Redevelopment exception in the Barton Springs Zone (commercial only; Council approval threshold: >2,000 additional vehicle trips/day)\n    59→- LDC § 25-8-27 — Redevelopment exception in Water Supply Rural and Water Supply Suburban watersheds (same Council approval threshold)\n    60→- LDC Subchapter E, § 4.3.3.D — VMU Neighborhood Design Standards\n    61→- LDC Subchapter E, § 4.3.3.E — VMU dimensional standards exemption for DDBP-participating projects\n    62→- LDC Chapter 25-10 — Sign regulations and overlay sign district requirements\n    63→- LDC § 25-10-124 — Scenic Roadway sign regulations\n    64→- LDC § 25-10-133 — UNO sign district regulations\n    65→- LDC § 25-10-152 — Nonconforming Signs Ordinance area requirements\n    66→- LDC § 25-2-585 — Exterior lighting restriction (GO, LR, CS, CS-1 adjacent to LA, RR, SF-1)\n    67→- Ord. 20130620-077, Part 9.3 — Core Transit Corridor Design Standards for CS-MU-V properties\n    68→\n    69→## Documents to Review\n    70→\n    71→- **Site Plan / Cover Sheet** — Site Data Table; sign compliance note; density bonus note; neighborhood plan statement; UNO election notation; ETOD notation; compatibility notes referencing current ordinance (Ord. 20240516-004)\n    72→- **Zoning Map / Verification Letter** — Confirm base zoning, combining districts, overlays (CO, MU, UNO, VMU, TOD, ERC, ETOD, WO, CURE), and PUD/MUD designations\n    73→- **Conditional Overlay (CO) Ordinance** — Use restrictions, trip generation limits, and any development standard modifications in the CO\n    74→- **PUD / MUD Ordinance and Exhibits** — Special use permissions, setbacks, design guidelines, street profile plans, and any provisions superseding Subchapter E\n    75→- **DDBP Approval Letter / Design Commission Letter / Great Streets Approval** — Confirm bonus FAR and height allowances, gatekeeper compliance, and all required program approvals\n    76→- **Restrictive Covenant / Development Agreement** — Any recorded FAR caps, use restrictions, or development conditions (e.g., Brackenridge Agreement, density covenant)\n    77→- **Chapter 245 Determination / Accommodation Letters** — Applicable development standards if vesting or site-specific mitigation is claimed; confirm accommodation letter provisions are addressed on plan\n    78→- **Project Review Form / Environmental Report** — Confirm watershed classification for redevelopment exception projects (urban, suburban, Barton Springs Zone, WSR, or WSS)\n    79→- **Accessibility / Tenant Documentation** — Accessible unit percentages for UNO multifamily; tenant notification records for multifamily demolition or displacement\n    80→- **DDBP Bonus Calculation Exhibit** — Bonus area delineation and bonus amount calculations on site plan\n    81→- **ETOD Ordinance / Regulating Plan** — ETOD-specific use permissions, design standards, and dimensional modifications for sites with ETOD combining district designation\n    82→\n    83→## Checklist Items\n    84→\n    85→| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n    86→|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n    87→| ZLU-32.1 | Project in CBD district proposes FAR or height above the base maximum and it is not clarified whether DDBP participation is intended — applicant must formally confirm DDBP participation or non-participation prior to site plan approval per LDC § 25-2-586(H). | LDC § 25-2-586(H) | Only when site is in CBD district and proposes FAR or height exceeding base zoning maximum. Check: zoning field in facts.md for CBD designation; compare proposed FAR/height in zoning data table against base maximum |\n    88→| ZLU-32.2 | DDBP: formal request not submitted including project description, requested additional density, and documentation of compliance with DDBP Ordinance 20140227-054 per LDC § 25-2-586(E). | LDC § 25-2-586(E) | Only when DDBP participation is confirmed. Check: site plan cover sheet, zoning data table, or DDBP approval letter in submittal package for confirmation of DDBP participation |\n    89→| ZLU-32.3 | DDBP: areas of the site eligible for density bonuses not delineated on the site plan per the DDBP formal request requirements of LDC § 25-2-586(E) and Ordinance 20140227-054. Note: LDC § 25-2-714 governs WO additional floor area and is not the applicable citation for DDBP bonus area delineation. | LDC § 25-2-586(E) | Only when DDBP participation is confirmed. Check: site plan cover sheet, zoning data table, or DDBP approval letter in submittal package for confirmation of DDBP participation |\n    90→| ZLU-32.4 | DDBP: bonus amount calculation not shown on the site plan — the approved bonus FAR and height are established by the director's written notice of approval per LDC § 25-2-586(I) and must be reflected in the site plan's zoning data table. Note: LDC § 25-2-714 governs WO additional floor area and is not the applicable citation for DDBP bonus calculations. | LDC § 25-2-586(I) | Only when DDBP participation is confirmed. Check: site plan cover sheet, zoning data table, or DDBP approval letter in submittal package for confirmation of DDBP participation |\n    91→| ZLU-32.5 | DDBP approval letter not provided, or letter does not confirm the project's allowable FAR and height per LDC § 25-2-586(I). | LDC § 25-2-586(I) | Only when DDBP participation is confirmed. Check: site plan cover sheet, zoning data table, or DDBP approval letter in submittal package for confirmation of DDBP participation |\n    92→| ZLU-32.6 | DDBP: Design Commission recommendation on Urban Design Guidelines compliance not obtained per LDC § 25-2-586(C)(1)(a)(ii). | LDC § 25-2-586(C)(1)(a)(ii) | Only when DDBP participation is confirmed. Check: site plan cover sheet, zoning data table, or DDBP approval letter in submittal package for confirmation of DDBP participation |\n    93→| ZLU-32.7 | DDBP: Great Streets program restrictive covenant not obtained — Great Streets is a mandatory gatekeeper requirement for DDBP participation per LDC § 25-2-586(C). | LDC § 25-2-586(C) | Only when DDBP participation is confirmed. Check: site plan cover sheet, zoning data table, or DDBP approval letter in submittal package for confirmation of DDBP participation |\n    94→| ZLU-32.8 | DDBP: Restrictive Covenant for Design Plan not recorded per LDC § 25-2-586(C)(1). | LDC § 25-2-586(C)(1) | Only when DDBP participation is confirmed. Check: site plan cover sheet, zoning data table, or DDBP approval letter in submittal package for confirmation of DDBP participation |\n    95→| ZLU-32.9 | DDBP: Downtown Development Bonus Agreement not executed and recorded with the Urban Design Division per Ordinance 20140227-054 (DDBP enabling ordinance). | LDC § 25-2-586; Ord. 20140227-054 | Only when DDBP participation is confirmed. Check: site plan cover sheet, zoning data table, or DDBP approval letter in submittal package for confirmation of DDBP participation |\n    96→| ZLU-32.10 | DDBP: site plan does not include a density bonus note confirming that density, FAR, compatibility (except compatibility setback), and open space standards do not apply per LDC Subchapter E, § 4.3.3.E (dimensional standards exemption for DDBP-participating projects). | LDC Subchapter E, § 4.3.3.E | Only when DDBP participation is confirmed. Check: site plan cover sheet, zoning data table, or DDBP approval letter in submittal package for confirmation of DDBP participation |\n    97→| ZLU-32.11 | DMU zoning: building in the first four floors does not comply with the 10-foot maximum front and street-side yard setback requirement per LDC § 25-2-594 (Downtown Design Maximum Setback, applicable to CBD and DMU per § 25-2-591), and no applicable exception (historic property, remodel, qualifying use, or exempted geographic area) is documented. Note: LDC § 25-2-722 governs public works in the Waterfront Overlay and is not applicable to DMU setback standards. | LDC § 25-2-594 | Only when site is in DMU zoning and a modified front setback is proposed. Check: zoning field in facts.md |\n    98→| ZLU-32.12 | TOD Live/Work/Flex Subdistrict: proposed density exceeds maximum (typically 45 dwelling units per acre) without a qualifying development bonus program and applicable program not identified. | Applicable TOD regulating plan | Only when site is in a TOD Live/Work/Flex Subdistrict and proposed density exceeds the maximum. Check: overlay field in facts.md for TOD Live/Work/Flex Subdistrict designation; compare proposed density against subdistrict maximum in applicable TOD regulating plan |\n    99→| ZLU-32.13 | University Neighborhood Overlay (UNO): cover sheet does not include notation indicating whether property owner elects to be governed by Division 9 UNO requirements or remains subject to base zoning per LDC § 25-2-751. Note: UNO parking under § 25-6-591 is a maximum cap (no more than 60% of Appendix A parking may be provided), not a minimum. | LDC § 25-2-751 | Only when site is in UNO. Check: zoning field in facts.md for \"UNO\" in zoning designation |\n   100→| ZLU-32.14 | TOD subdistrict: proposed development exceeds the applicable subdistrict density, height, or FAR allowances without required approval — identify which TOD governing document applies (ERC Regulating Plan, Plaza Saltillo TOD, MLK TOD, Lamar/Justin Lane TOD, NBG Regulating Plan, or LDC §§ 25-2-766.11–766.14 initial regulations) before applying any specific standard. | Applicable TOD regulating plan | Only when site is in a TOD subdistrict. Check: overlay field in facts.md |\n   101→| ZLU-32.15 | Waterfront Overlay District: use provisions not addressed — verify whether proposed residential uses are authorized under applicable overlay provisions per LDC § 25-2-691(B). WO applies to all property within 16 geographically defined subdistricts along Town Lake and the Colorado River; check for \"WO\" in the zoning string. | LDC § 25-2-691(B) | Only when site is in the Waterfront Overlay District. Check: zoning field in facts.md for \"WO\" suffix |\n   102→| ZLU-32.16 | ERC Core Transit Corridor: compliance with ERC Regulating Plan requirements and adopted ASMP standards not demonstrated. | ERC Regulating Plan | Only when site is in an ERC Core Transit Corridor. Check: overlay field in facts.md |\n   103→| ZLU-32.17 | Adopted corridor plan applies to site: compliance with corridor design standards and ATD-approved corridor configuration not demonstrated. | Reviewer Convention | Only when site is subject to an adopted corridor plan. Check: corridor plan field in facts.md |\n   104→| ZLU-32.18 | Site is in a PUD, MUD, or other special zone: compliance with applicable special standards (use permissions, setbacks, design guidelines, street profiles, CEF buffers) beyond base zoning not demonstrated. | Applicable PUD/MUD ordinance | Only when site is in a PUD, MUD, or other special zone. Check: zoning field in facts.md |\n   105→| ZLU-32.19 | CS-MU-V zoned property: Core Transit Corridor Design Standards per Ordinance 20130620-077, Part 9.3 not addressed on site plan. | Ord. 20130620-077, Part 9.3 | Only when site is zoned CS-MU-V. Check: zoning field in facts.md for CS-MU-V designation |\n   106→| ZLU-32.20 | Site plan does not include a statement addressing how the proposed development complies with the applicable adopted neighborhood plan. | Reviewer Convention | Only when site is subject to an adopted neighborhood plan. Check: neighborhood plan field in facts.md |\n   107→| ZLU-32.21 | Site plan does not demonstrate compliance with current LDC Article 10 Compatibility Standards (LDC §§ 25-2-1051–25-2-1063, eff. July 15, 2024). Article 10 applies to sites zoned MF-4 or less restrictive (MF-4, MF-5, MF-6, commercial, or industrial) AND within 75 feet of a triggering property. A triggering property is a site with 1–3 dwelling units zoned SF-5 or more restrictive (LA, RR, SF-1, SF-2, SF-3, SF-4A, SF-4B, or SF-5). The dwelling unit count on the adjacent parcel must be verified — an SF-3-zoned property containing only an office building does not qualify as a triggering property. CBD and DMU sites are excluded from Article 10. | LDC § 25-2-1051 | Only when site is zoned MF-4 or less restrictive and within 75 feet of a triggering property. Not applicable to CBD or DMU sites. Check: zoning and adjacent property characteristics in facts.md and site plan |\n   108→| ZLU-32.22 | Compatibility notes not included on site plan specifying which LDC sections are applicable, or notes do not reflect the current Article 10 ordinance (Ord. 20240516-004, eff. July 15, 2024) per LDC § 25-2-1063. Notes referencing § 25-2-1065 (repealed), § 25-2-1067 (repealed), or old distance tiers (100 ft, 300 ft, 540 ft) do not comply with current code. | LDC § 25-2-1063 | Only when compatibility standards are triggered. Check: same applicability as ZLU-32.21 |\n   109→| ZLU-32.23 | Site in GO, LR, CS, or CS-1 zoning with a building exceeding 35 feet in height, adjacent to LA, RR, or SF-1 zoning: site plan does not include a note prohibiting exterior lighting above the second floor per LDC § 25-2-585. Note: § 25-2-585 applies only to GO, LR, CS, and CS-1 (not GR); the adjacency trigger is LA, RR, or SF-1 only (not all SF-5 or more restrictive districts); and the 35-foot height threshold is a required condition. | LDC § 25-2-585 | Only when site is in GO, LR, CS, or CS-1 zoning with a building exceeding 35 feet in height, adjacent to LA, RR, or SF-1 zoning. Check: zoning, building height, and adjacent property zoning in facts.md |\n   110→| ZLU-32.24 | On-site amenity available exclusively to residents or occupants (e.g., pool, court, playground) proposed within 25 feet of a triggering property per LDC § 25-2-1063(D): amenity location must be moved, redesigned, or documented as compliant. Multi-use trails are specifically excluded from this 25-foot restriction. Note: the former 50-foot buffer under § 25-2-1067 was repealed July 15, 2024; the current standard is 25 feet. | LDC § 25-2-1063(D) | Only when Article 10 applies (site zoned MF-4 or less restrictive, within 75 ft of triggering property) and an on-site amenity is proposed within 25 feet of the triggering property. Check: site plan layout and distances |\n   111→| ZLU-32.25 | Community Recreation use (conditional use): baseball, softball, or football field proposed less than 300 feet from SF-5 or more restrictive zoning district without required approval per LDC § 25-2-837(E). Note: the LDC use classification is \"Community Recreation\" (§ 25-2-837), not \"Outdoor Sports and Recreation.\" The violation is proximity less than 300 feet from SF-5 or more restrictive zoning; a field located at least 300 feet away is compliant. | LDC § 25-2-837(E) | Only when Community Recreation (baseball/softball/football field) is proposed as a conditional use within 300 feet of SF-5 or more restrictive zoning. Check: distance from site to SF-5 or more restrictive zoning boundary |\n   112→| ZLU-32.26 | Outdoor noise sources (including amplified music) on the side of the building or site adjacent to a triggering property may cause sound levels exceeding 70 decibels measured at the shared property line or alleyway — compliance with the noise limit per LDC § 25-2-1063(B) not demonstrated. Note: outdoor amplified music restrictions may also be established by project-specific conditional overlay (CO) conditions; check applicable CO ordinance. | LDC § 25-2-1063(B) | Only when Article 10 applies (site zoned MF-4 or less restrictive, within 75 ft of triggering property) and outdoor uses or noise sources are proposed. Check: site plan and CO ordinance |\n   113→| ZLU-32.27 | VMU building or mixed-use building adjacent to SF-5 or more restrictive zoning: Neighborhood Design Standards (LDC Subchapter E, Table D) not demonstrated per LDC Subchapter E, § 4.3.3.D. Note: for VMU Neighborhood Design Standards, the adjacency trigger is broader — it applies when adjacent to an SF-5 or more restrictive zoning district OR adjacent to property containing a use permitted in an SF-5 or more restrictive district (e.g., a single-family home on MF-2-zoned land). | LDC Subchapter E, § 4.3.3.D | Only when VMU or mixed-use building is adjacent to SF-5 or more restrictive zoning or to property with uses permitted in SF-5 or more restrictive districts. Check: zoning and adjacent property information in facts.md and site plan |\n   114→| ZLU-32.28 | Rain garden proposed within 25 feet of a triggering property as the basis for amenity placement under LDC § 25-2-1063(D): rain garden design incorporates concrete, which disqualifies it from qualifying as a compliant amenity setback arrangement under the on-site amenity restriction. | LDC § 25-2-1063(D) | Only when a rain garden is proposed within 25 feet of a triggering property as the basis for amenity placement within that distance. Check: site plan showing rain garden location relative to triggering property and materials specification |\n   115→| ZLU-32.29 | Site within 75 feet of a triggering property does not document whether an Article 10 exception under LDC § 25-2-1052 applies — Article 10 does not apply to: (1) sites zoned CBD or DMU; (2) structural alterations that do not increase square footage, area, or height; or (3) sites used for duplex, single-family, SF-attached, small-lot SF, two-unit, three-unit, adult care (limited or general), or child care (limited or general) use. When an exception applies, the site plan should note the applicable exception to prevent unnecessary compliance conditions. | LDC § 25-2-1052 | Only when site appears to be within 75 feet of a triggering property but claims or may qualify for an Article 10 exception based on zoning designation (CBD/DMU) or use type. Check: zoning designation and proposed use in facts.md |\n   116→| ZLU-32.30 | Site plan does not demonstrate compliance with the Equitable Transit-Oriented Development (ETOD) combining district requirements under LDC § 25-2-182 — ETOD overlay-specific use permissions, dimensional modifications, or design standards have not been identified or addressed. | LDC § 25-2-182 | Only when site has ETOD combining district in zoning designation. Check: zoning field in facts.md for \"ETOD\" suffix |\n   117→| ZLU-32.31 | Townhouse project: each townhouse unit not located on a separate townhouse lot as required by LDC § 25-2-775 — if separate lots cannot be provided, project must be redesignated as Multifamily Residential and all documentation revised accordingly. | LDC § 25-2-775 | Only when townhouse use is proposed. Check: proposed use in facts.md or cover sheet |\n   118→| ZLU-32.32 | Accessory Dwelling Unit accessory to a civic use (e.g., Religious Assembly): site plan does not include a notation that the ADU must be occupied by a family with at least one member employed on-site for security, maintenance, management, supervision, or personal service per LDC § 25-2-897. | LDC § 25-2-897 | Only when an ADU is proposed as accessory to a civic use. Check: proposed use in facts.md or cover sheet for ADU accessory to civic use (e.g., Religious Assembly) |\n   119→| ZLU-32.33 | Multifamily residential in UNO combining district: fewer than 10% of dwelling units are designed to be accessible for persons with mobility impairments per LDC § 25-2-754(C)(3). Note: this requirement applies only to multifamily within the UNO combining district (Division 9); it is not a general multifamily requirement. Accessibility requirements outside UNO derive from applicable federal (Fair Housing Act, ADA) or state (Texas Accessibility Standards) law. | LDC § 25-2-754(C)(3) | Only when multifamily residential use is proposed within the UNO combining district. Check: zoning field in facts.md for \"UNO\" in zoning designation |\n   120→| ZLU-32.34 | Multifamily residential in UNO combining district: fewer than 2% of dwelling units are designed to be accessible for persons with hearing or visual disabilities per LDC § 25-2-754(C)(4). Note: this requirement applies only to multifamily within the UNO combining district (Division 9); it is not a general multifamily requirement. | LDC § 25-2-754(C)(4) | Only when multifamily residential use is proposed within the UNO combining district. Check: zoning field in facts.md for \"UNO\" in zoning designation |\n   121→| ZLU-32.35 | Multifamily residential use: project is not within one-half mile walking distance of a public transit route per LDC § 25-1-703(B)(4) and documentation of an approved waiver from the director has not been provided. A director's waiver may be granted when the project is in a high opportunity area, Capital Metro transit plans are available, LIHTC criteria apply, or fair housing purposes are served. | LDC § 25-1-703(B)(4) | Only when multifamily use is proposed. Check: transit route proximity on vicinity map or facts.md |\n   122→| ZLU-32.36 | Demolition, alteration, or repair that displaces tenants of a multifamily residential structure: 120-day advance Tenant Notification required by LDC § 25-1-712 not demonstrated; or 270-day advance notice required for mobile home park tenant displacement not demonstrated. | LDC § 25-1-712 | Only when demolition, alteration, or repair that displaces tenants of a multifamily residential structure or mobile home park is proposed. Check: scope of work description, demolition notes, or applicant statement |\n   123→| ZLU-32.37 | Condominium use: open space and all other applicable requirements of LDC § 25-2-776 not demonstrated. | LDC § 25-2-776 | Only when condominium use is proposed. Check: proposed use in facts.md or cover sheet |\n   124→| ZLU-32.38 | Project claims the redevelopment exception: the Project Review Form or environmental documentation does not confirm the site is in an urban or suburban watershed as required for eligibility under LDC § 25-8-25. Note: § 25-8-25 applies to urban and suburban watersheds; \"Smart Growth Zone\" is an informal administrative term on application forms corresponding to the Edwards Aquifer Recharge Zone within the Desired Development Zone — the legal standard for § 25-8-25 eligibility is watershed classification (urban or suburban), not Smart Growth Zone designation. | LDC § 25-8-25 | Only when redevelopment exception is claimed and site is in an urban or suburban watershed. Check: watershed field in facts.md or Project Review Form |\n   125→| ZLU-32.39 | Project claims the redevelopment exception in the Barton Springs Zone (§ 25-8-26) or in a Water Supply Rural or Water Supply Suburban watershed (§ 25-8-27): proposed development generates more than 2,000 additional vehicle trips per day above the estimated traffic level of the most recently authorized use, triggering City Council approval requirement. Note: this 2,000-trip threshold applies only to §§ 25-8-26 and 25-8-27 — it does NOT apply to § 25-8-25 urban/suburban redevelopment exception projects. | LDC § 25-8-26(B)(3); LDC § 25-8-27 | Only when redevelopment exception is claimed in the Barton Springs Zone or a WSR/WSS watershed and proposed development adds more than 2,000 vehicle trips per day. Check: watershed classification in facts.md |\n   126→| ZLU-32.40 | Site plan does not demonstrate compliance with trip generation limits established by the applicable zoning ordinance or conditional overlay restriction. | CO Ordinance (project-specific) | Only when zoning ordinance or CO establishes vehicle trip generation limits. Check: CO ordinance |\n   127→| ZLU-32.41 | Group D nonconforming use expansion proposed: site has already undergone one prior expansion — LDC § 25-2-947(D) limits expansion of a Group D nonconforming use to one time only on the same lot; the proposed second expansion is not permitted. | LDC § 25-2-947(D) | Only when a Group D nonconforming use expansion is proposed and site has already undergone one prior expansion. Check: expansion history |\n   128→| ZLU-32.42 | Vesting rights claimed or project references earlier development standards under Chapter 245 of the Texas Local Government Code: a written Vested Rights Determination letter from the City Director has not been provided per LDC § 25-1-541(D), or documentation of the vesting date, project description, and filing of a Vested Rights Petition per LDC § 25-1-533 is not included in the submittal. The governing LDC sections are §§ 25-1-531–25-1-552 (Article 12) — \"LDC 25-1-12\" is not a valid section number. | LDC §§ 25-1-533, 25-1-541 | Only when vesting rights are claimed or earlier regulations are cited. Check: facts.md or applicant statement |\n   129→| ZLU-32.43 | Site-specific accommodation letter applies to the property (e.g., a 2009 Accommodation Letter specifying 1985 development standards): the applicable provisions are not identified on the site plan or demonstrated as complied with. | Reviewer Convention | Only when an area-specific accommodation letter, mitigation agreement, or site-specific code provision has been identified for the property. Check: Chapter 245 determination, facts.md |\n   130→| ZLU-32.44 | Site plan does not include a note stating that all signs must comply with the requirements of LDC Chapter 25-10. | LDC Chapter 25-10 | All sites |\n   131→| ZLU-32.45 | Site is in the University Neighborhood Overlay: signage must comply with UNO sign district regulations per LDC § 25-10-133, and this compliance is not addressed. | LDC § 25-10-133 | Only when site is in UNO. Check: zoning field in facts.md for \"UNO\" in zoning designation |\n   132→| ZLU-32.46 | Site is on or adjacent to a designated Scenic Roadway: sign compliance with LDC § 25-10-124 Scenic Roadway sign regulations not demonstrated. | LDC § 25-10-124 | Only when site is on or adjacent to a Scenic Roadway. Check: Scenic Roadway designation in facts.md |\n   133→| ZLU-32.47 | Site is within a Nonconforming Signs Ordinance area: all signs must comply with LDC § 25-10-152, and this requirement is not addressed on the site plan. | LDC § 25-10-152 | Only when site is within a Nonconforming Signs Ordinance area. Check: overlay or special district field in facts.md |\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"38ff40a3-baa1-4588-8a44-429884d301ed","uuid":"b9eb2762-d139-4364-a19d-557a40e5cd1a","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/bureau/jurisdictions/austin/review-guides/zlu-md-exp/32.md","content":"# Zoning Overlay Programs, Compatibility Standards, and Special Use Requirements\n\n## Description\n\nThis guide covers Austin site plan review checks for overlay-specific and special district requirements beyond base use permissions and dimensional standards. It addresses the Downtown Density Bonus Program (DDBP), DMU downtown design standards, TOD and UNO subdistrict requirements, Waterfront Overlay, corridor plans, PUD/MUD compliance, Article 10 Compatibility Standards, ETOD requirements, residential use-specific standards (townhouse, ADU, UNO accessibility, multifamily transit proximity, tenant notification, condominium), the redevelopment exception and watershed rules, vesting rights and nonconforming use limits, and signage compliance.\n\n## Regulatory Overview\n\nThis guide covers overlay-specific and special district compliance checks applied after basic use authorization and dimensional standards have been confirmed. Several combining districts and overlays materially affect development standards and must be identified before applying any specific standard. The **Downtown Density Bonus Program (DDBP)** under LDC § 25-2-586 allows CBD and eligible downtown properties to exceed base FAR and height in exchange for community benefits. The downtown DDBP boundary runs roughly from 17th Street (north) to Lady Bird Lake (south), Lamar Blvd (west) to IH-35 (east). All DDBP approvals must be obtained before site plan submittal (§ 25-2-586(H)); gatekeeper requirements include Urban Design Guidelines compliance (with Design Commission recommendation), Great Streets restrictive covenant, and Austin Energy Green Building 2-star certification. The director issues a written approval notice stating allowable FAR and height (§ 25-2-586(I)) — this letter must be present in the submittal. **Critical:** LDC § 25-2-714 governs WO additional floor area and is NOT a DDBP provision; citing § 25-2-714 for DDBP bonus calculations is a citation error. In the Rainey Street WO subdistrict, DDBP applies only for FAR above 8:1 or height above 350 feet; below those thresholds, LDC § 25-2-739 governs. The **UNO** (University Neighborhood Overlay, map code: UNO) is located roughly west of UT campus, bounded by W. 29th St (north), Guadalupe/University Ave (east), MLK Blvd (south), and a complex western boundary. UNO parking under § 25-6-591 is a **maximum ceiling** — a UNO project may provide no more than 60% of Appendix A parking; this is not a minimum. Division 9 regulations apply only upon owner election (§ 25-2-751). The **ETOD** (Equitable Transit-Oriented Development) combining district (map code: ETOD) under LDC § 25-2-182 is a recently established combining district for sites near transit corridors; check for \"ETOD\" in the zoning string and address applicable overlay requirements. The **CURE** combining district (§ 25-2-311) is property-specific; its parking provisions under § 25-6-593 were repealed November 13, 2023 — do not apply them.\n\nArticle 10 Compatibility Standards was completely repealed and replaced effective July 15, 2024 (Ord. 20240516-004). The current Article 10 (LDC §§ 25-2-1051–25-2-1063) applies to sites zoned **MF-4 or less restrictive** AND within **75 feet** of a **triggering property**. A triggering property is a site with 1–3 dwelling units zoned SF-5 or more restrictive. **\"SF-5 or more restrictive\"** means: LA (Lake Austin), RR (Rural Residence), SF-1, SF-2, SF-3, SF-4A, SF-4B, or SF-5 — combining district suffixes (-NP, -CO, -H) do not affect this hierarchy; only the base zoning district code matters. Article 10 does NOT apply to: sites zoned CBD or DMU; structural alterations that do not increase square footage, area, or height; or projects with uses limited to duplex, SF, SF-attached, small-lot SF, two-unit, three-unit, adult care, or child care (§ 25-2-1052). Current Article 10 development standards: height limits of 40 ft (within 50 ft of triggering property), 60 ft (50–75 ft), or base zoning (≥75 ft); a 25-foot compatibility buffer along shared property lines with triggering property (reduced to 15 ft if the site is less than 75 ft wide); and screening, shielded lighting, 70 dB noise limit, and 25-foot amenity setback requirements at the shared property line (§ 25-2-1063). Condominium and townhouse uses are exempt from the buffer and setback requirements (§ 25-2-1062) but remain subject to height limits and screening/noise/design requirements. The former provisions at § 25-2-1065 (scale and clustering), § 25-2-1067 (50-foot intensive recreational use buffer), and § 25-2-1081 (BOA compatibility setback waiver) were all repealed July 15, 2024 and no longer govern. The former four-tier distance framework at 50/100/300/540 feet no longer applies.\n\nThe redevelopment exception is an elective pathway for sites where existing development exceeds current watershed impervious cover limits. Three parallel sections govern based on watershed type: § 25-8-25 (urban/suburban watersheds — any development); § 25-8-26 (Barton Springs Zone — commercial only; City Council approval required if project adds more than 2,000 vehicle trips per day); and § 25-8-27 (Water Supply Rural/Suburban — commercial or residential >2 DU/lot; same Council approval trigger). **The 2,000 additional-trip threshold does NOT appear in § 25-8-25** (urban/suburban) — applying a trip generation limit to § 25-8-25 projects is an error. For vesting rights claims, a written Vested Rights Determination letter from the City Director under § 25-1-541(D) is required; the governing sections are LDC §§ 25-1-531–25-1-552 (Article 12). The shorthand \"25-1-12\" is not a valid section number. § 25-2-841 (group home proximity requirement) was repealed December 18, 2023 — do not apply it.\n\n**Key Thresholds:**\n- **Article 10 site applicability:** Site zoned MF-4 or less restrictive AND within 75 feet of a triggering property (§ 25-2-1051)\n- **Article 10 height:** 40 ft maximum within 50 ft of triggering property; 60 ft at 50–75 ft; base zoning at ≥75 ft (§ 25-2-1061)\n- **Article 10 compatibility buffer:** 25 ft along shared property line; 15 ft if site width <75 ft from shared line (§ 25-2-1062)\n- **Article 10 noise limit:** 70 dB maximum at shared property line or alleyway (§ 25-2-1063(B))\n- **Article 10 amenity setback:** 25 ft from triggering property for on-site amenities available only to residents/occupants; multi-use trails are excepted (§ 25-2-1063(D))\n- **DDBP:** All approvals must precede site plan submittal (§ 25-2-586(H)); director's approval letter required per § 25-2-586(I)\n- **Rainey Street subdistrict:** DDBP applies only above 8:1 FAR / 350 ft height; § 25-2-739 governs below those thresholds\n- **UNO parking:** 60% of Appendix A is the maximum allowed — not a minimum floor (§ 25-6-591)\n- **Downtown Design maximum setback:** 10-foot maximum front and street-side yard setback for first 4 stories in CBD and DMU (§ 25-2-594; applicability per § 25-2-591)\n- **Redevelopment exception 2,000-trip Council approval trigger:** Applies only to § 25-8-26 (Barton Springs Zone) and § 25-8-27 (WSR/WSS) — not to § 25-8-25 (urban/suburban)\n\n**Exceptions:**\n- Article 10 does NOT apply to CBD/DMU sites; structural alterations; or duplex, SF, SF-attached, small-lot SF, two-unit, three-unit, adult care, or child care uses (§ 25-2-1052)\n- Article 10 compatibility buffer and setback (§ 25-2-1062) do NOT apply to condominium or townhouse use (height limits and § 25-2-1063 screening/noise requirements still apply to those uses)\n- Site-specific compatibility amendments may be granted by Council through rezoning per § 25-2-1054\n- CURE combining district regulations supersede Subchapter E requirements to the extent of conflict (LDC Subchapter E, § 1.2.5(B)(2)(c))\n- § 25-2-714 (WO additional floor area) governs Waterfront Overlay bonus FAR — it is NOT a DDBP provision; do not cite for DDBP compliance\n- § 25-6-593 (CURE parking) is RESERVED/repealed effective November 13, 2023 — do not apply\n- § 25-2-841 (group home proximity) is REPEALED effective December 18, 2023 — do not apply\n\n**Calculation Methods:**\n- **DDBP bonus amount:** Established by the director's written approval notice (§ 25-2-586(I)); delineated on the site plan per the DDBP formal request (§ 25-2-586(E))\n\n## Code References\n\n- LDC § 25-2-182 — ETOD (Equitable Transit-Oriented Development) Combining District\n- LDC § 25-2-586 — Downtown Density Bonus Program (DDBP) requirements and approvals\n- LDC § 25-2-591 — Downtown Design Subpart B applicability (CBD and DMU)\n- LDC § 25-2-594 — Downtown Design Maximum Setback Requirement (CBD and DMU)\n- LDC § 25-2-739 — Rainey Street Subdistrict Regulations\n- LDC § 25-2-691(B) — Waterfront Overlay District use provisions\n- LDC §§ 25-2-754, 25-2-775, 25-2-776 — UNO multifamily, townhouse, and condominium use standards\n- LDC § 25-2-751 — UNO Division 9 election and applicability\n- LDC § 25-2-837(E) — Community Recreation use proximity requirement\n- LDC § 25-2-897 — Accessory Dwelling Unit accessory to civic use occupancy requirement\n- LDC § 25-2-947(D) — Group D nonconforming use expansion limit\n- LDC § 25-2-1054 — Site-Specific Compatibility Amendments\n- LDC §§ 25-2-1051–25-2-1063 — Article 10 Compatibility Standards (eff. July 15, 2024): § 25-2-1051 applicability; § 25-2-1052 exceptions; § 25-2-1053 triggering property; § 25-2-1054 site-specific amendments; § 25-2-1061 height limits; § 25-2-1062 buffers/setbacks; § 25-2-1063 screening/noise/design\n- LDC § 25-1-703(B)(4) — Multifamily residential transit proximity requirement\n- LDC § 25-1-712 — Tenant notification for multifamily demolition/displacement\n- LDC §§ 25-1-531–25-1-552 — Vesting rights (Article 12): petition, determination, criteria, expiration\n- LDC § 25-8-25 — Redevelopment exception in urban and suburban watersheds\n- LDC § 25-8-26 — Redevelopment exception in the Barton Springs Zone (commercial only; Council approval threshold: >2,000 additional vehicle trips/day)\n- LDC § 25-8-27 — Redevelopment exception in Water Supply Rural and Water Supply Suburban watersheds (same Council approval threshold)\n- LDC Subchapter E, § 4.3.3.D — VMU Neighborhood Design Standards\n- LDC Subchapter E, § 4.3.3.E — VMU dimensional standards exemption for DDBP-participating projects\n- LDC Chapter 25-10 — Sign regulations and overlay sign district requirements\n- LDC § 25-10-124 — Scenic Roadway sign regulations\n- LDC § 25-10-133 — UNO sign district regulations\n- LDC § 25-10-152 — Nonconforming Signs Ordinance area requirements\n- LDC § 25-2-585 — Exterior lighting restriction (GO, LR, CS, CS-1 adjacent to LA, RR, SF-1)\n- Ord. 20130620-077, Part 9.3 — Core Transit Corridor Design Standards for CS-MU-V properties\n\n## Documents to Review\n\n- **Site Plan / Cover Sheet** — Site Data Table; sign compliance note; density bonus note; neighborhood plan statement; UNO election notation; ETOD notation; compatibility notes referencing current ordinance (Ord. 20240516-004)\n- **Zoning Map / Verification Letter** — Confirm base zoning, combining districts, overlays (CO, MU, UNO, VMU, TOD, ERC, ETOD, WO, CURE), and PUD/MUD designations\n- **Conditional Overlay (CO) Ordinance** — Use restrictions, trip generation limits, and any development standard modifications in the CO\n- **PUD / MUD Ordinance and Exhibits** — Special use permissions, setbacks, design guidelines, street profile plans, and any provisions superseding Subchapter E\n- **DDBP Approval Letter / Design Commission Letter / Great Streets Approval** — Confirm bonus FAR and height allowances, gatekeeper compliance, and all required program approvals\n- **Restrictive Covenant / Development Agreement** — Any recorded FAR caps, use restrictions, or development conditions (e.g., Brackenridge Agreement, density covenant)\n- **Chapter 245 Determination / Accommodation Letters** — Applicable development standards if vesting or site-specific mitigation is claimed; confirm accommodation letter provisions are addressed on plan\n- **Project Review Form / Environmental Report** — Confirm watershed classification for redevelopment exception projects (urban, suburban, Barton Springs Zone, WSR, or WSS)\n- **Accessibility / Tenant Documentation** — Accessible unit percentages for UNO multifamily; tenant notification records for multifamily demolition or displacement\n- **DDBP Bonus Calculation Exhibit** — Bonus area delineation and bonus amount calculations on site plan\n- **ETOD Ordinance / Regulating Plan** — ETOD-specific use permissions, design standards, and dimensional modifications for sites with ETOD combining district designation\n\n## Checklist Items\n\n| ID | Deficiency | Code Citation | Applicability |\n|----|------------|---------------|---------------|\n| ZLU-32.1 | Project in CBD district proposes FAR or height above the base maximum and it is not clarified whether DDBP participation is intended — applicant must formally confirm DDBP participation or non-participation prior to site plan approval per LDC § 25-2-586(H). | LDC § 25-2-586(H) | Only when site is in CBD district and proposes FAR or height exceeding base zoning maximum. Check: zoning field in facts.md for CBD designation; compare proposed FAR/height in zoning data table against base maximum |\n| ZLU-32.2 | DDBP: formal request not submitted including project description, requested additional density, and documentation of compliance with DDBP Ordinance 20140227-054 per LDC § 25-2-586(E). | LDC § 25-2-586(E) | Only when DDBP participation is confirmed. Check: site plan cover sheet, zoning data table, or DDBP approval letter in submittal package for confirmation of DDBP participation |\n| ZLU-32.3 | DDBP: areas of the site eligible for density bonuses not delineated on the site plan per the DDBP formal request requirements of LDC § 25-2-586(E) and Ordinance 20140227-054. Note: LDC § 25-2-714 governs WO additional floor area and is not the applicable citation for DDBP bonus area delineation. | LDC § 25-2-586(E) | Only when DDBP participation is confirmed. Check: site plan cover sheet, zoning data table, or DDBP approval letter in submittal package for confirmation of DDBP participation |\n| ZLU-32.4 | DDBP: bonus amount calculation not shown on the site plan — the approved bonus FAR and height are established by the director's written notice of approval per LDC § 25-2-586(I) and must be reflected in the site plan's zoning data table. Note: LDC § 25-2-714 governs WO additional floor area and is not the applicable citation for DDBP bonus calculations. | LDC § 25-2-586(I) | Only when DDBP participation is confirmed. Check: site plan cover sheet, zoning data table, or DDBP approval letter in submittal package for confirmation of DDBP participation |\n| ZLU-32.5 | DDBP approval letter not provided, or letter does not confirm the project's allowable FAR and height per LDC § 25-2-586(I). | LDC § 25-2-586(I) | Only when DDBP participation is confirmed. Check: site plan cover sheet, zoning data table, or DDBP approval letter in submittal package for confirmation of DDBP participation |\n| ZLU-32.6 | DDBP: Design Commission recommendation on Urban Design Guidelines compliance not obtained per LDC § 25-2-586(C)(1)(a)(ii). | LDC § 25-2-586(C)(1)(a)(ii) | Only when DDBP participation is confirmed. Check: site plan cover sheet, zoning data table, or DDBP approval letter in submittal package for confirmation of DDBP participation |\n| ZLU-32.7 | DDBP: Great Streets program restrictive covenant not obtained — Great Streets is a mandatory gatekeeper requirement for DDBP participation per LDC § 25-2-586(C). | LDC § 25-2-586(C) | Only when DDBP participation is confirmed. Check: site plan cover sheet, zoning data table, or DDBP approval letter in submittal package for confirmation of DDBP participation |\n| ZLU-32.8 | DDBP: Restrictive Covenant for Design Plan not recorded per LDC § 25-2-586(C)(1). | LDC § 25-2-586(C)(1) | Only when DDBP participation is confirmed. Check: site plan cover sheet, zoning data table, or DDBP approval letter in submittal package for confirmation of DDBP participation |\n| ZLU-32.9 | DDBP: Downtown Development Bonus Agreement not executed and recorded with the Urban Design Division per Ordinance 20140227-054 (DDBP enabling ordinance). | LDC § 25-2-586; Ord. 20140227-054 | Only when DDBP participation is confirmed. Check: site plan cover sheet, zoning data table, or DDBP approval letter in submittal package for confirmation of DDBP participation |\n| ZLU-32.10 | DDBP: site plan does not include a density bonus note confirming that density, FAR, compatibility (except compatibility setback), and open space standards do not apply per LDC Subchapter E, § 4.3.3.E (dimensional standards exemption for DDBP-participating projects). | LDC Subchapter E, § 4.3.3.E | Only when DDBP participation is confirmed. Check: site plan cover sheet, zoning data table, or DDBP approval letter in submittal package for confirmation of DDBP participation |\n| ZLU-32.11 | DMU zoning: building in the first four floors does not comply with the 10-foot maximum front and street-side yard setback requirement per LDC § 25-2-594 (Downtown Design Maximum Setback, applicable to CBD and DMU per § 25-2-591), and no applicable exception (historic property, remodel, qualifying use, or exempted geographic area) is documented. Note: LDC § 25-2-722 governs public works in the Waterfront Overlay and is not applicable to DMU setback standards. | LDC § 25-2-594 | Only when site is in DMU zoning and a modified front setback is proposed. Check: zoning field in facts.md |\n| ZLU-32.12 | TOD Live/Work/Flex Subdistrict: proposed density exceeds maximum (typically 45 dwelling units per acre) without a qualifying development bonus program and applicable program not identified. | Applicable TOD regulating plan | Only when site is in a TOD Live/Work/Flex Subdistrict and proposed density exceeds the maximum. Check: overlay field in facts.md for TOD Live/Work/Flex Subdistrict designation; compare proposed density against subdistrict maximum in applicable TOD regulating plan |\n| ZLU-32.13 | University Neighborhood Overlay (UNO): cover sheet does not include notation indicating whether property owner elects to be governed by Division 9 UNO requirements or remains subject to base zoning per LDC § 25-2-751. Note: UNO parking under § 25-6-591 is a maximum cap (no more than 60% of Appendix A parking may be provided), not a minimum. | LDC § 25-2-751 | Only when site is in UNO. Check: zoning field in facts.md for \"UNO\" in zoning designation |\n| ZLU-32.14 | TOD subdistrict: proposed development exceeds the applicable subdistrict density, height, or FAR allowances without required approval — identify which TOD governing document applies (ERC Regulating Plan, Plaza Saltillo TOD, MLK TOD, Lamar/Justin Lane TOD, NBG Regulating Plan, or LDC §§ 25-2-766.11–766.14 initial regulations) before applying any specific standard. | Applicable TOD regulating plan | Only when site is in a TOD subdistrict. Check: overlay field in facts.md |\n| ZLU-32.15 | Waterfront Overlay District: use provisions not addressed — verify whether proposed residential uses are authorized under applicable overlay provisions per LDC § 25-2-691(B). WO applies to all property within 16 geographically defined subdistricts along Town Lake and the Colorado River; check for \"WO\" in the zoning string. | LDC § 25-2-691(B) | Only when site is in the Waterfront Overlay District. Check: zoning field in facts.md for \"WO\" suffix |\n| ZLU-32.16 | ERC Core Transit Corridor: compliance with ERC Regulating Plan requirements and adopted ASMP standards not demonstrated. | ERC Regulating Plan | Only when site is in an ERC Core Transit Corridor. Check: overlay field in facts.md |\n| ZLU-32.17 | Adopted corridor plan applies to site: compliance with corridor design standards and ATD-approved corridor configuration not demonstrated. | Reviewer Convention | Only when site is subject to an adopted corridor plan. Check: corridor plan field in facts.md |\n| ZLU-32.18 | Site is in a PUD, MUD, or other special zone: compliance with applicable special standards (use permissions, setbacks, design guidelines, street profiles, CEF buffers) beyond base zoning not demonstrated. | Applicable PUD/MUD ordinance | Only when site is in a PUD, MUD, or other special zone. Check: zoning field in facts.md |\n| ZLU-32.19 | CS-MU-V zoned property: Core Transit Corridor Design Standards per Ordinance 20130620-077, Part 9.3 not addressed on site plan. | Ord. 20130620-077, Part 9.3 | Only when site is zoned CS-MU-V. Check: zoning field in facts.md for CS-MU-V designation |\n| ZLU-32.20 | Site plan does not include a statement addressing how the proposed development complies with the applicable adopted neighborhood plan. | Reviewer Convention | Only when site is subject to an adopted neighborhood plan. Check: neighborhood plan field in facts.md |\n| ZLU-32.21 | Site plan does not demonstrate compliance with current LDC Article 10 Compatibility Standards (LDC §§ 25-2-1051–25-2-1063, eff. July 15, 2024). Article 10 applies to sites zoned MF-4 or less restrictive (MF-4, MF-5, MF-6, commercial, or industrial) AND within 75 feet of a triggering property. A triggering property is a site with 1–3 dwelling units zoned SF-5 or more restrictive (LA, RR, SF-1, SF-2, SF-3, SF-4A, SF-4B, or SF-5). The dwelling unit count on the adjacent parcel must be verified — an SF-3-zoned property containing only an office building does not qualify as a triggering property. CBD and DMU sites are excluded from Article 10. | LDC § 25-2-1051 | Only when site is zoned MF-4 or less restrictive and within 75 feet of a triggering property. Not applicable to CBD or DMU sites. Check: zoning and adjacent property characteristics in facts.md and site plan |\n| ZLU-32.22 | Compatibility notes not included on site plan specifying which LDC sections are applicable, or notes do not reflect the current Article 10 ordinance (Ord. 20240516-004, eff. July 15, 2024) per LDC § 25-2-1063. Notes referencing § 25-2-1065 (repealed), § 25-2-1067 (repealed), or old distance tiers (100 ft, 300 ft, 540 ft) do not comply with current code. | LDC § 25-2-1063 | Only when compatibility standards are triggered. Check: same applicability as ZLU-32.21 |\n| ZLU-32.23 | Site in GO, LR, CS, or CS-1 zoning with a building exceeding 35 feet in height, adjacent to LA, RR, or SF-1 zoning: site plan does not include a note prohibiting exterior lighting above the second floor per LDC § 25-2-585. Note: § 25-2-585 applies only to GO, LR, CS, and CS-1 (not GR); the adjacency trigger is LA, RR, or SF-1 only (not all SF-5 or more restrictive districts); and the 35-foot height threshold is a required condition. | LDC § 25-2-585 | Only when site is in GO, LR, CS, or CS-1 zoning with a building exceeding 35 feet in height, adjacent to LA, RR, or SF-1 zoning. Check: zoning, building height, and adjacent property zoning in facts.md |\n| ZLU-32.24 | On-site amenity available exclusively to residents or occupants (e.g., pool, court, playground) proposed within 25 feet of a triggering property per LDC § 25-2-1063(D): amenity location must be moved, redesigned, or documented as compliant. Multi-use trails are specifically excluded from this 25-foot restriction. Note: the former 50-foot buffer under § 25-2-1067 was repealed July 15, 2024; the current standard is 25 feet. | LDC § 25-2-1063(D) | Only when Article 10 applies (site zoned MF-4 or less restrictive, within 75 ft of triggering property) and an on-site amenity is proposed within 25 feet of the triggering property. Check: site plan layout and distances |\n| ZLU-32.25 | Community Recreation use (conditional use): baseball, softball, or football field proposed less than 300 feet from SF-5 or more restrictive zoning district without required approval per LDC § 25-2-837(E). Note: the LDC use classification is \"Community Recreation\" (§ 25-2-837), not \"Outdoor Sports and Recreation.\" The violation is proximity less than 300 feet from SF-5 or more restrictive zoning; a field located at least 300 feet away is compliant. | LDC § 25-2-837(E) | Only when Community Recreation (baseball/softball/football field) is proposed as a conditional use within 300 feet of SF-5 or more restrictive zoning. Check: distance from site to SF-5 or more restrictive zoning boundary |\n| ZLU-32.26 | Outdoor noise sources (including amplified music) on the side of the building or site adjacent to a triggering property may cause sound levels exceeding 70 decibels measured at the shared property line or alleyway — compliance with the noise limit per LDC § 25-2-1063(B) not demonstrated. Note: outdoor amplified music restrictions may also be established by project-specific conditional overlay (CO) conditions; check applicable CO ordinance. | LDC § 25-2-1063(B) | Only when Article 10 applies (site zoned MF-4 or less restrictive, within 75 ft of triggering property) and outdoor uses or noise sources are proposed. Check: site plan and CO ordinance |\n| ZLU-32.27 | VMU building or mixed-use building adjacent to SF-5 or more restrictive zoning: Neighborhood Design Standards (LDC Subchapter E, Table D) not demonstrated per LDC Subchapter E, § 4.3.3.D. Note: for VMU Neighborhood Design Standards, the adjacency trigger is broader — it applies when adjacent to an SF-5 or more restrictive zoning district OR adjacent to property containing a use permitted in an SF-5 or more restrictive district (e.g., a single-family home on MF-2-zoned land). | LDC Subchapter E, § 4.3.3.D | Only when VMU or mixed-use building is adjacent to SF-5 or more restrictive zoning or to property with uses permitted in SF-5 or more restrictive districts. Check: zoning and adjacent property information in facts.md and site plan |\n| ZLU-32.28 | Rain garden proposed within 25 feet of a triggering property as the basis for amenity placement under LDC § 25-2-1063(D): rain garden design incorporates concrete, which disqualifies it from qualifying as a compliant amenity setback arrangement under the on-site amenity restriction. | LDC § 25-2-1063(D) | Only when a rain garden is proposed within 25 feet of a triggering property as the basis for amenity placement within that distance. Check: site plan showing rain garden location relative to triggering property and materials specification |\n| ZLU-32.29 | Site within 75 feet of a triggering property does not document whether an Article 10 exception under LDC § 25-2-1052 applies — Article 10 does not apply to: (1) sites zoned CBD or DMU; (2) structural alterations that do not increase square footage, area, or height; or (3) sites used for duplex, single-family, SF-attached, small-lot SF, two-unit, three-unit, adult care (limited or general), or child care (limited or general) use. When an exception applies, the site plan should note the applicable exception to prevent unnecessary compliance conditions. | LDC § 25-2-1052 | Only when site appears to be within 75 feet of a triggering property but claims or may qualify for an Article 10 exception based on zoning designation (CBD/DMU) or use type. Check: zoning designation and proposed use in facts.md |\n| ZLU-32.30 | Site plan does not demonstrate compliance with the Equitable Transit-Oriented Development (ETOD) combining district requirements under LDC § 25-2-182 — ETOD overlay-specific use permissions, dimensional modifications, or design standards have not been identified or addressed. | LDC § 25-2-182 | Only when site has ETOD combining district in zoning designation. Check: zoning field in facts.md for \"ETOD\" suffix |\n| ZLU-32.31 | Townhouse project: each townhouse unit not located on a separate townhouse lot as required by LDC § 25-2-775 — if separate lots cannot be provided, project must be redesignated as Multifamily Residential and all documentation revised accordingly. | LDC § 25-2-775 | Only when townhouse use is proposed. Check: proposed use in facts.md or cover sheet |\n| ZLU-32.32 | Accessory Dwelling Unit accessory to a civic use (e.g., Religious Assembly): site plan does not include a notation that the ADU must be occupied by a family with at least one member employed on-site for security, maintenance, management, supervision, or personal service per LDC § 25-2-897. | LDC § 25-2-897 | Only when an ADU is proposed as accessory to a civic use. Check: proposed use in facts.md or cover sheet for ADU accessory to civic use (e.g., Religious Assembly) |\n| ZLU-32.33 | Multifamily residential in UNO combining district: fewer than 10% of dwelling units are designed to be accessible for persons with mobility impairments per LDC § 25-2-754(C)(3). Note: this requirement applies only to multifamily within the UNO combining district (Division 9); it is not a general multifamily requirement. Accessibility requirements outside UNO derive from applicable federal (Fair Housing Act, ADA) or state (Texas Accessibility Standards) law. | LDC § 25-2-754(C)(3) | Only when multifamily residential use is proposed within the UNO combining district. Check: zoning field in facts.md for \"UNO\" in zoning designation |\n| ZLU-32.34 | Multifamily residential in UNO combining district: fewer than 2% of dwelling units are designed to be accessible for persons with hearing or visual disabilities per LDC § 25-2-754(C)(4). Note: this requirement applies only to multifamily within the UNO combining district (Division 9); it is not a general multifamily requirement. | LDC § 25-2-754(C)(4) | Only when multifamily residential use is proposed within the UNO combining district. Check: zoning field in facts.md for \"UNO\" in zoning designation |\n| ZLU-32.35 | Multifamily residential use: project is not within one-half mile walking distance of a public transit route per LDC § 25-1-703(B)(4) and documentation of an approved waiver from the director has not been provided. A director's waiver may be granted when the project is in a high opportunity area, Capital Metro transit plans are available, LIHTC criteria apply, or fair housing purposes are served. | LDC § 25-1-703(B)(4) | Only when multifamily use is proposed. Check: transit route proximity on vicinity map or facts.md |\n| ZLU-32.36 | Demolition, alteration, or repair that displaces tenants of a multifamily residential structure: 120-day advance Tenant Notification required by LDC § 25-1-712 not demonstrated; or 270-day advance notice required for mobile home park tenant displacement not demonstrated. | LDC § 25-1-712 | Only when demolition, alteration, or repair that displaces tenants of a multifamily residential structure or mobile home park is proposed. Check: scope of work description, demolition notes, or applicant statement |\n| ZLU-32.37 | Condominium use: open space and all other applicable requirements of LDC § 25-2-776 not demonstrated. | LDC § 25-2-776 | Only when condominium use is proposed. Check: proposed use in facts.md or cover sheet |\n| ZLU-32.38 | Project claims the redevelopment exception: the Project Review Form or environmental documentation does not confirm the site is in an urban or suburban watershed as required for eligibility under LDC § 25-8-25. Note: § 25-8-25 applies to urban and suburban watersheds; \"Smart Growth Zone\" is an informal administrative term on application forms corresponding to the Edwards Aquifer Recharge Zone within the Desired Development Zone — the legal standard for § 25-8-25 eligibility is watershed classification (urban or suburban), not Smart Growth Zone designation. | LDC § 25-8-25 | Only when redevelopment exception is claimed and site is in an urban or suburban watershed. Check: watershed field in facts.md or Project Review Form |\n| ZLU-32.39 | Project claims the redevelopment exception in the Barton Springs Zone (§ 25-8-26) or in a Water Supply Rural or Water Supply Suburban watershed (§ 25-8-27): proposed development generates more than 2,000 additional vehicle trips per day above the estimated traffic level of the most recently authorized use, triggering City Council approval requirement. Note: this 2,000-trip threshold applies only to §§ 25-8-26 and 25-8-27 — it does NOT apply to § 25-8-25 urban/suburban redevelopment exception projects. | LDC § 25-8-26(B)(3); LDC § 25-8-27 | Only when redevelopment exception is claimed in the Barton Springs Zone or a WSR/WSS watershed and proposed development adds more than 2,000 vehicle trips per day. Check: watershed classification in facts.md |\n| ZLU-32.40 | Site plan does not demonstrate compliance with trip generation limits established by the applicable zoning ordinance or conditional overlay restriction. | CO Ordinance (project-specific) | Only when zoning ordinance or CO establishes vehicle trip generation limits. Check: CO ordinance |\n| ZLU-32.41 | Group D nonconforming use expansion proposed: site has already undergone one prior expansion — LDC § 25-2-947(D) limits expansion of a Group D nonconforming use to one time only on the same lot; the proposed second expansion is not permitted. | LDC § 25-2-947(D) | Only when a Group D nonconforming use expansion is proposed and site has already undergone one prior expansion. Check: expansion history |\n| ZLU-32.42 | Vesting rights claimed or project references earlier development standards under Chapter 245 of the Texas Local Government Code: a written Vested Rights Determination letter from the City Director has not been provided per LDC § 25-1-541(D), or documentation of the vesting date, project description, and filing of a Vested Rights Petition per LDC § 25-1-533 is not included in the submittal. The governing LDC sections are §§ 25-1-531–25-1-552 (Article 12) — \"LDC 25-1-12\" is not a valid section number. | LDC §§ 25-1-533, 25-1-541 | Only when vesting rights are claimed or earlier regulations are cited. Check: facts.md or applicant statement |\n| ZLU-32.43 | Site-specific accommodation letter applies to the property (e.g., a 2009 Accommodation Letter specifying 1985 development standards): the applicable provisions are not identified on the site plan or demonstrated as complied with. | Reviewer Convention | Only when an area-specific accommodation letter, mitigation agreement, or site-specific code provision has been identified for the property. Check: Chapter 245 determination, facts.md |\n| ZLU-32.44 | Site plan does not include a note stating that all signs must comply with the requirements of LDC Chapter 25-10. | LDC Chapter 25-10 | All sites |\n| ZLU-32.45 | Site is in the University Neighborhood Overlay: signage must comply with UNO sign district regulations per LDC § 25-10-133, and this compliance is not addressed. | LDC § 25-10-133 | Only when site is in UNO. Check: zoning field in facts.md for \"UNO\" in zoning designation |\n| ZLU-32.46 | Site is on or adjacent to a designated Scenic Roadway: sign compliance with LDC § 25-10-124 Scenic Roadway sign regulations not demonstrated. | LDC § 25-10-124 | Only when site is on or adjacent to a Scenic Roadway. Check: Scenic Roadway designation in facts.md |\n| ZLU-32.47 | Site is within a Nonconforming Signs Ordinance area: all signs must comply with LDC § 25-10-152, and this requirement is not addressed on the site plan. | LDC § 25-10-152 | Only when site is within a Nonconforming Signs Ordinance area. Check: overlay or special district field in facts.md |","numLines":133,"startLine":1,"totalLines":133}}}
