{"level":30,"time":1776973745805,"pid":123,"hostname":"844f2260-bff","step":"review-runs","item":"15.md","index":6,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01TYgxZcmXBJjFYZxzsh9LGN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good! Now I have a clear picture of what sheets are available. For this setback review grouping, the most relevant sheets are likely:\n- Sheet 1 (Cover) - for submission date and general info\n- Sheet 2 (Plat) - for property boundaries and easements\n- Sheet 9 (Site Plan) - main site plan with setback lines\n- Sheet 10 (Fire Protection Plan) - may show fire access requirements\n- Sheet 21 (Electrical Design) - may show transformer locations\n- Sheet 31 (Landscape Plan) - may show buffer/screening\n\nLet me start by reading the guide.md files for Sheet 1 (Cover) and Sheet 9 (Site Plan) to understand what's on those sheets, then move to Sheet 31 (Landscape Plan) for compatibility buffer information.\n\nI need to:\n1. Check the submission date to determine if pre- or post-July 15, 2024 Article 10 applies\n2. Check zoning, compatibility trigger information\n3. Verify setback lines are shown on site plan\n4. Check building heights and distances from SF-3 triggering property\n5. Look for compatibility buffer, screening, etc.\n6. Check for transformer pads, easements, hazardous pipelines, fences, etc.\n\nLet me start with the cover sheet and site plan sheets.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":12,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3366,"cache_read_input_tokens":42727,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3366,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67c41a7e-de71-427c-8abb-98ffa8658f39","uuid":"bc8a6a6d-79b9-43df-8102-849698a2f8c6"}
{"level":30,"time":1776973746109,"pid":123,"hostname":"844f2260-bff","step":"review-runs","item":"16.md","index":2,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_0164fLiX7QmmiykvcVeMWrai","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Alright, I've read the grouping file 16.md. This is a comprehensive grouping on \"Adjacent Property Context, Compatibility Screening, and Buffering\". Let me understand the key points:\n\n**Grouping Overview:**\n- Covers documentation of adjacent property context (land uses, zoning, building footprints)\n- Covers buffering and screening requirements under Article 10 of the LDC\n- Also covers general compatibility noise/lighting controls\n- Applies conditionally based on zoning and proximity to triggering properties\n\n**Critical regulatory points:**\n1. Article 10 (§§ 25-2-1051–1063) was completely replaced effective July 15, 2024\n2. Former Article 10 sections (1064, 1066, 1067) are now repealed - all requirements consolidated in § 25-2-1063\n3. Article 10 applies only when:\n   - Site is zoned MF-4 or LESS restrictive (MF-4, MF-5, MF-6, commercial, industrial)\n   - Site is within 75 feet of a \"triggering property\"\n   - Triggering property = 1-3 dwelling units AND zoned SF-5 or more restrictive\n\n4. Key distances:\n   - 75 ft: Applicability threshold\n   - 50 ft: Height limit zone\n   - 25 ft: Standard buffer width (15 ft for narrow sites)\n   - 10 ft: Screening zone\n   - 15 ft: Refuse slab setback (Article 10); also restricted zone\n   - 20 ft: Dumpster setback (General Notes)\n\n**Compatibility Buffer requirements (§ 25-2-1062 & § 25-8-700):**\n- 25 feet wide (or 15 feet for narrow sites)\n- Must have TWO labeled zones:\n  - Screening zone (first 10 ft): Trees + understory + shrubs every 25 linear feet; NO parking/equipment\n  - Restricted zone (remaining 15 ft): May have parking, driveways, equipment\n- Must use ECM Appendix N plants, irrigated and maintained\n- Exception: Not required for condominium, townhouse, residential ≤16 units, or NO/LO/LR ≤40 ft height\n\n**Screening requirements (§ 25-2-1063):**\n- Vehicle lights, mechanical equipment, outdoor storage, refuse, amenity areas must not be visible from shared property line\n- Lighting: Must be hooded/shielded (no light source visible)\n- Noise: ≤70 dB(A)\n- Refuse slab: ≥15 feet\n\nOn-site amenities need to be positioned ≥25 feet away from the triggering property, with multi-use trails as an exception. Beyond the Article 10 scope, broader site plan standards apply universally across all sites. Lighting fixtures must be hooded and shielded when adjacent to any residential use, and nothing above the second floor can face residential areas. Mechanical equipment is capped at ≤70 dB(A) at the property line next to residential. Dumpsters require ≥20 feet from SF-5+ properties, which is more restrictive than the 15-foot slab setback. For parking, non-residential lots adjacent to residential need ≥4 feet of wall or fence screening, while residential parking with >10 spaces requires ≥6 feet.\n\nThe Waterfront Overlay applies only to properties marked with \"WO\" designation and mandates that surface parking be screened from Town Lake, the Colorado River, parkland, and named creeks. Parking structures must incorporate architectural screening with pedestrian-oriented ground-level uses. Service stations need building or landscape screening with shade trees from the street. Connectivity requirements kick in when a site is ≥3 acres of non-residential use or has parking positioned between the building and principal street. Common mistakes include referencing outdated sections like 25-2-1064, 1066, and 1067 instead of current § 25-2-1063, citing proximity to elementary schools which is no longer applicable, creating a single undifferentiated buffer instead of two distinct labeled zones, placing equipment or parking in the first 10 feet of the buffer, and conflating landscaping buffer requirements.\n\nI need to determine the project's applicability by reviewing the README and facts.md to identify the site's zoning designation, adjacent zoning, whether triggering properties are nearby, whether Article 10 compatibility applies, and any other relevant features like WO overlay or service station use.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":12,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9529,"cache_read_input_tokens":23655,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9529,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"158c3787-95dd-4091-81cc-891db6165072","uuid":"4ed5a919-9774-4291-8188-559012705833"}
