{"level":30,"time":1776973764377,"pid":123,"hostname":"844f2260-bff","step":"review-runs","item":"32.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01UwPkVsvU4e3vqKZTgCAoh6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the grouping file (32.md) and the project context. Let me understand what I need to do:\n\n1. **Grouping:** ZLU-32 - Zoning Overlay Programs, Compatibility Standards, and Special Use Requirements\n2. **Project:** Valley View Townhomes, MF-2 zoned, 39 units\n3. **Key Facts from facts.md:**\n   - Zoning: MF-2 (Multi-Family Residence)\n   - Adjacent SF-3 parcel triggers LDC Subchapter F compatibility standards\n   - Within 25 ft of SF-3: max 2 stories / 30 ft height, step-up schedule applies\n   - Nonconforming Signs Area: Yes\n   - Residential Design Standards (Subchapter F): Active overlay\n   - No CBD/DMU designation\n   - No DDBP, UNO, TOD, WO, ETOD, PUD, MUD, ERC designations mentioned\n   - Not in group home proximity area (§ 25-2-841 repealed anyway)\n\nThis is a critical finding. The grouping covers many overlay programs and special requirements, but most don't apply to this project. Let me systematically evaluate applicability:\n\n**Not Applicable (NPA) Items:**\n- ZLU-32.1 – 32.10: DDBP items (site not in CBD) → N/A\n- ZLU-32.11: DMU setback (site not in DMU) → N/A\n- ZLU-32.12: TOD Live/Work/Flex (no TOD designation) → N/A\n- ZLU-32.13: UNO notation (no UNO designation) → N/A\n- ZLU-32.14: TOD subdistrict (no TOD designation) → N/A\n- ZLU-32.15: WO use provisions (no WO designation) → N/A\n\n\n- ZLU-32.16 through 32.19: ERC, corridor plans, PUD/MUD, and CS-MU-V zoning all absent from this site → N/A\n- ZLU-32.25: Community Recreation use designation doesn't apply → N/A\n- ZLU-32.30: ETOD requirements not triggered → N/A\n- ZLU-32.31: Townhouse provisions need evaluation\n\n. The site plan indicates \"39 multi-family townhome units,\" so I need to determine whether this constitutes a townhouse use under § 25-2-775 or is simply townhome-style architecture.\n- ZLU-32.32 through 32.34: ADU accessory to civic use, UNO multifamily accessibility, and condominium use all inapplicable → N/A\n- ZLU-32.41 through 32.42: Group D nonconforming use and vesting rights not claimed → N/A\n- ZLU-32.45: UNO signage requirements absent → N/A\n\n**Potentially Applicable Items:**\n- ZLU-32.20: South Lamar NPA is suspended, so no active neighborhood plan → N/A\n- ZLU-32.21: Article 10 Compatibility Standards requires clarification. The site is MF-2 adjacent to SF-3, and facts.md references \"Compatibility Standards: LDC Subchapter F triggered by adjacent SF-3 parcel.\" I need to determine whether this refers to Article 10 (§§ 25-2-1051–25-2-1063) or Subchapter F (Residential Design Standards).\n- ZLU-32.22 and 32.24: Compatibility notes and on-site amenity requirements depend on resolving the Article 10 question.\n- ZLU-32.23: GO/LR/CS/CS-1 exterior lighting doesn't apply to this zone → N/A\n- ZLU-32.26: Outdoor noise standards need review.\n- ZLU-32.27: VMU building requirements don't apply here → N/A\n- ZLU-32.28: Rain garden within 25 feet of triggering property depends on Article 10 applicability and whether one is proposed.\n- ZLU-32.29: Article 10 exception documentation depends on Article 10 applicability.\n- ZLU-32.35: Multifamily transit proximity under § 25-1-703 applies to this multifamily residential project.\n- ZLU-32.36: Tenant notification for demolition/displacement applies if displacement occurs.\n- ZLU-32.38 through 32.40: Redevelopment exception, trip generation limits, and site-specific accommodation letter need verification.\n- ZLU-32.43 and 32.44: General sign compliance under § 25-10 applies to all sites.\n- ZLU-32.46: Scenic Roadway signs don't apply based on the facts.\n- ZLU-32.47: Nonconforming Signs Ordinance applies per facts.md.\n\nThere's a key distinction between Article 10 (§§ 25-2-1051–25-2-1063, effective July 15, 2024) and Subchapter F. The facts indicate Subchapter F is triggered by the adjacent SF-3 parcel, imposing a 2-story/30-foot height limit within 25 feet of that lot line with a step-up schedule beyond that distance. This is separate from the Article 10 compatibility standards being evaluated in this grouping.\n\nArticle 10 applies only to sites zoned MF-4 or less restrictive (meaning MF-5, MF-6, or commercial/industrial zones) within 75 feet of a triggering property. Since the subject property is zoned MF-2, which is more restrictive than MF-4, Article 10 may not actually apply here.\n\nI need to clarify the zoning hierarchy. In Austin's system, MF-1 allows the highest density while MF-6 is the most restrictive. When the regulation says \"MF-4 or less restrictive,\" it's referring to zones that permit greater density—so MF-1, MF-2, and MF-3 would fall into that category, not the higher-numbered zones.\n\nSince the subject property is zoned MF-2, Article 10 would apply if it sits within 75 feet of a triggering property. A triggering property must have 1-3 dwelling units and be zoned SF-5 or more restrictive. The adjacent SF-3 parcel doesn't meet this definition because SF-3 is less restrictive than SF-5, so it wouldn't qualify as a triggering property under Article 10's specific requirements.\n\nHowever, facts.md references \"Subchapter F\" compatibility standards for this same SF-3 neighbor. This suggests Subchapter F may be the predecessor standard—the original Article 10 that was completely repealed and replaced on July 15, 2024. The current regulatory framework appears to be the post-2024 version, which could explain why the terminology and triggering criteria differ from what's documented in the facts. old compatibility standards that predate the July 2024 update. The standards in facts.md—2 stories / 30 ft within 25 ft with a step-up schedule—don't match the current Article 10 limits of 40 ft within 50 ft, 60 ft at 50–75 ft. This suggests the property's constraints come from an earlier version of the code, not the current ordinance the grouping is analyzing.\n\nSince the site plan was submitted in May 2025, well after the July 15, 2024 effective date, I need to determine whether Subchapter F is simply the new designation for Article 10 or if it's a distinct requirement running parallel to it. The site plan documents themselves should clarify which standards actually govern this project. I'll examine the cover sheet and zoning data table to see what compatibility standards the applicant is working under.\n\nMy approach will be to review the cover sheet and supplementary materials first, then systematically check each relevant section—looking at the zoning data table for compatibility notes, checking sheets for signage compliance and nonconforming sign areas, verifying multifamily requirements like transit proximity and tenant notification, and confirming whether any redevelopment exceptions or trip generation accommodations are claimed.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7955,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11183,"cache_read_input_tokens":23655,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11183,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"38ff40a3-baa1-4588-8a44-429884d301ed","uuid":"e8db1178-63d9-4968-8f72-bbdb34da41ce"}
