{"level":30,"time":1776973850625,"pid":123,"hostname":"844f2260-bff","step":"review-runs","item":"32.md","index":7,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NCjQ5vxJCUXoTAMe6qUGAZ","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Drainage Engineering Review\n     2→\n     3→*Reviews the project's storm water management, drainage easements, retaining walls, dam safety, and DCM waiver requirements.*\n     4→\n     5→DE 1 – Current Status: Cleared\n     6→* U1: Comment cleared.\n     7→* U0: If impervious cover is being claimed for existing drainage calculations, please demonstrate that this impervious cover was constructed prior to 1977 or has been permitted prior to this application.\n     8→\n     9→DE 2 – Current Status: Cleared\n    10→* U1: Comment cleared.\n    11→* U0: Show location of all existing drainage structures on or adjacent to the project site on all drainage sheets.\n    12→\n    13→DE 3 – Current Status: Pending\n    14→* U1: Comment cleared.\n    15→* U0: Clearly show and label all existing and proposed drainage easements on drainage layout sheet as per final plat or by separate instrument. Please provide the drainage easement document for the area circled in blue with the next submittal for this drainage easement. Our GIS system only shows the drainage easement north of that.\n    16→\n    17→[Figure: Aerial map showing an existing drainage easement highlighted in blue]\n    18→\n    19→[Figure: Left: Site plan snippet showing a proposed drainage pipe (18\" RCP) and easements. Right: Aerial map highlighting multiple overlapping drainage and wastewater easements]\n    20→\n    21→DE 4 – Current Status: Pending\n    22→* U1: Waiver has been sent to Watershed Protection Department for review.\n    23→* U0: The detention basin shall have a minimum fifteen (15) foot setback from any property line adjacent to a residential development. Please demonstrate compliance.\n    24→DCM 1.2.4(E)(4)(a)\n    25→\n    26→DE 5 – Current Status: Cleared\n    27→* U1: Comment cleared.\n    28→* U0: Please show the direction of flows from building roofs/gutters.\n    29→\n    30→DE 6 – Current Status: Pending\n    31→* U1: The drainage area outline is difficult to differentiate from the proposed contours. Please ensure drainage area maps are legible.\n    32→* U0: The existing contours are thick and dark, making it difficult to see the proposed contours. Please make the existing contour linework thinner in the proposed drainage area map.\n    33→\n    34→DE 7 – Current Status: Cleared\n    35→* U1: Comment cleared.\n    36→* U0: Free standing dry stacked rock walls are not acceptable in any SCM. Grouted rock walls are acceptable only if the design includes an impermeable barrier such as an approved geomembrane liner or reinforced concrete retaining wall.\n    37→ECM 1.6.3. B. 7.\n    38→\n    39→DE 8 – Current Status: Pending\n    40→* U1: Waiver letter has been sent to Watershed Protection Department for review. Please note if waiver is denied, you will be required to provide a 12-foot ramp.\n    41→* U0: Demonstrate compliance with all applicable maintenance and construction requirements outlined in the Drainage Criteria Manual. The 5ft maintenance ramp does not comply with DCM 1.2.4.e.i or DCM 1.2.4e. iv.\n    42→Reference: DCM 1.2.4(E)\n    43→\n    44→DE 9 – Current Status: Pending\n    45→* U1: Comment remains, the 25-foot drainage easement is located on your site plans property. You are required to demonstrate adequate conveyance of the offsite flow going through your property. You also cannot have a retaining wall within a drainage easement unless it has been approved. If you need to provide the reference sheets, please do so. \n    46→* U0: Demonstrate that there is adequate conveyance for the offsite flow. Provide plan/profile sheets for all channels in ROW or public drainage easements to demonstrate compliance with DCM Section 6. Profiles should clearly identify:\n    47→  - 25- and 100-year depths of flows, velocities, flowrates for each segment of the channel\n    48→  - 25- and 100-year HGLs\n    49→  - Beginning and end of construction stations (proceeding from downstream to upstream)\n    50→  - Beginning and end stations for permanent erosion control material, if used\n    51→\n    52→DE 10 – Current Status: Pending\n    53→* U1: Comment remains.\n    54→* U0: Provide cross sections for all segments of all channels in ROW or public drainage easements. Cross sections should clearly identify the following:\n    55→  - Bottom width\n    56→  - Side slopes\n    57→  - Concrete pilot channel (if used)\n    58→  - Height of channel lining (if used)\n    59→  - Maximum and minimum depth of channel\n    60→  - Manning's \"n\" values\n    61→Reference: DCM Section 6; DCM Table 6-2\n    62→\n    63→DE 11 – Current Status: Pending\n    64→* U1: Comment remains.\n    65→* U0: No public storm drain system should be allowed to go through an inlet box. However, a stub out from one or two lots in a commercial subdivision can be tied into an existing inlet if it does not impede the function of the inlet and if the laterals and overflow provisions contain the capacity to convey the additional flows. Please demonstrate compliance.\n    66→Reference: DCM 5.2.0(H)\n    67→\n    68→DE 12 – Current Status: Pending\n    69→* U1: Comment pending. We still need an analysis of the existing channel on the site. If you are planning to discharge to it, we will need to ensure it has capacity. Please note that your outfall is within an electrical easement. You will need to provide approval from the easements’ owner to do this. Please note the intent of this code is also to cause no adverse impact to your neighbors.\n    70→* U0: If the development is located within 550 feet of an existing storm system, the developer shall provide all the necessary infrastructure required to connect to that system. If there is no existing storm sewer, the outlet design shall provide for a change in the discharge pattern from concentrated flow back to sheet flow.\n    71→Reference: DCM 1.2.3(C)\n    72→\n    73→DE 13 – Current Status: Pending\n    74→* U1: Provide additional bottom of wall, or toe of bank elevations to verify the pond does not exceed 6’. For instance, the red X below appears to have a 6 ft difference.\n    75→\n    76→[Figure: Cross-section detail of a detention pond structure showing a wall with TW 677.50, FG 672.00, and a 6\" PVC discharge pipe at 1.00% slope onto a 3' x 4' concrete splash pad]\n    77→\n    78→* U0: Any hydraulic structure designed to impound storm water that has a height greater than 6 feet at any point along the perimeter of the SWM pond is a dam. The height of the hydraulic structure (dam) is measured from the top of the structure to the downstream intersection of the structure and the natural or excavated ground, whichever is lower. Provide additional top of wall, bottom of wall, or toe of bank elevations to verify the pond does not exceed 6’.\n    79→Reference: DCM 8.3.3(B)\n    80→\n    81→DE 14 – Current Status: Pending\n    82→* U1: Comment pending more verification.\n    83→* U0: Dams must be designed to pass the minimum design flood hydrograph which is the greater of:\n    84→  a) 75% of the PMF; or\n    85→  b) The percentage of the PMF as defined in Texas Administrative Code Chapter 299 Dams and Reservoirs (Figure: 30 TAC § 299.15(a)(1)(A)).\n    86→Reference: DCM 8.3.3(B)(3)\n    87→\n    88→DE 15 – Current Status: Pending\n    89→* U1: Comment pending more verification.\n    90→* U0: Adequate design of the dam is evidenced by certification using the statement provided in DCM 8.3.3.B.3 by an engineer licensed in the State of Texas. The certification statement may be divided into the four disciplines of hydrology, hydraulics, structural and geotechnical and independently certified. Please add the following Dam Safety Certification Statement to the cover sheet: “I [name of professional engineer] Texas license number [number] certify that the design of the dam in this set of plans can safely pass the minimum design flood hydrograph as required by the City of Austin and the State of Texas based on the hydrologic, hydraulic, structural and geotechnical analysis using standard accepted engineering practices.”\n    91→Reference: DCM 8.3.3(B)\n    92→\n    93→DE 16 – Current Status: Pending\n    94→* U1: Comment pending more verification.\n    95→* U0: SWM ponds that are considered dams as defined in the Drainage Criteria Manual may not be designed or constructed with any trees or other woody vegetation on the dam structure or within 20-feet of the upstream or downstream toe of the dam. This 20-foot clear zone must be called out on the site plan and for City maintained facilities must be part of the drainage easement dedicated for the dam facility. The toe of the dam is the junction of the constructed dam structure with the natural ground. Please Demonstrate compliance.\n    96→Reference: DCM 8.3.3(B)(5)\n    97→\n    98→DE 17 – Current Status: Cleared\n    99→* U1: Comment cleared.\n   100→* U0: Provide details of the detention pond outlet structure. Please show the elevations for the 2-, 10-, 25-, and 100-year storm events in the detention outlet structure detail.\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c1ccbdc1-20f6-4e62-a4c2-17685db82f6f","uuid":"fab16640-223f-47c4-8868-33d55fc8c9d4","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/supplementary-docs/master-comment-report/04-drainage-engineering-review.md","content":"# Drainage Engineering Re