ing wall but appears incomplete.\n\nThe retaining walls may satisfy the requirement if they're documented as 6+ feet tall and effectively shield the parking areas from adjacent residential properties. Without confirmed heights, the status remains uncertain. ZLU-16.31 and ZLU-16.32 don't apply since there's no Waterfront Overlay designation. Moving to ZLU-16.33 regarding service stations.\n\nThe proposed use is residential townhomes, not a service station, so ZLU-16.33 is inapplicable. For exterior lighting requirements, the site plan explicitly notes full cut-off, fully shielded fixtures complying with Subchapter E 2.5, which satisfies ZLU-16.34. ZLU-16.35 addresses lighting above the second floor near residential properties, and since adjacent parcels are zoned for multifamily residential uses, this requirement applies and needs verification against the site plan specifications.\n\nHowever, no photometric or lighting plan showing fixture heights and orientations was provided, making it impossible to confirm that no lighting above the second floor faces the adjacent residential properties. For ZLU-16.36, the site plan references Subchapter E 2.5 rather than repealed Article 10 sections, so there's no evidence of citing outdated standards. ZLU-16.37 concerns mechanical equipment noise demonstration.\n\nMechanical equipment must meet 70 dB(A) standards when adjacent to residential uses, which applies here since neighboring properties are residential. A mechanical equipment schedule with noise specifications would be needed to verify compliance, but none appears to have been submitted. For ZLU-16.1, while the east side correctly identifies Chateaux Ridge Condominiums as an existing apartment building and the west shows Valley View Road, the north and south adjacent uses lack proper identification—the north shows only zoning designation (MF-4-CO) without describing the actual land use, and the south lists an owner name without clarifying whether the property is residential, office, or vacant.\n\nThe site plan also fails to document properties across Valley View Road to the west, which is required under ZLU-16.2. Zoning labels are missing for the south and west (across ROW) properties per ZLU-16.4, and no boundary lines distinguish between the multiple zoning districts that border the site. Additionally, the plan lacks notes indicating that no buildings exist within 50 feet on the north and south sides, as required by ZLU-16.10.\n\nFor ZLU-16.13, I need to verify whether parking sits between the building and principal street—if so, connections to adjacent lots should be shown but aren't. The plan also provides no confirmation of Article 10 applicability analysis. Regarding ZLU-16.30, while retaining walls are shown where parking abuts the residential district, their heights aren't documented, making compliance unverifiable. The lighting plan is absent entirely, so ZLU-16.35 cannot be verified, and mechanical equipment noise requirements under ZLU-16.37 haven't been addressed.\n\nI should also examine whether mylars were submitted and what they contain, since zoning information is typically required on mylars per ZLU-16.8. Without reviewing the actual mylar sheets, I can't confirm whether zoning is properly documented there. This is a critical gap that needs clarification before finalizing the assessment.\n\nSeveral items are failing requirements: adjacent land uses lack documentation for the North and South properties, and land uses across Valley View Road aren't shown. The South and West zoning designations are missing from the site plan. Zoning boundary lines where multiple districts meet the site aren't delineated, and the required notation about no buildings within 50 feet on adjoining lots is absent for North and South. Article 10 applicability also hasn't been confirmed on the plan. The parking screening wall details cannot be verified without additional documentation, and I'm unable to confirm zoning accuracy against the City GIS system.\n\nFor the remaining items, there's no lighting plan to verify fixture locations don't exceed second-floor height facing residential areas, and no mechanical equipment schedule with noise ratings has been provided. The parking screening requirement needs clarification—retaining walls are shown along the south boundary where parking exists, but their heights aren't documented on the main site plan. These details might be included on the separate wall plan sheets, which I should review to determine if the 6-foot screening height requirement is actually met.\n\nRegarding property connections, Valley View Road functions as the principal street on the west side. Since parking is positioned between the buildings and this principal street, the code requires cross-access connections to adjacent parcels. The site plan shows no pedestrian or vehicular connection features to neighboring properties, which appears to be a code violation.\n\nNow compiling the findings: Adjacent land uses lack documentation in all directions—the north boundary only shows a zoning label while the south shows just a property name without specifying the actual use type. The west side across Valley View Road's right-of-way also doesn't indicate adjacent land uses. Similarly, zoning designations for the south and west properties aren't shown despite multiple districts bordering the site. Zoning district boundary lines themselves aren't drawn or labeled on the plan. The accuracy of adjacent zoning information can't be confirmed without access to current City GIS records.\n\nFor the building setback notation, there's no statement indicating whether buildings exist on adjoining lots within 50 feet of the north and south boundaries. Property connections to adjacent parcels aren't identified, and the parking area sits between the building and Valley View Road rather than behind it. The site plan doesn't confirm Article 10 compatibility applicability, though this may not actually apply since the zoning is MF-2. Parking area screening heights aren't documented—they might appear on detail sheets but need verification against the 6-foot minimum. No lighting plan exists to verify that exterior lighting above the second floor doesn't face residential areas. Additionally, there's no mechanical equipment schedule showing noise ratings at the property line.\n\nReconsidering ZLU-16.14, I need to check the applicability trigger more carefully. The rule only applies when the site is adjacent to SF-5 or more restrictive zoning or when any Article 10 checklist item is triggered. I should verify the site's zoning designation and what zones border it to determine if this standard actually applies. not apply because site is zoned MF-2.\" I think the applicant should have documented this analysis, especially since an SF-3 property is adjacent and would otherwise trigger the requirement.\n\nThe site plan lacks explicit notation of the Article 10 applicability determination, so this item fails. Moving through the remaining checklist items, I need to verify I haven't overlooked anything. ZLU-16.3 addresses vacant adjoining properties—the grouping applies to all sites, and there's no evidence of unlabeled vacant properties here. ZLU-16.5 concerns adjacent zoning design standards.\n\nThe adjacent zoning shown on the site plan (MF-2-CO to the east, MF-4-CO to the north) doesn't fully align with what facts.md lists (SF-3, GO, MF-3, MF-3-CO, MF-2-CO). The northern MF-4-CO designation appears nowhere in the documented adjacent properties, though a zoning case reference might explain multiple properties. Without access to current GIS data, I can't confirm whether the site plan's zoning notations are accurate. Similarly, ZLU-16.8 requires zoning designations on mylars, but I haven't reviewed those documents, so this also cannot be verified. to all sites. The requirement is that zoning designations appear on the mylars themselves. I haven't located mylars in the submission materials—the supplementary documents listed in the README don't include them. Since mylars are typically signed and sealed by the engineer and represent the official submitted plans, their absence means I can't verify this requirement.\n\nI'm marking **ZLU-16.8: FAIL** because the mylars haven't been provided in the documents I've reviewed. Without them, I can't confirm whether zoning designations are properly included and consistent with the site plan sheets. -VERIFIABLE** since I haven't confirmed whether mylars were actually submitted with the project documents.\n\nLooking at what I've reviewed so far, several key documents are missing or unclear: mylars haven't been clearly identified in the submission, the photometric report and lighting plan are absent, and the mechanical equipment schedule wasn't provided. These gaps mean I can't fully verify certain requirements. I should note which findings depend on these missing documents as I compile my assessment.\n\nMy findings break down as follows: ZLU-16.1 and 16.2 fail because adjacent land uses aren't documented in all directions or across streets. ZLU-16.4 fails since South and West zoning isn't shown. ZLU-16.6 fails because zoning district boundaries lack labels. ZLU-16.10 fails—there's no note about the 50-foot building setback. ZLU-16.13 fails as property connections to adjacent lots aren't identified. ZLU-16.14 fails because Article 10 compatibility isn't confirmed on the site plan. For items I can't verify: ZLU-16.5 can't be checked against GIS data, ZLU-16.8 depends on mylars not being clearly provided, ZLU-16.30 lacks wall and fence height documentation, ZLU-16.35 has no lighting plan to review upper-floor lighting, and ZLU-16.37 has no mechanical schedule with noise specifications.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":47647,"cache_read_input_tokens":19314,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":47647,"ephemeral_