{"level":30,"time":1776974189496,"pid":123,"hostname":"844f2260-bff","step":"review-runs","item":"15.md","index":6,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_012AnFppmrZqpSYL8w9Kgq5G","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good. So the submittal date is May 20, 2025, which is AFTER July 15, 2024. So the NEW Article 10 applies (§ 25-2-1051 onwards).\n\nHowever, the cover sheet does NOT cite any Article 10 compatibility standards at all. That's a potential deficiency - if Article 10 applies (due to adjacent SF-3 property within 75 ft), the plan should show the applicable compatibility standards, buffer lines, height limits, etc.\n\nLet me now think about what I need to evaluate from the checklist:\n\nThe key items are:\n- ZLU-15.1 - Required setback lines not shown/labeled/dimensioned\n- ZLU-15.2 - Zoning setbacks not maintained\n- ZLU-15.7 - Compatibility applicability incorrectly determined\n- ZLU-15.8 - Height limits not shown/maintained\n- ZLU-15.9 - Triggering property not located on exhibit\n- ZLU-15.10 - Buffer not shown at correct width\n- ZLU-15.11 - Buffer line mislabeled or undimensioned\n- ZLU-15.12 - Buffer applied where not triggering\n- ZLU-15.13 - Triggering property not clearly identified\n- ZLU-15.14 - Property line not prominent/screening not shown\n- ZLU-15.15 - Prohibited improvements in screening zone\n- ZLU-15.26 - Separate compatibility exhibit not provided\n- ZLU-15.27 - Compatibility cross-sections not provided\n- ZLU-15.40 - Transformer clearances not maintained\n- ZLU-15.46 - Fence height issues\n- And many others\n\nLet me think about the research phase. What have I found so far?\n\nFROM FACTS.MD:\n- Site: MF-2 zoned\n\n\n- Adjacent SF-3 property at 0406100210 with compatibility standards triggered within 25 ft of that lot line: maximum 2 stories / 30 ft height\n\nFROM VISION ANALYSIS OF SHEET 9 (Site Plan):\n- Property lines marked with heavy solid lines and bearing/distance measurements\n- Setback distances shown (e.g., \"25.0'\", \"21.3'\", \"44.2'\", \"46.9'\") with dimension lines\n- But no explicit \"Setback Line\" or \"Building Setback\" labels\n- No compatibility buffer lines or \"no-build\" zones indicated\n- Adjacent SF-3 zoning labeled on plan\n- Buildings 6, 7, 8 positioned closest to southern SF-3 boundary, with Building 6 at only 21.3' and Building 8 at 46.9' from that line\n\nFROM VISION ANALYSIS OF SHEET 31 (Landscape Plan):\n- No compatibility buffer lines, screening zones, or buffer width notations visible\n- Vegetation density doesn't show distinct treatment at SF-3 boundary\n- Driveway and parking area positioned near southern boundary with minimal landscape strip and wooden fence only\n\nFROM VISION ANALYSIS OF SHEET 21 (Electrical):\n- Four transformer pads present with no clearance dimensions shown around them\n\n, no clearance labels specified, and no notation indicating which clearance standard applies\n\nFROM VISION ANALYSIS OF SHEET 1 (Cover):\n- Submittal dated 5/20/2025, which means the new Article 10 compatibility standards apply\n- No references to Article 10, § 25-2-1051, or any compatibility-related sections\n- No waivers or variances documented for compatibility issues\n\nThe plans lack a separate compatibility exhibit, cross-sections showing compatibility treatment, labeling of the triggering SF-3 property, dimensioned buffers, screening zone notations, and any Article 10 references on the cover sheet. Since the site is zoned MF-2 with SF-3 property within 75 feet, Article 10 compatibility requirements are triggered and should be addressed. parking, driveways, mechanical equipment, refuse receptacles (≥ 15 ft setback), and amenities (≥ 25 ft setback). Lighting and noise restrictions also apply.\n\nLooking at what's actually shown on the plans, the compatibility buffer isn't depicted, height limit zones lack labels, and there's no separate compatibility exhibit or cross-sections demonstrating the height restrictions at different distance bands. The SF-3 zoning appears on the site plan, though the triggering property itself isn't specifically identified.\n\nExamining the building heights from the sheets, Building 3 reaches 36'-1\" as the tallest structure. Both Building 6 (21.3' from the SF-3 line) and Building 8 (46.9' from the SF-3 line) fall within the 50-foot zone, meaning they're subject to the 40-foot maximum height limit.\n\nThe buildings appear to stay within the 33-36' range, which would satisfy the 40-foot requirement. However, the real issue is whether Article 10 compliance measures are actually demonstrated on the plans. Since the site qualifies as MF-2 zoning within 75 feet of an SF-3 property, the compatibility standards should apply. The plans don't show the required 25-foot landscaped buffer along the shared property line, nor do they demonstrate the compatibility framework that should govern setbacks, screening, and other protective measures.\n\nAdditionally, while the SF-3 zoning is labeled on the site plan, the specific triggering parcel and its dwelling unit count aren't identified. The set appears to lack both a dedicated compatibility exhibit sheet and the required cross-sections showing height transitions at the 50-foot and 75-foot distance bands. These missing exhibits represent significant documentation gaps in demonstrating compliance with the compatibility standards.\n\nFor the transformer pad clearances, Sheet 21 shows no dimensions or labels indicating which clearance standard applies. The setback lines themselves have dimension annotations but lack explicit labeling as \"Setback Lines,\" creating some ambiguity about whether the requirement is fully satisfied. Looking at what's actually missing versus what's just unclear, the compatibility buffer lines are definitively absent from both the plans and landscape design, and the separate compatibility exhibit sheet doesn't appear in the sheet list at all.\n\nThe cross-sections for compatibility analysis aren't visible on the available plans. For the triggering property, I can't clearly determine the dwelling unit count from what's provided. Height limit zones aren't shown on the plans, so I can't verify compliance with the 30-foot maximum within 25 feet of the SF-3 lot line. Let me reconsider the project facts to ensure I'm applying the right standards—the adjacent SF-3 parcel triggers Subchapter F compatibility requirements, which means the two-story/30-foot height limit applies in that buffer zone.\n\nLooking at the regulatory framework more carefully, there's a distinction between the old Subchapter F standards and the new Article 10 framework that became effective July 15, 2024. The project was submitted May 20, 2025, well after that effective date, so the new Article 10 standards should apply rather than the old framework referenced in the project facts.\n\nNow I need to systematically evaluate each checklist item against the actual requirements. For ZLU-15.1, which applies to all sites, I'm checking whether the required zoning district setback lines are properly shown and labeled on the site plan. The code requires front yard, rear yard, interior side, and street side setback lines for the MF-2 district. While dimension lines with distances appear on the plan, I need to verify whether the actual setback lines themselves are explicitly labeled as such.\n\nThe vision analysis indicates dashed lines running parallel to the property boundaries, with dimensions provided at key setback locations. Even though there's no explicit \"Setback Line\" label, the combination of visible lines and dimensioned distances likely satisfies the requirement's intent—the checklist item flags cases where setback indicators are completely absent. Given that both the lines and dimensions are present, this appears to be a pass.\n\nFor ZLU-15.2, I'm now evaluating whether the zoning district setback distances are actually being maintained across the site.\n\nThe applicability is straightforward—this applies to all sites. The code references require buildings to stay within required front, rear, interior side, and street side setbacks according to the MF-2 zoning table. I can see four setback distances marked on the plan (25.0', 21.3', 44.2', 46.9'), but the critical issue is that I don't have the actual required MF-2 setback values from § 25-2-492 to compare against. Without knowing what the code mandates for this zone, I can't determine if these measurements represent compliance or violations. The grouping file doesn't include the regulatory table, so this needs to be marked as not-verifiable unless I locate the setback requirements elsewhere.\n\nFor ZLU-15.3, the shoreline setback rule only applies to properties with frontage on Lady Bird Lake, Lake Austin, or LA zoning—none of which apply here since the site is on Valley View Road. ZLU-15.4 addresses through lots fronting two parallel streets; the site description mentions being \"between Valley View Road and Clawson Road,\" so I need to verify whether it actually fronts both streets. ZLU-15.5 requires checking if proposed dumpster enclosures meet setback requirements—the plans show trash containers, but I need to determine if there's a formal dumpster enclosure and its location. ZLU-15.6 applies when fences are proposed near setback boundaries; the site plan shows wooden privacy fencing that needs verification against setback lines. For ZLU-15.7, the compatibility standards apply since the site is MF-2 zoned and an adjacent SF-3 property exists within 75 feet, which is correctly labeled on the plans, though I need to confirm whether the applicability determination itself is complete.\n\nThe critical issue with ZLU-15.7 is that Article 10 compatibility requirements aren't documented or referenced anywhere on the plans—there's no analysis of the 75-foot proximity test or verification that the SF-3 property meets the triggering criteria. For ZLU-15.8, since compatibility is triggered, the height-distance zones should be shown: under 50 feet requires 40-foot maximum, 50-74 feet requires 60-foot maximum, and 75+ feet allows base zoning. Buildings 6 and 8 are positioned within the 50-foot zone at 21.3 and 46.9 feet respectively, with heights of 35'-10.75\" and 33'-4.75\" that comply with the 40-foot limit, but the plans don't visually display these height-limit zones or demonstrate the compliance analysis.\n\nZLU-15.9 requires a compatibility exhibit showing existing structures on the triggering SF-3 property, which is completely absent from the submission. The compatibility buffer requirement under ZLU-15.10 is also unaddressed—no buffer lines appear on either the site or landscape plans, and the required 25-foot width isn't shown or documented.\n\nSince the buffer itself is missing, ZLU-15.11's labeling and dimensioning requirements become moot, and ZLU-15.12 doesn't apply because there's no buffer present to be incorrectly applied. The triggering property identification under ZLU-15.13 remains incomplete.\n\nFor ZLU-15.13, the SF-3 zoning appears on the site plan, but the parcel number and dwelling unit count aren't clearly documented, making this NOT-VERIFIABLE. Property lines are shown prominently with heavy solid lines and bearing/distance annotations per ZLU-15.14, though screening vegetation documentation is unclear. The 10-ft screening zone restrictions on parking, driveways, and structures need verification against what's actually depicted on the plans.\n\nThe driveway runs parallel to the southern property line with a landscape buffer between them, but without exact dimensions I can't confirm whether it complies with the 10-ft setback requirement—NOT-VERIFIABLE. A dedicated compatibility exhibit sheet is missing from the 39-sheet set, which is a clear FAIL. Similarly, cross-section drawings showing height-distance relationships to the SF-3 property aren't present, also a FAIL.\n\nFor the transformer pads on Sheet 21, there are four units positioned near residential buildings and drive aisles, but no clearance dimensions or labels appear on the plans. Austin Energy Design Criteria 1.10.4 specifies various clearance distances depending on context—10-ft to hot-stick sides, 5-ft to masonry or 12-ft to non-masonry buildings, 15-ft in confined spaces—yet without knowing the building types or having any dimensions shown, I cannot verify compliance. This is a FAIL. The fence heights also appear to exceed what's permitted under LDC § 25-2-899.\n\nThe wooden privacy fence shown on site is subject to a 6-ft maximum height standard, with allowances up to 7-ft for grade changes or 8-ft under specific conditions. Since the plans don't label fence heights, I cannot determine if they meet code requirements—this is either NOT-VERIFIABLE or FAIL depending on whether dimensions should be shown. Site-specific fence requirements don't apply here since the property isn't in an NCCD, PUD, or subject to a named ordinance, so that item is N/A.\n\nThe major deficiencies cluster around missing compatibility documentation and inadequate transformer clearance details. The compatibility buffer, exhibit, and cross-sections are all absent from the submittal, and the transformer pad clearances lack the dimensional verification needed for code compliance.\n\nFor ZLU-15.7, I need to determine whether the applicant incorrectly assessed whether Article 10 compatibility standards apply. The plans don't reference Article 10 at all, but that absence doesn't automatically mean they made an incorrect determination—they may have simply concluded it doesn't apply and omitted the citation. Without explicit evidence showing they evaluated and rejected applicability, I can't definitively call this a failure; it's more likely NOT-VERIFIABLE unless the plans should have documented their compatibility analysis. The distinction matters here—a FAIL means the plans explicitly state an incorrect determination, while NOT-VERIFIABLE means I can't confirm whether they've properly evaluated it. Since the plans don't clearly document their Article 10 applicability analysis, I can't verify the determination was done correctly, but I also can't prove it was done wrong.\n\nLooking at this more carefully, the real question is whether the required compatibility elements actually appear on the plans themselves. If Article 10 applies, specific items need to be shown: the compatibility buffer and height limits per the standards, identification of the triggering property, a separate compatibility exhibit, cross-sections demonstrating height-distance relationships, and screening/lighting/noise requirements. The absence of these elements on the plans is what matters for the checklist.\n\nThe core findings should focus on what's missing: the compatibility buffer isn't shown, the compatibility exhibit isn't provided, and the compatibility cross-sections aren't included. There's also the transformer clearance dimensioning issue to consider.\n\nBefore finalizing, I need to verify whether Article 10 actually applies by checking the applicability criteria—specifically confirming the site zoning and whether any SF-5-or-more-restrictive parcels with 1–3 dwelling units exist within 75 feet of the site boundary.\n\nThe site is MF-2, which satisfies the first requirement. The adjacent property is SF-3, and reviewing the regulatory overview confirms SF-3 is indeed one of the triggering zones. So Article 10 does apply, meaning these requirements should be on the plans but aren't.\n\nFor findings, the clear violations are: the compatibility buffer isn't shown at the required width along the SF-3 boundary, no separate compatibility exhibit was provided, and the cross-sections documenting height-distance relationships are missing. Additionally, transformer pad clearances lack dimensioning—no clearance circles or notes appear on the drawings.\n\nI should also consider whether to flag setback line labeling, height limit documentation with distance-based zones, identification of triggering property structures, and whether prohibited items in the screening zone can be verified. Before finalizing any findings though, I need to cross-reference what couldn't be verified in my earlier analysis to ensure I'm being thorough and not missing anything. all structures, parking, driveways, and mechanical equipment must stay out of the 10-ft screening zone adjacent to the SF-3 boundary. The applicant needs to provide a compatibility exhibit showing property lines, zoning designations, and distances to the triggering property, plus cross-sections demonstrating how building heights comply with the distance-based limits at the 50-ft and 75-ft thresholds. The triggering property itself needs clear identification including its zoning and unit count.\n\nLooking at the site plan, the property line is clearly marked and the adjacent SF-3 zoning is labeled, with setback dimensions provided. However, the plan is missing the 25-ft landscaped buffer delineation, no height restriction zones are shown at the required distances, and there's no documentation of how many dwelling units exist on the triggering property.\n\nThe landscape plan shows planting areas and the property boundary but lacks any notation of the 10-ft or 25-ft screening zones, doesn't clearly distinguish vegetation density in the buffer area, and doesn't dimension the parking and driveway distances from the SF-3 line. The electrical plan identifies four transformer locations but provides no clearance dimensions or distance specifications from the property line. Across the entire 39-sheet plan set, there's no separate compatibility exhibit, no cross-section sheets, no height limit zone labeling, and no transformer clearance specifications. The cover sheet contains no Article 10 applicability determination or citation.\n\nFor the setback requirements, the site plan shows dimensioned clearances (25.0', 21.3', 44.2', 46.9') from buildings to property lines, though I can't verify whether these satisfy the MF-2 minimums without the setback table from § 25-2-492. The dashed lines parallel to boundaries are present, but they lack explicit \"Setback Line\" labels. Since the dimensions are provided, this partially addresses the requirement, though the labeling component appears incomplete.\n\nZLU-15.2 - Zoning setbacks not maintained\n- Applicability: All sites - YES\n- Status: NOT-VERIFIABLE - cannot confirm compliance without the required setback values\n\nZLU-15.3 through ZLU-15.6 don't apply here since the project has no shoreline frontage, through-lot conditions, dumpster areas, or fence requirements. ZLU-15.7 addresses whether Article 10 compatibility was properly evaluated. The plans show SF-3 zoning but don't explicitly document the applicability determination for Article 10. Since there's no evidence of an incorrect determination, just missing documentation, I'll mark this as NOT-VERIFIABLE rather than a clear failure.\n\nActually, reconsidering: the applicability rule states Article 10 applies only when the site is MF-4 or less restrictive. This project is MF-2, which qualifies. With an adjacent SF-3 property within 75 feet, Article 10 should apply. The plans show no Article 10 compliance measures, which could mean either the applicability was incorrectly determined as not applying, or it applies but wasn't formally documented.\n\nSince the checklist item specifically addresses \"applicability incorrectly determined,\" and the omission of any Article 10 documentation suggests they concluded it doesn't apply when it actually does, this appears to be a failure. I need to verify the exact triggering conditions—SF-6 doesn't trigger Article 10 since it's not more restrictive than SF-5, and Historic designation alone doesn't create a triggering property either.\n\nThe plans contain no reference to Article 10 or any compatibility analysis, making it impossible to confirm whether they've correctly evaluated applicability under the new rules. Common mistakes include applying outdated framework standards, incorrectly treating SF-6 as a trigger, claiming Historic designation as an exemption, or using the wrong distance test. Given this lack of documentation, I should mark this as NOT-VERIFIABLE rather than definitively failed, since I can't determine if they made an error or simply didn't address it.\n\nNow looking at the actual deficiencies: The buffer requirement is straightforward—Vision found no buffer lines on either the site or landscape plans, but § 25-2-1062(B) mandates a 25-foot buffer along the SF-3 line. The compatibility exhibit is also missing entirely from the sheet list (sheets 1-39), which violates § 25-2-1051. Similarly, there are no cross-section sheets showing the height-distance bands needed to demonstrate § 25-2-1061 compliance. For the transformer pads, Vision identified four locations with no clearance dimensions or specifications referenced to Austin Energy Design Criteria 1.10.4.\n\nOn the not-verifiable side, I can't confirm MF-2 setback compliance without the setback table from § 25-2-492, and height compliance can't be fully assessed without Article 10 height zones marked on the plans.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":11,"cache_creation_input_tokens":869,"cache_read_input_tokens":65107,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":869,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67c41a7e-de71-427c-8abb-98ffa8658f39","uuid":"f871c3b3-6104-41ec-aab7-609ad3055583"}
